[Event "New York"]
[Site "New York, NY USA"]
[Date "1924.03.23"]
[Round "6"]
[White "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Black "Savielly Tartakower"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "A85"]
[PlyCount "103"]
[EventDate "1924.03.16"]

1. d4 e6 2. Nf3 f5 3. c4 Nf6 4. Bg5 {[pgndiagram] This move is already a very
rare option against the Dutch, but it is playable.   The opening theory was
less developed back in the day anyway. And less important.} Be7 5. Nc3 O-O 6.
e3 b6 7. Bd3 Bb7 {The position resembles some sort of Queen's Indian now.
However, White's dark squared bishop is usually on d2 or c1 and in similar
positions, and not outside the pawn chain on g5. This gives Black extra
options.} 8. O-O Qe8 {A typical move, but not the strongest} (8... c5 {Is
playable option here.} 9. Qe2 (9. d5 {Is met by simple tactical trick} Nxd5 10.
cxd5 $1 Bxg5 11. dxe6 dxe6 12. Bc4 Nc6 13. Bxe6+ Kh8 {Gives White very little})
9... Ne4 {And Black is very solid}) 9. Qe2 {[pgndiagram] There is nothing
White can do to prevent the Ne4 jump} (9. Qc2 {Is met by} Bxf3 10. gxf3 Qg6 {
And once again White loses because of the bishop on g5}) (9. Rc1 {Might be
better, deploying the rook on a very useful square.} Ne4 10. Bxe4 fxe4 11. Bxe7
Qxe7 12. Nd2 d5 13. cxd5 exd5 14. Qb3) 9... Ne4 {Now Black has simplified the
position and most probably equalized} 10. Bxe7 Nxc3 $6 {I am not sure whether
this exchange was necessary. But back in the day when you had the opportunity
to double the pawns, you didn't think twice.} (10... Qxe7 {Might be healthier,
waiting for a move to see what White will do} 11. Bxe4 fxe4 12. Nd2 d5 {Is
pretty healthy for Black}) 11. bxc3 {Later it was demonstrated that White has
considerable potential in similar positions.  c5 or d5 advances are both
legitimate tries for advantage} Qxe7 12. a4 $6 {But this move is not necessary}
(12. e4 {Is pretty direct}) (12. Rab1 {Trying to get in c5, is also typical
move in this positions}) 12... Bxf3 $1 {[pgndiagram] A good move, not
presenting White with another chance of exchanging the light squared bishops.
The knight is a better piece in this blocked position} 13. Qxf3 Nc6 14. Rfb1 (
14. e4 {Was still possible nevertheless} fxe4 15. Qxe4 g6 {And White enjoys a
slight advantage}) 14... Rae8 15. Qh3 {So far, Capablanca hasn't found the
strongest plan in the position} Rf6 16. f4 Na5 {Probing the c4 weakness} 17.
Qf3 d6 (17... c5 {Is a typical move in this structure, blocking the c-pawns
from moving and preparing an assault on them.   Remember Nimzowitsch's words:
First restrain, then blockade, finally destroy.} 18. Re1 {White can't really
pursue the e4 plan now, but for demonstration sake...} g6 19. e4 cxd4 20. cxd4
Nb3 {And d4 falls}) 18. Re1 {Finally preparing e4} Qd7 19. e4 {[pgndiagram]Now
this move is possible. There aren't any Nb3 tricks.} fxe4 20. Qxe4 g6 21. g3
Kf8 $2 {But this move is really unclear to me} (21... c5 {Was once again
perfectly possible} 22. d5 e5 {And Black is slightly better}) 22. Kg2 (22. Bf1
{Was perhaps even better} c5 23. dxc5 bxc5 24. Rad1 {White prepares Bh3 next,
greatily activating the bishop.}) 22... Rf7 (22... c5 {Ignoring this one once
again}) (22... Nxc4 {Wasn't really an option} 23. Bxc4 d5 24. Qe5 {The f6 rook
is attacked}) 23. h4 {[pgndiagram] Black's hesitant play has given White
enough time to generate chances on the kingside} d5 $6 (23... Nxc4 {Was now
perfectly possible however} 24. Bxc4 d5 25. Bb5 dxe4 26. Bxd7 Rxd7 27. Rxe4 {
And White is slightly more active, but Black should be able to hold with best
play}) 24. cxd5 exd5 25. Qxe8+ Qxe8 26. Rxe8+ Kxe8 {[pgndiagram] Capa
simplifies to a better endgame. His kingside pawn majority is very dangerous}
27. h5 Rf6 (27... gxh5 {Is obviously bad due to} 28. Rh1) 28. hxg6 hxg6 29. Rh1
Kf8 30. Rh7 Rc6 31. g4 $2 {Objectively, this move is of a dubious value,
because Black has some amazing resources that allow him to stay in the game} (
31. Rd7 {Was the most precise move} Nb3 {Now doesn't work, due to} (31... Rxc3
32. Bxg6) 32. Rxd5 Nc1 33. Be4 Ne2 (33... Rxc3 34. Bxg6) 34. Rd7 Rxc3 35. Kf2
Nxg3 36. Bxg6 {[pgndiagram] And at the end of the long variation White retains
the better chances. Although even here Black has practical chances of saving
the game}) 31... Nc4 $6 {Missing the chance to save the game} (31... Nb3 $1 {
Was correct here} 32. g5 Nc1 33. Bb1 {Keeping an eye on g6} Ne2 34. Kf3 Nxc3 {
And Black has enough counterplay for a draw}) 32. g5 Ne3+ 33. Kf3 Nf5 34. Bxf5
gxf5 {Tartakower surely counted on this position when he played 31.. Nc4, but
unfortunately for him, Capablanca shows a historic endgame idea that will find
its way to endgame manuals for decades to come} 35. Kg3 $3 {[pgndiagram]
Giving up two pawns for the sake of activity. The best example of importance
of the king position in the endgame.  This move is the main reason why this
game is famous.} Rxc3+ 36. Kh4 Rf3 {Black's position was probably very
difficult already, but going for the second pawn makes his defence even harder}
(36... c5 37. dxc5 bxc5 38. g6 {Is still pretty hopeless though}) 37. g6 Rxf4+
38. Kg5 Re4 39. Kf6 {[pgndiagram] The difference between the kings is now
obvious. It is important that Black's pawn is still on f5, as it is shielding
White's king from potential checks along the f file.} Kg8 {Mate was threatened}
40. Rg7+ {An important check, forcing the black king on an unfavorable square}
Kh8 41. Rxc7 {The mating threat still exists} Re8 42. Kxf5 {[pgndiagram] White
finally captures on f5. He has regained his material investment with great
dividens. The remaining Black pawns are about to fall as well.} Re4 43. Kf6 {
"The king is a strong piece. Use it"} Rf4+ 44. Ke5 {Now d5 also falls.
Tartakower could have resigned here. The rest of the game is of no interest}
Rg4 45. g7+ Kg8 46. Rxa7 Rg1 47. Kxd5 Rc1 48. Kd6 Rc2 49. d5 Rc1 50. Rc7 Ra1
51. Kc6 Rxa4 52. d6 {[pgndiagram] One of the most instructive endgames ever
played.} 1-0

[Event "New York"]
[Site "New York, NY USA"]
[Date "1924.03.23"]
[Round "6"]
[White "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Black "Savielly Tartakower"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "A85"]
[PlyCount "103"]
[EventDate "1924.03.16"]

1. d4 e6 2. Nf3 f5 3. c4 Nf6 4. Bg5 {[pgndiagram] This move is already a very
rare option against the Dutch, but it is playable.   The opening theory was
less developed back in the day anyway. And less important.} Be7 5. Nc3 O-O 6.
e3 b6 7. Bd3 Bb7 {The position resembles some sort of Queen's Indian now.
However, White's dark squared bishop is usually on d2 or c1 and in similar
positions, and not outside the pawn chain on g5. This gives Black extra
options.} 8. O-O Qe8 {A typical move, but not the strongest} (8... c5 {Is
playable option here.} 9. Qe2 (9. d5 {Is met by simple tactical trick} Nxd5 10.
cxd5 $1 Bxg5 11. dxe6 dxe6 12. Bc4 Nc6 13. Bxe6+ Kh8 {Gives White very little})
9... Ne4 {And Black is very solid}) 9. Qe2 {[pgndiagram] There is nothing
White can do to prevent the Ne4 jump} (9. Qc2 {Is met by} Bxf3 10. gxf3 Qg6 {
And once again White loses because of the bishop on g5}) (9. Rc1 {Might be
better, deploying the rook on a very useful square.} Ne4 10. Bxe4 fxe4 11. Bxe7
Qxe7 12. Nd2 d5 13. cxd5 exd5 14. Qb3) 9... Ne4 {Now Black has simplified the
position and most probably equalized} 10. Bxe7 Nxc3 $6 {I am not sure whether
this exchange was necessary. But back in the day when you had the opportunity
to double the pawns, you didn't think twice.} (10... Qxe7 {Might be healthier,
waiting for a move to see what White will do} 11. Bxe4 fxe4 12. Nd2 d5 {Is
pretty healthy for Black}) 11. bxc3 {Later it was demonstrated that White has
considerable potential in similar positions.  c5 or d5 advances are both
legitimate tries for advantage} Qxe7 12. a4 $6 {But this move is not necessary}
(12. e4 {Is pretty direct}) (12. Rab1 {Trying to get in c5, is also typical
move in this positions}) 12... Bxf3 $1 {[pgndiagram] A good move, not
presenting White with another chance of exchanging the light squared bishops.
The knight is a better piece in this blocked position} 13. Qxf3 Nc6 14. Rfb1 (
14. e4 {Was still possible nevertheless} fxe4 15. Qxe4 g6 {And White enjoys a
slight advantage}) 14... Rae8 15. Qh3 {So far, Capablanca hasn't found the
strongest plan in the position} Rf6 16. f4 Na5 {Probing the c4 weakness} 17.
Qf3 d6 (17... c5 {Is a typical move in this structure, blocking the c-pawns
from moving and preparing an assault on them.   Remember Nimzowitsch's words:
First restrain, then blockade, finally destroy.} 18. Re1 {White can't really
pursue the e4 plan now, but for demonstration sake...} g6 19. e4 cxd4 20. cxd4
Nb3 {And d4 falls}) 18. Re1 {Finally preparing e4} Qd7 19. e4 {[pgndiagram]Now
this move is possible. There aren't any Nb3 tricks.} fxe4 20. Qxe4 g6 21. g3
Kf8 $2 {But this move is really unclear to me} (21... c5 {Was once again
perfectly possible} 22. d5 e5 {And Black is slightly better}) 22. Kg2 (22. Bf1
{Was perhaps even better} c5 23. dxc5 bxc5 24. Rad1 {White prepares Bh3 next,
greatily activating the bishop.}) 22... Rf7 (22... c5 {Ignoring this one once
again}) (22... Nxc4 {Wasn't really an option} 23. Bxc4 d5 24. Qe5 {The f6 rook
is attacked}) 23. h4 {[pgndiagram] Black's hesitant play has given White
enough time to generate chances on the kingside} d5 $6 (23... Nxc4 {Was now
perfectly possible however} 24. Bxc4 d5 25. Bb5 dxe4 26. Bxd7 Rxd7 27. Rxe4 {
And White is slightly more active, but Black should be able to hold with best
play}) 24. cxd5 exd5 25. Qxe8+ Qxe8 26. Rxe8+ Kxe8 {[pgndiagram] Capa
simplifies to a better endgame. His kingside pawn majority is very dangerous}
27. h5 Rf6 (27... gxh5 {Is obviously bad due to} 28. Rh1) 28. hxg6 hxg6 29. Rh1
Kf8 30. Rh7 Rc6 31. g4 $2 {Objectively, this move is of a dubious value,
because Black has some amazing resources that allow him to stay in the game} (
31. Rd7 {Was the most precise move} Nb3 {Now doesn't work, due to} (31... Rxc3
32. Bxg6) 32. Rxd5 Nc1 33. Be4 Ne2 (33... Rxc3 34. Bxg6) 34. Rd7 Rxc3 35. Kf2
Nxg3 36. Bxg6 {[pgndiagram] And at the end of the long variation White retains
the better chances. Although even here Black has practical chances of saving
the game}) 31... Nc4 $6 {Missing the chance to save the game} (31... Nb3 $1 {
Was correct here} 32. g5 Nc1 33. Bb1 {Keeping an eye on g6} Ne2 34. Kf3 Nxc3 {
And Black has enough counterplay for a draw}) 32. g5 Ne3+ 33. Kf3 Nf5 34. Bxf5
gxf5 {Tartakower surely counted on this position when he played 31.. Nc4, but
unfortunately for him, Capablanca shows a historic endgame idea that will find
its way to endgame manuals for decades to come} 35. Kg3 $3 {[pgndiagram]
Giving up two pawns for the sake of activity. The best example of importance
of the king position in the endgame.  This move is the main reason why this
game is famous.} Rxc3+ 36. Kh4 Rf3 {Black's position was probably very
difficult already, but going for the second pawn makes his defence even harder}
(36... c5 37. dxc5 bxc5 38. g6 {Is still pretty hopeless though}) 37. g6 Rxf4+
38. Kg5 Re4 39. Kf6 {[pgndiagram] The difference between the kings is now
obvious. It is important that Black's pawn is still on f5, as it is shielding
White's king from potential checks along the f file.} Kg8 {Mate was threatened}
40. Rg7+ {An important check, forcing the black king on an unfavorable square}
Kh8 41. Rxc7 {The mating threat still exists} Re8 42. Kxf5 {[pgndiagram] White
finally captures on f5. He has regained his material investment with great
dividens. The remaining Black pawns are about to fall as well.} Re4 43. Kf6 {
"The king is a strong piece. Use it"} Rf4+ 44. Ke5 {Now d5 also falls.
Tartakower could have resigned here. The rest of the game is of no interest}
Rg4 45. g7+ Kg8 46. Rxa7 Rg1 47. Kxd5 Rc1 48. Kd6 Rc2 49. d5 Rc1 50. Rc7 Ra1
51. Kc6 Rxa4 52. d6 {[pgndiagram] One of the most instructive endgames ever
played.} 1-0

[Event "Casual Game"]
[Site "New York, NY USA"]
[Date "1918.06.18"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Black "Marc Fonaroff"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C66"]
[PlyCount "43"]
[EventDate "1918.??.??"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 d6 4. Nc3 Nf6 {[pgndiagram] Some strange sort of
Philidor /Four knights game is on the board.} 5. Bb5 {And now Capa transposes
to Ruy Lopez. Don't ask me what is the name of variation, I am really confused
with the move order :D.  Probably some sort of Berlin/Steinitz defence with d6}
Bd7 6. O-O Be7 {Black is typicaly passive, but rather solid in this positions.
He doesn't have immediate problems with development or many weaknesses, but he
does have less space.} 7. Re1 (7. d5 {It is always a question whether White
wants to play this advance in similar structures} Nb8 8. Be2 O-O {And
objectively White should be better here, but Black has counterattacing chances
on the kingside and it probably didn't suit Capablanca's style.}) 7... exd4 {
[pgndiagram] Exchanging the pieces is usually in favour of player with less
space.} 8. Nxd4 Nxd4 9. Qxd4 Bxb5 10. Nxb5 {[pgndiagram] White is still
slightly better in this type of pawn structure, where White's e4 pawn faces
Black's d6 counterpart.   However, an exchange of two pairs of minor pieces is
definitely to Black's advantage. Typicaly White has attacking chances on the
kingside in similar positions and exchanges reduce his attacking potential} O-O
11. Qc3 {This move is slightly pointless} c6 $2 {But this creates a weakness
on d6 and justified White's previous move} (11... a6 {Was correct. The c7 pawn
is indirectly defended} 12. Nd4 (12. Nxc7 {Would of course be a horrible
blunder due to} Rc8) 12... Nd7 13. Nf5 Bf6 {[pgndiagram] And the difference
between a6 and c6 is apparent - the d6 pawn is not hanging.}) 12. Nd4 {Now
White's knight will feel very good on f5, hitting Black's d6 pawn and creating
threats on the kingside} Nd7 $2 {[pgndiagram] Not sensing the danger} (12...
Re8 {Was probably better}) 13. Nf5 {Now White has a very strong attack} Bf6 14.
Qg3 Ne5 15. Bf4 (15. Be3 {Was probably even better, not allowing Qb6}) 15...
Qc7 (15... Qb6 {Was a possibility here} 16. Nxd6 Ng6 17. e5 Bh4 {But this is
too computerish even for today's standards, let alone for the 1918.}) 16. Rad1
Rad8 17. Rxd6 $5 {[pgndiagram] Starting a very beautiful combination that is
slightly flawed. Still, Capablanca never avoided tactics when he thought the
position required them} (17. h4 {Maintaning the tension was probably
objectively stronger, but much less fun}) 17... Rxd6 18. Bxe5 Rd1 $2 {
[pgndiagram] The problems prove to be too difficult for Fonaroff and he
commits a decisive mistake of the game} (18... Qa5 {Attacking the rook on e1,
was the way to go.} 19. Bc3 Bxc3 20. bxc3 Rg6 21. Ne7+ Kh8 22. Nxg6+ hxg6 23.
Qd6 {[pgndiagram] And despite White's pawn advantage, the position should be
equal}) 19. Rxd1 Bxe5 {Now Capablanca finishes the game with an elegant
tactical sequence, exploiting the weakness of the back rank and the
unfortunate position of the piece on e5} 20. Nh6+ Kh8 21. Qxe5 $1 {[pgndiagram]
Forcing the Black queen on e5} Qxe5 22. Nxf7+ {[pgndiagram] And now winning a
piece, because taking with the rook is impossible due to a back rank mate. A
very attractive game by Capablanca, even if the class of the opponent was not
very high. The combination immediately became classic.} 1-0

[Event "Capablanca - Corzo"]
[Site "Habana CUB"]
[Date "1901.12.15"]
[Round "11"]
[White "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Black "Juan Corzo"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D05"]
[PlyCount "119"]
[EventDate "1901.11.17"]

1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 c5 3. e3 {[pgndiagram] The Colle system. Harmless, but at the
same time also very dangerous system for Black.} Nc6 4. b3 {This particular
variation, featuring an early b3, is topical even today. Hell, it was even
played in the 11th game of the Carlsen - Karjakin World Championship match.} e6
{But this is already a rare response. Black locks his light squared bishop} (
4... Nf6 {Is considered as a main move nowadays} 5. Bb2 (5. dxc5 Bg4 {Is also
ok for Black}) 5... cxd4 6. exd4 Bg4 {And Black shouldn't have problems}) 5.
Bb2 Nf6 6. Nbd2 cxd4 7. exd4 {Still, Black hasn't done anything wrong so far
and has a perfectly playable position} Bd6 8. Bd3 O-O 9. O-O Nh5 $2 {But this
is already slightly misguided. Black embarks on kingside activity instead of
completing his development calmly} (9... b6 10. Re1 Bb7 {Would nowadays be
played by any Candidate Master most probably}) 10. g3 (10. Re1 Nf4 11. Bf1 {
Was also perfectly playable}) 10... f5 $6 {[pgndiagram] Once you have said A,
you usually say B. But here Black should have admitted his mistake and
retreated his knight} (10... Nf6) 11. Ne5 {Apart from exploiting the weakness
of e5, White could have also opened things up with c4} (11. c4 Nf6 12. a3 Ne4
13. Rc1 {And White is better}) 11... Nf6 12. f4 $6 {And this move is hard to
judge. On one hand it strengthens White's control of e5, but on the other hand
it hands the e4 square to Black} (12. c4 {Was probably once again preferable})
12... Bxe5 $2 {But this is a serious error. Although the pawn on e5 shields
Black's weak e6 pawn, this exchange increases White's control of space.} (12...
Ne4 {Taking advantage of White's last move, was preferable.} 13. Ndf3 Nxe5 14.
Nxe5 b6 15. Qe2 Bb7 {And the weakness of e5 is compensated by control of e4.
The game is balanced}) 13. fxe5 Ng4 $2 (13... Ne4 {Was once again better}) 14.
Qe2 Qb6 15. Nf3 Bd7 16. a3 (16. c3 {Was probably even better. White's next
couple of moves would intend to move the pieces toward kingside and then start
a direct attack} Rac8 17. Bc1 Qd8 18. Bd2 Ne7 19. Rae1 Qe8 20. h3 Nh6 21. Nh4 {
And here you can see how the pawn on e5 benefits White, as it is much easier
for him to bring his pieces toward kingside. He already has a grat advantage
here}) 16... Kh8 17. h3 Nh6 18. Qf2 Nf7 19. Kg2 g5 $6 {[pgndiagram] This
decision is also objectively dubious. An ancient chess rule claims that it is
better not to play on the side of the board where your opponent has the
advantage. But on the other hand Black is desperate for counterplay in this
position} (19... a5 {Is the alternative} 20. g4 a4 21. b4 Ne7 {And Black's
position is probably better than the one he got in the actual game}) 20. g4 $1
{I like this decision by Capablanca} Ne7 (20... f4 {Would be horrible due to}
21. h4 {With decisive opening of the kingside}) (20... fxg4 21. hxg4 {And
White's heavy pieces will bring havoc on the h-file}) 21. Qe3 (21. Bc1 {Might
be even more precise}) 21... Rg8 $6 (21... Ng6 {Was a possibility here and
this is the main reason why 21 Bc1 is probably a better move}) 22. Rae1 $6 (22.
a4 {Preventing the exchange of the light squared bishops is probably better
here. This is the best example of how space advantage allows you to play
simultaneously on both sides of the board}) 22... Ng6 $6 (22... Bb5 {Was
slightly annoying for White here as he can't continue with his plan} 23. gxf5
Nxf5 {And he is suddenly worse}) 23. gxf5 $2 {[pgndiagram] But this is a
serious mistake by Capablanca.} (23. Kh1 f4 24. Qe2 {And White still remains
in control}) 23... Nf4+ (23... exf5 {Was good for Black. The tactical point is
that} 24. e6 f4 $1 {Important intermezzo} (24... Rae8 25. exf7) 25. Qe2 Rae8 $1
{Is good for Black as} 26. exf7 Rxe2+ {Now comes with check}) 24. Kh2 (24. Kh1
{The computer wants to retreat here. The main reason is once again that in
some variations the king will be checked if the rook take the queen on e2} Nxd3
25. cxd3 Qxb3 (25... exf5 26. e6) 26. Re2) 24... Nxd3 25. Qxd3 (25. cxd3 exf5
26. e6 f4 27. Qe2 Rae8) 25... exf5 {[pgndiagram] So far Black has played very
well in the complications and managed to outplay Capablanca who missed some
important tactical nuances (Kasparov speculated that this tendency of being
superfluous was to cost him dearly in later stages of his career)} 26. c4 $5 {
On the outside, White's pawn mass looks extremely threatening, but Black has
strong countermeasures available} Qe6 $2 {This, unfortunately for Corzo, is
not one of them. The text move prepares Bb5 in the future, but Corzo overlooks
the possibility of a queen sacrifice} (26... g4 {Was very unpleasant} 27. Nh4
g3+ 28. Kg2 dxc4 {And White's position is unenviable} 29. Qxc4 Be6 {Is winning
for Black} 30. d5 Rac8 31. Qd4 Rc2+ {Just wins} 32. Kg1 Ng5) (26... dxc4 27.
Qxc4 Be6 28. d5 Rac8 29. Qd4 {Is now good for White}) (26... f4 {Is also a
better move.} 27. cxd5 Bb5 {And now there is zero possibility of a queen
sacrifice, so Black just wins an exchange}) 27. cxd5 Qxd5 28. e6 {[pgndiagram]
After this, White is winning. But Capa does it in a spectacular fashion} Bb5 {
Probably Corzo was counting on this move, but this is met by a spectacular
riposte, which is the main reason why this game is included in this collection}
(28... Bxe6 29. Rxe6 Qxe6 30. d5+ {Is naturally, over}) 29. Qxb5 $3 {
[pgndiagram] A fantastic sacrifice, although strictly speaking, it was not 100
% necessary} (29. Qd2 {Was more prosaic and probably even stronger. White wins
material} Bxf1 (29... Nd6 30. Re5 Qc6 31. Rxb5) 30. exf7 Rgf8 31. Nxg5) 29...
Qxb5 30. d5+ Rg7 31. exf7 {White has obtained only two pieces for the queen.
But his pawn on f7 is a monster, and the pin via g7 is extremely annoying} h6
32. Nd4 {not the strongest move actually, as it allows Black to continue
resisting for some time now} (32. Nh4 {Was actually the strongest finish here}
gxh4 (32... Qxf1 33. Rxf1 gxh4 34. Rg1) (32... Qxb3 33. Bxg7+ Kxg7 34. Nxf5+)
33. Rg1 {And wins}) 32... Qxf1 $1 {[pgndiagram] The best chance. Black
exploits the fact that for the moment rook on g7 is not pinned, and sacrifices
his queen. Unfortunately for him, he still ends up in a losing engame} 33. Rxf1
Rxf7 34. Rxf5 {Now Capa needs to display his technique, which is not a problem
for him in this position. The game is essentialy over} Rxf5 35. Nxf5+ Kh7 36.
Ne7 Rf8 37. Kg2 h5 38. d6 g4 39. hxg4 hxg4 40. Be5 (40. Kg3 {Was faster}) 40...
Kh6 41. d7 {[pgndiagram] For the moment it seems tha Capa has overextended} Rd8
42. Ng8+ {But this cheeky move allows him to enter an endgame where he will be
a clear piece up} Rxg8 (42... Kg6 43. Nf6 {With Bc7 to follow, is also hopeless
}) 43. Bf6 Kg6 44. d8=Q Rxd8 45. Bxd8 {Corzo could have easily resigned here}
b5 46. Kf2 Kf5 47. Ke3 Ke5 48. Kd3 Kd5 49. Kc3 g3 50. Bh4 g2 51. Bf2 a5 52. b4
Ke4 53. Bb6 Kd5 54. Kd3 Kc6 55. Bg1 Kd5 56. Bh2 Kc6 57. Kd4 a4 58. Ke5 Kb6 59.
Kd5 Ka6 60. Kc5 {One of those games that demonstrated Capablanca's
resourcefullness in objectively worse positions and which shows that Capa
could have played as energetically and riskily as for instance Lasker and
Alekhine.} 1-0

[Event "Capablanca - Marshall"]
[Site "New York, NY USA"]
[Date "1909.06.23"]
[Round "23"]
[White "Frank James Marshall"]
[Black "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "D34"]
[PlyCount "98"]
[EventDate "1909.04.19"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 c5 {[pgndiagram] The Tarrasch! Capa wasn't afraid of
the isolated pawn back in the day} 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. g3 Be6 (6... Nf6
{Is the main move nowadays, not deciding where to put the light squared bishop
yet} 7. Bg2 Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. b3 Bg4 {The point is that in some variations
Black can take more flexible approach and develop on g4 instead}) 7. Bg2 Be7 8.
O-O Nf6 9. Bg5 {[pgndiagram] The players have transposed into mainline
nevertheless} (9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. b3 O-O 11. Bb2) 9... Ne4 $2 {This move shows
that such dynamic positions were pretty revolutionary for those times} (9... c4
) (9... O-O) 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 11. Ne5 $2 {[pgndiagram] And here Kasparov's
comment is priceless: "A typical Marshall way of trying to initiate some
tactics out of nothing"} (11. dxc5 {Was simple and good. After} Nxc3 12. bxc3
Qxc5 13. Rb1 b6 14. Ng5 {White has extremely pleasant position}) 11... Nxd4 {
A cold shower. Capa simplifies the position} (11... Nxe5 12. Nxe4 dxe4 13. dxe5
{Would be for instance in Marshall's favour}) 12. Nxe4 dxe4 13. e3 Nf3+ {
Another good decision. Capa decides to give up a pawn on e4 in this way, and
as a result he will have a 3-2 pawn majority on the queenside} (13... Nc6 14.
Nxc6 bxc6 15. Bxe4 {Is of course terrible}) 14. Nxf3 exf3 15. Qxf3 O-O {
[pgndiagram] It is time to pause for a moment. The position should be dead
equal; with the great number of the pieces on the board, the pawn majority of
the queenside shouldn't be regarded as an advantage per se. However, under the
influence of this games, for quite some time it has been regarded that
queenside pawn majority brings automatic advantage} 16. Rfc1 (16. Qxb7 {Would
of course be terrible due to} Qxb7 17. Bxb7 Rab8 18. Bf3 Rxb2 {And Black has a
sizeable advantage}) (16. Rfd1 {Grabbing an open file, was also logical}) 16...
Rab8 17. Qe4 Qc7 {Capa intends to push his pawns} 18. Rc3 $2 {And this is the
first move of questonable value. It is not clear what White hopes to achieve
by doubling on the semi open c-file} (18. Rd1 {Is perfectly logical} Rfd8 19.
a3 b5 20. Qc2 c4 21. Be4 {And White has nothing to fear}) 18... b5 19. a3 $6 (
19. b3 {Reducing the effect of c4, was better, and more consistent with the
previous move} c4 20. bxc4 bxc4 21. Bf1) 19... c4 {[pgndiagram] Now suddenly
White has an unpleasant position} 20. Bf3 $2 {Again showing indifference
toward the d-file} (20. Rd1 Rfd8 21. Rcc1 a5 {And still, White should be able
to defend after} 22. Rd4) 20... Rfd8 21. Rd1 Rxd1+ 22. Bxd1 Rd8 23. Bf3 g6 {
[pgndiagram] Now White's pieces are not coordinated and Black enjoys activity}
24. Qc6 $2 {Another suboptimal move.} Qe5 $1 25. Qe4 {There is now nothing
better} (25. Kg2 Rd2) 25... Qxe4 {Now, after queen exchange, b5 will not be
attacked, so Black should go for the exchange} 26. Bxe4 Rd1+ 27. Kg2 a5 {
[pgndiagram] The game is almost over; Black's queenside pawn majority will
soon come crashing through. It is amazing how quickly Marshall, a top 10
player, lost his way in a seemingly harmless position} 28. Rc2 b4 29. axb4 (29.
h4 {Would be met by} b3 30. Re2 c3 31. bxc3 Ra1 {And wins}) 29... axb4 30. Bf3
Rb1 31. Be2 (31. Rd2 {Would come to very similar things} b3 32. Be2 Rc1 33. Bd1
(33. Kf3 Rc2) 33... c3 34. bxc3 b2 {As in the game}) 31... b3 32. Rd2 Rc1 33.
Bd1 c3 34. bxc3 b2 35. Rxb2 Rxd1 {[pgndiagram] Capa wins the piece and with it
the game. Marshall could have resigned here, but back in the day resigning
wasn't done as easily as today} 36. Rc2 Bf5 37. Rb2 Rc1 38. Rb3 Be4+ 39. Kh3
Rc2 40. f4 h5 41. g4 hxg4+ 42. Kxg4 Rxh2 43. Rb4 f5+ 44. Kg3 Re2 45. Rc4 Rxe3+
46. Kh4 Kg7 47. Rc7+ Kf6 48. Rd7 Bg2 49. Rd6+ Kg7 {[pgndiagram] An impressive
game, which had a massive influence on many subsequent generations of the
chess players.} 0-1

[Event "New York"]
[Site "New York, NY USA"]
[Date "1924.04.12"]
[Round "19"]
[White "Savielly Tartakower"]
[Black "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C33"]
[PlyCount "60"]
[EventDate "1924.03.16"]

1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Be2 {[pgndiagram] Already a very rare variation of the
King's gambit} d5 $1 {Very thematic, simple and direct. Black strikes in the
centre} (3... Qh4+ 4. Kf1 {Is probably playable, but it is not easy to decide
on it without thorough preparation}) 4. exd5 Nf6 {I very much like Capa's
treatment of the opening} (4... Qxd5 5. Nf3 Bd6 6. d4 {Would lead to the type
of game White wanted to obtain from the very start- free development, obvious
moves and tactical threats from the very beginning}) 5. c4 c6 6. d4 (6. dxc6
Nxc6 {Is just terrible for White - the weakness of d4 is deciding}) 6... Bb4+ (
6... cxd5 {Is perfectly normal as well}) 7. Kf1 $6 {[pgndiagram] A very
strange and not necessary decision} (7. Bd2 {Was the lesser evil; White got
outplayed in the opening all the same and it is time to start thinking about
equalizing} Bxd2+ 8. Qxd2 cxd5 9. Nf3 O-O 10. O-O) 7... cxd5 {White has no
compensation for the position of his king or the pawn deficit} 8. Bxf4 dxc4 9.
Bxb8 $2 {Overlooking a very nice defensive resource} Nd5 $1 {A strong riposte}
(9... Rxb8 10. Qa4+ {Would, of course, be a disaster}) 10. Kf2 Rxb8 11. Bxc4
O-O {[pgndiagram] White has restored material equality, but the position of
his king is what still hurts him} 12. Nf3 Nf6 13. Nc3 b5 $1 14. Bd3 (14. Nxb5 {
Is not very wise} Ne4+ 15. Kg1 Bg4) 14... Ng4+ 15. Kg1 Bb7 {[pgndiagram] All
Black's pieces are pointed toward the king} 16. Bf5 (16. h3 {Retained more hope
} Nf6 17. Qe2 {Although even here, White's position is terrible}) 16... Bxf3
17. gxf3 Ne3 {Now Black wins instantly} 18. Bxh7+ Kh8 $1 {The strongest} (18...
Kxh7 19. Qd3+ Kg8 20. Qxe3 Re8 {Was also sufficient though}) 19. Qd3 Bxc3 20.
bxc3 Nd5 {Black has many threats. Qg5 and g6 for instance} 21. Be4 Nf4 22. Qd2
Qh4 23. Kf1 f5 {[pgndiagram] Now the rook wil join the attack. White's rooks
haven't made a single move yet} 24. Bc6 Rf6 25. d5 Rd8 26. Rd1 Rxc6 27. dxc6
Rxd2 28. Rxd2 Ne6 29. Rd6 Qc4+ 30. Kg2 Qe2+ {[pgndiagram] An impressive,
effortless victory and the best example of the validity of  "Never play King's
gambit against Capablanca" saying} 0-1

[Event "New York"]
[Site "New York, NY USA"]
[Date "1927.02.20"]
[Round "2"]
[White "Aron Nimzowitsch"]
[Black "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "D30"]
[PlyCount "82"]
[EventDate "1927.02.19"]

1. c4 Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 d5 4. e3 {A strange way of playing the Queen's
gambit, with the dark squared bishop locked behind the pawn chain} (4. Bg5 {Is
more common}) 4... Be7 {This time there is no Tarrasch. Capablanca plays more
classicaly} 5. Nbd2 {I don't like this move. White refrains from Nc3, putting
some pressure on the d5 pawn, and allows Black to carry out his own c5 advance
easily} (5. Nc3 {Is more normal}) 5... O-O 6. Bd3 c5 $1 {[pgndiagram] Pretty
thematic in similar positions and a good move, putting immediate pressure on
the White centre.} 7. dxc5 $6 {Not the best reaction in my opinion} (7. b3 {
Given White's developing scheme, this move was the most sensible, trying to
keep his centre intact}) 7... Na6 $1 {Strong play by Capablanca. The knight
will reach the c5 with gain of tempo} 8. O-O (8. cxd5 Nxc5 9. Be2 Qxd5 {Is
excellent for Black}) 8... Nxc5 9. Be2 {A sad, but necessary retreat} b6 $5 {
Black has no problems in developing his pieces and is already slightly better}
10. cxd5 $6 {Bringing another piece to the centre} (10. b3 {Trying to develop
the bishop on b2 and waiting to capture on c4 with the knight, was wiser})
10... Nxd5 11. Nb3 {Nimzowitsch moves a knight for the second time, while his
dark squared bishop is still undeveloped} Bb7 $1 {[pgndiagram] Simple and
strong} (11... Ne4 {Also came into consideration}) 12. Nxc5 Bxc5 13. Qa4 $6 (
13. Bd2 {Trying to develop finally, was probably better} Qf6 14. Qb3 Rfd8 15.
Rfd1 {And nothing terrible for White is apparent}) 13... Qf6 {Making the
development of the bishop unavailable for the moment} (13... a5 {Not allowing
the exchange of the bishops, was probably even better}) 14. Ba6 (14. Bd2 Qxb2)
14... Bxa6 15. Qxa6 Nb4 16. Qe2 $2 (16. Qc4 {Would appear to be better. The
point is that after} Rfc8 17. Bd2 {Black doesn't have a convenient discovered
attack on the queen} Bf8 (17... Qxb2 18. Rfb1 {Is out of the question} Qc2 19.
Rxb4 Bxb4 20. Qxb4 Rc4 21. Qb3) 18. Qg4 {And White holds}) 16... Rfd8 {
[pgndiagram] Now Black manages to install the knight on d3 and White can't
move his bishop all the same} 17. a3 (17. Bd2 Qxb2 {Now hurts White}) 17... Nd3
18. Ne1 $6 {It is natural that White wants to get rid of the knight
immediately, but this delays his development further and gives Black more time
to create some attacking chances} (18. Rd1 {Getting rid of the knight in
another fashion, was better} Ne5 19. Rxd8+ Rxd8 20. Ne1 $1 {The only defence.
Preventing Nd3. Now White threatens Bd2} Ng6 21. Ra2 $5 {A strange looking
move but a familiar idea. White intends b4 next and it seems that Black can't
prevent him from finishing development. But the nature of this moves shows
that White has complicated his task earlier and that particular ingenuinity
and creativity were required}) 18... Nxe1 19. Rxe1 Rac8 {[pgndiagram] It is
apparent that Black's pieces are much more active and that he has the upper
hand} 20. Rb1 (20. Ra2 {Once again came into consideration. However, without
the knights and with the rooks on d8 and c8, White stands worse than in the
positions examined before} Bf8 21. b4 Qc3 22. Bb2 Qb3 23. Bd4 g6 {And Black
retains the advantage}) 20... Qe5 21. g3 (21. b4 Bd6 22. g3 Qe4 23. Rb2 a5 {Is
unpleasant}) 21... Qd5 22. b4 Bf8 23. Bb2 {[pgndiagram] Nevertheless, due to
the symmetry in the position White should be able to hold the position.} Qa2
24. Ra1 Qb3 25. Bd4 (25. Rec1 {Not allowing the rook on c2, was probably wiser}
Rxc1+ {Black can try to press with the a5 advance} 26. Rxc1 a5 $5) 25... Rc2
26. Qa6 $6 {Nimzowitsch's sense of danger fails him. Moving the Queen away and
going after the a pawn is a luxury in this position} (26. Qd1 {With the threat
of Re2, was the best defence} Qc4 (26... e5 $2 27. Re2 Rc3 28. Qxb3 Rxb3 29.
Bxe5) 27. Rc1 Rc8 28. Rxc2 Qxc2 29. Qxc2 Rxc2 30. Kf1 {And White shouldn't
lose this endgame}) (26. Qf1 {Keeping the queen in the vicinity of the king,
was also a better choice}) 26... e5 $1 {[pgndiagram] Great play by Capa. At
the cost of a mere pawn he manages to double his rooks on the second rank} 27.
Bxe5 Rdd2 28. Qb7 $2 {The losing move. After the fall of f2 pawn, White is
simply left without any hope whatsoever} (28. Rf1 $2 {Would lose to} Qxe3) (28.
Qf1 {Was the only move.} h5 $5 (28... Rxf2 {Taking the queen for two rooks is
also a good choice, as White's king is rather weak} 29. Qxf2 Rxf2 30. Kxf2 h5 {
And again this idea yields Black an advantage in the position. The goal is to
weaken the kingside pawn structure})) 28... Rxf2 29. g4 Qe6 30. Bg3 Rxh2 $1 {
It is possible that Nimzowitsch missed this move} 31. Qf3 (31. Bxh2 {Would
lead to mate after} Qxg4+ 32. Kh1 Qh3) 31... Rhg2+ 32. Qxg2 Rxg2+ 33. Kxg2 Qxg4
{[pgndiagram] And now the game is essentialy over.} 34. Rad1 h5 35. Rd4 Qg5 36.
Kh2 a5 37. Re2 axb4 38. axb4 Be7 39. Re4 Bf6 40. Rf2 Qd5 41. Re8+ Kh7 {
[pgndiagram] Here Aaron finally decided to throw in the towel. A brilliant
game by Capablanca - healthy logical play in the opening, setting the opponent
with some problems in the middlegame and exploiting his mistakes in a tactical
manner when required. He was always a difficult opponent for Nimzowitsch.} 0-1

[Event "Margate"]
[Site "Margate ENG"]
[Date "1935.04.30"]
[Round "6"]
[White "Edward G Sergeant"]
[Black "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C01"]
[PlyCount "78"]
[EventDate "1935.04.24"]

1. e4 e6 {This is a game from the later stage of Capablanca's career, with the
peak of his chess strength long gone. However, he was still capable of
producing masterpieces reminiscent of his best days. Masterpieces such as this
one} 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. exd5 {I think that apart from the immediate
exchange variation, this delayed exchange variation of the French is the
second most annoying thing for any Winawer player} exd5 5. Bd3 Ne7 {
[pgndiagram] It is interesting to note that Capablanca puts his knight on e7
instead of f6. He is already intending to place his king on the kingside and
push the kingside pawns and he doesn't want his knight on the way of the f-pawn
} 6. Nf3 Bg4 (6... O-O {Is still safer, but without the will to risk things
Black can hardly play for the win in these structures}) 7. O-O (7. h3 {It is
always a question whether White should include this move. I personally don't
see how White can benefit here} Bh5 8. O-O Nbc6 9. Be3 Qd7 {And the pin is as
present as always}) 7... Nbc6 8. Ne2 $2 {A questionable decision} (8. Be3 Qd7 {
Is more normal}) 8... Bd6 (8... Bxf3 {I think Black could have shattered the
structure immediately and played for the control of the f4 square later on} 9.
gxf3 Bd6 10. c3 Qd7 {And next long castling, followed by Ng6 comes. Black is
already slightly better}) 9. c3 Qd7 (9... Bxf3 {Was now the last chance}) 10.
Ng3 $2 {[pgndiagram] The problem with this move is that it will enable Black
to push the h-pawn with tempo} (10. Ne1 {Was apparently the strongest move in
the position. White plans to reroute the knight to e3 via c2 and also removes
the dangerous pin}) 10... h5 $1 {Good play by Capablanca} 11. Re1 O-O-O (11...
h4 {Was probably more precise, not presenting White with the opportunity to
play h3 himself} 12. Nf1 h3 13. g3 O-O-O {And Black is slightly better}) 12. h3
h4 13. Nf1 Bh5 14. Be2 {Slightly too timid} (14. b4 $5 {Came into
consideration.} f6 15. a4 g5 16. Ne3 {With complicated position with chances
for both sides}) 14... Ng6 15. Ng5 Bxe2 16. Rxe2 Rde8 {[pgndiagram] The
symmetry of the position and the exchanges that follow should lead to equality,
but alas..} (16... Nf4 {Why not?} 17. Re1 Rde8 18. Rxe8+ Rxe8 19. Bxf4 Bxf4 20.
Nf3 {With equality}) 17. Rxe8+ Rxe8 18. Be3 Nf4 19. Qg4 $2 {[pgndiagram] The
first step in the wrong direction. This exchange, allowing the doubling of the
g-pawn, turns the h-pawn into a very strong soldier that will ultimately
decide the game} (19. Bxf4 Bxf4 20. Nf3 {Was perfectly allright for White})
19... Qxg4 20. hxg4 f6 {Capablanca plays this stage very strongly in my
opinion. First he makes sure he doesn't lose his f-pawn} (20... Nd3 $2 21. Nxf7
) 21. Bxf4 {Wise, preventing the jump to d3} (21. Nf3 Nd3 {Is unpleasant})
21... Bxf4 22. Nf3 (22. Nh3 {Was better, gaining a tempo and preventing the
rook to join the game} Bd6 (22... Bh6 23. f4 $1) 23. Ne3 {With equality}) 22...
Re2 $1 {[pgndiagram]Now the rook on the second rank hurts White} 23. Rb1 {Very
passive} (23. Nxh4 Rxb2 {Was probably a better chance nevertheless, but White
is still on the back foot}) 23... g5 24. Ne1 Na5 {Now the knight also joins
the game} 25. Nd3 Nc4 26. b3 Nd6 27. Rb2 Rxb2 (27... Re6 {Maybe Black could
have refrained from exchanging the rooks, since he controls the e-file}) 28.
Nxb2 Kd7 29. f3 {Now White's pawns on the kingside are fixed. It is amazing
that Sergeant loses this position in only 8 more moves, but still, he does
have an unpleasant ending} (29. g3 {Getting rid of the h4 pawn, was possibly a
better defence. Still, after} hxg3 30. fxg3 (30. Nxg3 Bxg3 31. fxg3 Ne4 {Loses}
) 30... Bc1 31. Nd3 Ba3 {Black retains the upper hand}) 29... b6 30. Kf2 Nb5
31. Nd1 c5 32. a4 Nc7 33. Ke2 {Faulty. White probably missed the tactical shot
Black has at his disposal} (33. Nfe3 {Trying to exchange a pair of minor
pieces, was probably better} Bxe3+ 34. Nxe3 a5 {And White has some chances of
surviving} (34... Kc6 35. b4)) 33... Ne6 34. Kd3 Kc6 {[pgndiagram] Takinga
moment to defend the d5 in advance} (34... Bd6 35. Nfe3 Kc6 36. Nxd5 {Leads to
equality, for instance}) 35. Nde3 (35. Nfe3 cxd4 36. cxd4 Bg3 {Loses to the
same tactical idea that happened in the game} 37. b4 Nf4+ 38. Kd2 Nxg2) (35.
dxc5 {Was the only defence} bxc5 36. c4 d4 37. Ke4 {Although White doesn't
want to willingly give a passed d4 pawn to Black if he doesn't regard it as an
absolute necessity. Sergeant can probably be excused for not sensing the danger
}) 35... Bc7 (35... Bxe3 {Was now the strongest} 36. Kxe3 {If White takes with
the knight, then the familiar Nf4+ - Nxg2 tactic works} cxd4+ 37. cxd4 b5 {But
with the king on e3, Black can make progress on the other wing. White can't
really move his knight or his king} 38. Nd2 Kb6 39. Nb1 bxa4 40. bxa4 Ka5 {And
Black will soon win}) 36. Nd2 $2 {Removing the knight from a solid defensive
position and completely overlooking Black's idea} (36. b4 {Would have allowed
White to resist. The deadly check on b5 is threatened} Nf4+ 37. Kd2 Nxg2 {
[pgndiagram] Now doesn't work} 38. b5+ $1 {A key move} Kd7 39. Nxg2 h3 40. Nge3
h2 41. Nxh2 Bxh2 42. Nxd5 {And suddenly White wins}) 36... Nf4+ {Now there is
nothing White can do and the game is over. The knight has been moved to d2 and
there is no check on b5} 37. Kc2 Nxg2 {[pgndiagram] Here Sergeant resigned. If
White takes the knight, this is what happens} 38. Nxg2 h3 39. Ne3 h2 {And the
pawn can't be stopped. A typical Capablanca game in which he displayed deeper
understanding of the position, better endgame technique and greater tactical
awareness and imagination than his opponent.} 0-1

[Event "Moscow"]
[Site "Moscow URS"]
[Date "1936.06.03"]
[Round "15"]
[White "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Black "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "B83"]
[PlyCount "116"]
[EventDate "1936.05.14"]

1. e4 c5 {It is interesting to observe that Capablanca even took up the
Sicilian in his later years.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 {The
Rauzer! Nowadays, this variation is not that often on the highest level, but
it still appears here and then. The most prominent  case was the Karjakin -
Caruana Candidates 2016 final round clash (the famous rook sacrifice game).} 6.
Be2 {Nowadays Bg5 is regarded as the main answer} Bd7 7. Be3 e6 8. Qd2 a6 9. f4
{[pgndiagram] So, the game transposes to some sort of Scheveningen position in
the end} Qc7 {I am not 100% sure that deciding on the position of the Queen so
early is the best approach} (9... b5 10. a3 Be7 11. Bf3 Rc8 {Is already very
modern approach.}) (9... Be7 10. O-O-O b5 11. a3 b4 {Is also possible.}) 10.
Nb3 $1 {Very good play by Lasker. He spends a tempo in order to prevent the
counterplay} (10. O-O-O b5 11. Nb3 (11. a3 Rc8 12. Nb3 b4 {Would create quick
counterplay for Black}) 11... Rb8 {With b4 coming soon and unpleasant turn of
events for White}) 10... b5 11. Bf3 $6 (11. a3 {Was now correct. Black's ideas
reach some sort of a dead end, since White hasn't yet castled.} b4 (11... Rb8
12. g4 {And the status quo is maintained}) 12. axb4 Nxb4 13. O-O {Is the point.
White can castle on the kingside and then start attacking on the queenside})
11... Rb8 $6 (11... b4 {Was now possible, with good position for Black}) 12.
Ne2 $2 (12. a3 {Once again ignoring this move. But I guess that this idea,
which has nowadays become typical, was going against the principles of those
times (never play on the side of the board where your opponent is attacking).
This nuance is the best demonstration of the effect computers have had on the
game}) 12... Be7 (12... a5 {Going all in on the queenside was now perfectly
possible. Since White is unable to castle queenside and attack immediately,
Black can simply improve his position first and then catch up with development}
) 13. O-O O-O 14. Ng3 {[pgndiagram] Still, this knight does very little on
this square and Black has a very good Sicilian} a5 {Better late then never} 15.
Nd4 $6 (15. Nc1 {Rerouting the knight on d3 is what engine wants to do. Still,
the thematic Sicilian move leads to a good game for Black} Rfc8 16. Nd3 e5 $1)
15... a4 (15... Nxd4 16. Bxd4 e5 17. fxe5 dxe5 18. Be3 g6 {And Black will
stomp White in the centre and on the c-file (observe how the knight on g3
doesn't have any sensible square to jump to)}) 16. Rae1 (16. c3 {Reinforcing
the knight is a possibility, but it weakens the c4 square} Na5 17. Qc1 Nc4 {
And Black is in great shape}) 16... Rfc8 {[pgndiagram] Somehow, quietly,
Capablanca creates a model Sicilian game. True, Lasker's treatment of the
position is not particularly impressive, but Capa, thanks to his incredible
talent, somehow understands what he needs to do without having a vast Sicilian
experience} 17. Rf2 $6 {Another passive move, defending the c2 pawn} (17. Nxc6
{Came to mind, defending the pawn in a crude way} Bxc6 18. c3 b4 19. Rc1 {And
although White is on the back foot, nothing is over yet}) 17... Ne8 $6 {
[pgndiagram] So far Capablanca has played excellently, but here he starts
being too cautious} (17... b4 {Simply continuing the attack was s strong} 18.
Nxc6 Bxc6 19. f5 e5 {And Black has a clear advantage}) 18. Nxc6 Bxc6 19. f5 {
Now with the knigh on e8, things aren't as simple} e5 20. Nh5 $6 (20. c3 {Was
a possibility, preventing b4 for the moment} b4 21. cxb4 d5 22. Bc5 Bxc5 23.
bxc5 d4 {And now the amazing shot} 24. f6 Nxf6 25. Nf5 {Creates some breathing
space for White and enables him to hold}) 20... Qb7 {Increasing pressure on e4,
which has been weakened by the departure of the g3 knight} 21. Bg5 f6 $1 {A
good decision, preventing the creation of attacking chances on the kingside.
It is not that hard to carry the d5 advance out, after which seemingly dead
bishop will breathe once again} (21... Bxg5 22. Qxg5 f6 23. Qe3 d5 {Was also
good for Black though. The knight on h5 is once again out of play}) 22. Be3 Nc7
{Another piece controls the d5 square} (22... Bxe4 {Is unecessary} 23. Bxe4
Qxe4 24. Ba7 Qc4 25. Bxb8 Rxb8 {Although even here Black retains some advantage
}) 23. Qd1 $1 {A good move, intending Rd2, adding defenders to d5} Rd8 24. Rd2
Kh8 $6 (24... d5 {Was possible nevertheless} 25. exd5 Bxd5 26. Bxd5+ Rxd5 27.
Rxd5 Nxd5 28. Bf2 Rc8 {And Black has a clear advantage}) 25. a3 (25. Bf2 {This
defensive move, removing the bishop from the attack of the knight on d5
probably made more sense than the a3 advance} d5 26. exd5 Bxd5 27. Bxd5 Rxd5
28. Rxd5 Nxd5 29. Qf3 Nb4 30. c3 Qxf3 31. gxf3 Nd3 32. Rd1 {And White might be
able to hold this. But who knows}) 25... Rbc8 $2 {Again refraining from d5,
which would have been very strong once again} 26. Bf2 d5 $1 {[pgndiagram]
Finally, this advance comes. But the fact that White has played a3 and Bf2 has
reduced the effect of this push. Somewhat} 27. exd5 Nxd5 28. Bxd5 $2 {But this
is the losing decision. Giving up the bishop pair in this position is just a
terrible decision} (28. c3 {And White might be able to hold. It was time to
strengthen the defences}) 28... Rxd5 29. Qg4 {I am not sure whether Lasker
simply acted out of desperation, or overestimated the power of the mating
threat} Bc5 (29... Bf8 30. Red1 Rxd2 31. Rxd2 b4 {And Black is in great shape})
(29... Rg8 {Preparing to open the g-file, is the computer's suggestion. As
usual, it starts looking terribly dangerous after one stops to evaluate its
consequences}) 30. Red1 Bxf2+ 31. Kxf2 Rcd8 {[pgndiagram] Capablanca could
have extracted more after Lasker's unfortunate decision, but even so, he
retains the superior position. The knight on h5 is once again the main reason
for that (Am I starting to repeat myself too often?)} 32. Rxd5 Rxd5 33. Rxd5
Bxd5 34. Qb4 $2 {Just as he came close to equalizing, Lasker suddenly makes an
active move that turns out to be a decisive mistake. His king gets chased into
open} (34. Kg1 {Was very safe move, securing the king.  Black retains the edge,
but scoring a full point from this position wuld require a lot of work and
cooperation from the opponent}) 34... Qa7+ 35. Ke2 (35. Kf1 Bc4+ {And White
would get mated}) 35... Bc4+ 36. Kf3 (36. Ke1 Qe3+ {Is mate in five} (36...
Qg1+ 37. Kd2 Qxg2+ {Also wins})) 36... Qa8+ $2 {[pgndiagram] Not the best .} (
36... e4+ $3 {Was incredibly strong resource in this position} 37. Kg4 (37.
Kxe4 Qa8+ 38. Kf4 Qxg2 39. Qf8+ Bg8 {And Black is winning}) 37... Be2+ 38. Kf4
Qb8+ 39. Ke3 Bxh5 {And Black wins}) 37. Kf2 Qd8 {Now White has some defensive
chances, but he needs to find a series of very precise moves, which turns out
to be impossible task even for the great Emanuel} 38. Qc3 (38. Ke1 {Taking the
control of the entry points along the d-file with the king, was the most
precise. Naturally, the engine immediately finds all the resources in the
position}) 38... Qd1 {Capablanca immediately takes the opportunity to activate
the Queen and gains a tempo along the way} 39. Ng3 h6 $1 {[pgndiagram] Another
strong move. Capa creates some space for his king and gets rid of the back
rank issues once and for all} 40. b3 $6 {From frying pan to fire} axb3 41. cxb3
Bxb3 (41... Qxb3 {Only not this capture} 42. Qxb3 Bxb3 43. Ne4 Bd5 44. Nd6 Bc6
45. Nc8 {And Black has troubles in converting his advantage}) 42. Nf1 Qb1 43.
g4 Bc4 {The game is essentialy over. Black has an additional pawn and a safer
king} 44. Ne3 Qh1 45. Nxc4 Qxh2+ 46. Ke1 Qg1+ 47. Kd2 Qf2+ 48. Kc1 Qf1+ 49. Kd2
bxc4 {Now only precision is required. And Capa never lacked precision in
winning endgames} 50. a4 Qf4+ 51. Kc2 Qxg4 52. a5 Qxf5+ 53. Kc1 Qf2 54. Qa3 Kh7
{[pgndiagram] Here Lasker finally resigned. The a pawn advance is countered}
55. a6 e4 56. a7 c3 57. a8=Q Qd2+ 58. Kb1 c2+ {And White will soon get mated}
0-1

[Event "Hastings"]
[Site "Hastings ENG"]
[Date "1919.08.11"]
[Round "1"]
[White "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Black "Fred Dewhurst Yates"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C90"]
[PlyCount "121"]
[EventDate "1919.08.11"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 {[pgndiagram] Nowadays, this continuation has
been pretty much overshadowed by the infamous Berlin endgame starting with 3...
Nf6.   But the text move is very much playable; the recent Carlsen - Karjakin
World Championship match saw the defending champion using it on a regular
basis.} 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 (7... O-O {Would be an
invitation to Marshall} 8. c3 d5 {Interestingly enough, Frank James Marshall
first played this pawn sacrifice against none other but Jose Raul Capablanca
himself. The Cuban managed to defend and even win the game in the end.}) 8. c3
Na5 {With this move Black chooses to play the so-called Chigorin variation of
the Ruy Lopez.} 9. Bc2 c5 10. d4 Qc7 11. Nbd2 Bg4 $6 {[pgndiagram] But this
move is not the best. Usually this move puts pressure on the d4 pawn, but here
White can simply close the center and the text move loses its point somewhat} (
11... cxd4 {Is the main move nowadays.} 12. cxd4 Bg4 $5 (12... Nc6 13. d5 Nb4
14. Bb1 {Is advantageous for White}) 13. d5 O-O {And the opening of the c-file
is in Black's favour}) 12. d5 (12. h3 {Might have some point here, forcing the
bishop to show its hand.} Bh5 13. d5 {And Black has lost the option of
retreating the bishop toward queenside.}) 12... g5 $2 {And this agression is
just plain bad, that shows that the level of understanding of similar Ruy
Lopez positions was very low at the time.   It is not possible for Black to
create an attack and this move simply creates a weakness on the kingside.
Usually Black castles kingside, but after this lunge he is unable to hide his
king there.} (12... O-O 13. h3 Bd7 14. b3 {Leads to normal Spanish position in
which White retains better chances}) 13. Nf1 $1 {[pgndiagram] Natural and good.
White immediately reroutes his knight toward the weakened f5 square and gains
a tempo by attacking g5 on the way} h6 (13... Rg8 {Is by no means better} 14.
h3 Bxf3 15. Qxf3 g4 16. hxg4 Nxg4 17. Ng3 {And Black doesn't have enough
pieces on the kingside to create real chances}) 14. Ng3 Rd8 $6 {I fail to
understand the purpose of this move} (14... Bd7 {Taking measures against a4
ideas an preparing the g4 advance was more sensible} 15. a4 {All the same!} b4
16. cxb4 cxb4 17. Bd3 Nb7 {And White is better, but Black's position is better
than the one he got in the game}) 15. a4 $1 {[pgndiagram] Now this move is
even stronger - the a6 pawn has been left undefended} b4 16. cxb4 cxb4 17. Bd3
{Black has to spend an additional move to defend a6} Bc8 {Obviously not a move
made willingly.} 18. Be3 (18. Bd2 {Not allowing Ng4 might have been even better
} Qb7 19. Rc1 Nd7 20. Be3 {And White has an excellent position}) 18... Ng4 19.
Rc1 $6 {Once again, Bd2, not allowing the exchane of the bishop, was the
alternative} (19. Bd2 Qb7 20. Rc1 Bd7 21. b3 {And White can slowly strengthen
his position (h3-Re2-Bishop moves - Rec2 etc)}) 19... Qb8 $2 {Black should
have taken the opportunity and exchanged White's bishop} (19... Nxe3 20. Rxc7
Nxd1 21. Rxd1 g4 22. Ne1 {And White is clearly better, but it is not
completely over yet}) 20. Bd2 {[pgndiagram] Capa refuses to give Yates another
opportunity} Qb6 21. Qe2 Nb3 22. Rc6 $1 Qa5 (22... Qxf2+ 23. Qxf2 Nxf2 24. Kxf2
g4 {Was a possible tactical idea, but White has an easy way of parrying this
idea} 25. Bc2 Na5 26. Rb6 $1 (26. Ng1 Nxc6 27. dxc6 d5 {Is less clear}) 26...
gxf3 27. Bxb4 {And White is winning}) 23. Bxa6 (23. h3 {Was probably even
stronger} Nf6 24. Be3 {With the deadly threat of Bb6} Nd7 25. Bc2 Nbc5 26. Nd2
{And White is winning; there is no defence against Nc4}) 23... Bd7 24. Bb5 $1 {
Still, this is a very attractive exchange sacrifice, which would make Tigran
Petrosian particularly proud.} Bxc6 25. Bxc6+ (25. dxc6 $5 {Is also possible}
Kf8 26. Nf5) 25... Kf8 26. Qc4 Nxd2 27. Nxd2 Qa7 28. Qe2 h5 29. Nf5 Bf6 30. Nc4
{[pgndiagram] The positioning and harmony of White's pieces is aesthetically
pleasing.} Qc5 31. b3 (31. a5 {Pushing the pawn immediately, was even stronger}
Nh6 32. Nxh6 Rxh6 33. a6 Kg7 34. Ra1 {And White is going to win soon}) 31...
Nh6 32. Nxh6 Rxh6 33. Qe3 {[pgndiagram] Capablanca once again doesn't hurry,
but realizes that Black s unable to disentangle himself and improves the
position of his pieces therefore.} (33. a5 $5) 33... Rc8 (33... Qxe3 34. Nxe3 {
Is bad for Black; he is unable to move his pieces due to the knight coming to
f5. It will be hard to defend the d6 pawn} Be7 {Is too slow} 35. Nf5 Rf6 36. a5
Rc8 37. a6) 34. Rc1 $2 {Again this leaning toward simplicity that Kasparov
criticizes in his books. With this move Capa presents Yates with a real
opportunity to get back into the game} (34. Qxc5 dxc5 35. Rd1 {Was most likely
winning as well. It is hard to suggest a move for White} Ke7 (35... Kg7 36. Nd6
) 36. a5) 34... Bd8 $2 (34... Qxe3 {Was now possible. With the rook on c8
Black has gained some defensive possibilities} 35. Nxe3 Bd8 $1 36. Nf5 (36. Ra1
{Is probably better} Rc7 37. a5 Ra7 38. a6 {But conversion of the advantage is
no longer a pure formality}) 36... Rf6 {And the a5 has been neutralized. White
is probably only slightly better here}) 35. Qxc5 dxc5 36. Nxe5 {[pgndiagram]
Now White wins two additional pawns and his task is much simpler. The soldier
on c5 falls as well} Ke7 (36... Be7 {Is also hopeless} 37. Nd7+ Kg7 38. Nxc5)
37. Rxc5 f5 38. Rc4 (38. exf5 {Wins as well}) 38... Ba5 39. Bb5 Rxc4 40. Nxc4
Bc7 41. e5 Bb8 42. Ne3 {[pgndiagram] Now the f5 pawn falls as well.} Rh7 43.
Nxf5+ Kf7 44. e6+ Kf6 45. e7 $6 {The question mark is for lazyness; once again
we can observe how Capablanca refuses to calculate variations but simply plays
the "obvious moves"} (45. Nd4 Bd6 46. Nc6 {Was simpler, not entering the
position with opposite coloured bishops as yet}) 45... Rxe7 46. Nxe7 Kxe7 47.
g3 {[pgndiagram] Three extra pawns are enough to outweigh the drawish
tendencies of the opposite coloured bishops endgame} Bc7 48. Kg2 Kd6 49. Be8 h4
50. Bf7 Ke5 51. Kh3 Bd8 52. Kg4 hxg3 53. fxg3 Kf6 54. Be6 Kg6 55. d6 Kf6 56.
Bf5 Bb6 57. d7 Bd8 58. h4 gxh4 59. gxh4 {The third passed pawn decides the
issue} Bc7 60. h5 Kg7 61. Be4 {[pgndiagram] And here Yates finally decided he
has had enough and resigned. All the criticism aside, this is a beautiful
positional game in which Cuban demonstrated deeper understanding of the Ruy
Lopez.} 1-0

[Event "New York"]
[Site "New York, NY USA"]
[Date "1927.02.24"]
[Round "5"]
[White "Alexander Alekhine"]
[Black "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "E16"]
[PlyCount "84"]
[EventDate "1927.02.19"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 {The Queen's Indian !? In their subsequent match
Capa would only play the Queen's gambit declined.} 4. g3 Bb7 5. Bg2 c5 6. d5 $1
{This move is typical of Alekhine's style. He sacrifices a pawn in the opening
in order to gain the initiative} exd5 7. Nh4 $1 {This idea would later become
standard. White exploits the pin on the h1-a8 diagonal and eyes the f5 square
with his knight.} g6 {[pgndiagram] Black probably has to make this move sooner
or later} (7... d6 8. Nc3 Be7 (8... g6 {Would transpose back to the game}) 9.
cxd5 O-O 10. Nf5 {And White is better}) 8. Nc3 Bg7 9. O-O O-O 10. Bf4 $6 {So
far Alekhine has played strongly, but here he makes the second best move} (10.
Bg5 {Trying to place a piece on d5 was better} h6 11. Bxf6 Qxf6 12. Nxd5) (10.
cxd5 {Recapturing immediately and delaying the development of the dark squared
bishop was also possible}) 10... d6 11. cxd5 Nh5 $5 {Emphasizing the drawback
of White's 10th move} 12. Bd2 Nd7 13. f4 $6 {Preventing Ne5, but pawns don't
move backwards} (13. e4 {Was more logical} Ba6 14. Re1 Ne5 15. Qa4 Bd3 {With
approximately even position}) 13... a6 $6 {Intending to advance the b5, but
Black had a stronger alternative available} (13... f5 $5 {Preventing e4, was
good for Black} 14. e4 fxe4 15. Nxe4 Bxb2 16. Nxd6 Ba6 $1) 14. Bf3 $6 (14. a4 {
Preventing b5 was the best} f5 15. e4 {With a6 and a4 included, f5 is not that
strong anymore} fxe4 16. Nxe4 Bxb2 17. Nxd6 {And here Ba6 is not possible and
White's advantage is almost decisive already}) 14... Nhf6 (14... b5 $5 {'A la
Fischer' was possible. But Fischer wasn't even born at that particular moment}
15. Bxh5 gxh5 16. e4 b4 {And Black starts taking over}) 15. a4 (15. e4 b5 16.
Qc2 Re8 17. Rae1 c4 {And Black has a very favourable version of the Benoni})
15... c4 $1 {Opening the c5 square for the knight} 16. Be3 (16. e4 {Comes too
late} Nc5 17. f5 b5 {Is just horrible for White}) 16... Qc7 17. g4 $6 {
[pgndiagram] Here White starts an unjustified attack. He had to think about
defending and equalizing in this position} (17. b3 {Getting rid of the c4
wedge was sensible.} cxb3 18. Qxb3 Nc5 19. Qb4 Nfd7 20. Rad1 {And White is
very much in the game}) (17. Bd4 {Would be desirable, but the problem is that
Black knight on b3 will hit the bishop} Nc5 18. Ra3 Nb3 {And White has to give
up the exchange or retreat the bishop. Whatever he does, he remains in an
inferior position}) 17... Nc5 18. g5 (18. Bd4 Nb3 {Is bad}) 18... Nfd7 19. f5 {
Once you have said A you have to say B. But at this point already, this move
might be the best practical chance} (19. Bd4 {Would once again run into forks
on b3} Bxd4+ 20. Qxd4 Nb3) (19. Qc2 Nb3 20. Rad1 Ndc5 {Was very unpleasant as
well}) 19... Rfe8 20. Bf4 Be5 $1 {[pgndiagram] Refusing to let the
imprisonment of the bishop after f6. Capa realizes that with the pawn on g5 it
is much harder for his opponent to exploit the weakening of the dark square
complex around his king} 21. Bg4 (21. Bxe5 Nxe5 {And it is clear that White
has zero attacking chances and that a very difficult defensive task lays in
front of him}) 21... Nb3 22. fxg6 $5 {[pgndiagram] Official desperation, but
objectively the best chance} (22. Ra2 Qc5+ 23. Kh1 Bxc3 24. bxc3 Bxd5+ {And
Black is completely winning}) 22... hxg6 (22... Nxa1 {Naturally, would be very
stupid} 23. gxf7+ Kxf7 24. Bxe5+) 23. Rb1 Bxc3 24. bxc3 Qc5+ 25. e3 Ne5 $1 {
Another piece joins the action} (25... Rxe3 {Black's position is so dominating,
that even this exchange sacrifice was possible here} 26. Bxe3 Qxe3+ 27. Kh1 Ne5
28. Ng2 Qxc3 {And the difference in the activity of the pieces is very obvious}
) 26. Bf3 (26. Bxe5 {Is terrible} Qxe3+ 27. Rf2 Qxe5) 26... Nd3 {[pgndiagram]
The Black knights create a remarkable impression and the b3-d3 duo dominates
the board.} 27. Kh1 Bxd5 28. Rxb3 {Here Alekhine could have resigned} Nxf4 (
28... cxb3 {Was even simpler} 29. Qxd3 Bc4) 29. Rb1 Rxe3 30. Ng2 Rxf3 31. Rxf3
Nxg2 32. Kxg2 Re8 33. Kf1 Bxf3 34. Qxf3 Qxg5 35. Re1 Rxe1+ 36. Kxe1 Qg1+ 37.
Kd2 Qxh2+ 38. Kc1 Qe5 39. Kb2 Kg7 40. Qf2 b5 41. Qb6 bxa4 42. Qxa6 Qe2+ {
[pgndiagram] A game in which Capablanca demonstrated superb handling of the
Benoni-like position. And you have to bear in mind that his opponent was not
"some Yates", but a future challenger and a master of dynamic positions,
Alexander Alekhine himself.   Wins such as this reinforced the myth about his
invulnerability. And indeed, after analyzing a game such as this one it is
very hard to imagine that only a couple of months later Alekhine managed to
score three wins in a short span of 12 games.} 0-1

[Event "Hastings"]
[Site "Hastings ENG"]
[Date "1895.08.17"]
[Round "10"]
[White "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Black "Curt von Bardeleben"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C54"]
[PlyCount "69"]
[EventDate "1895.08.05"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d4 exd4 6. cxd4 Bb4+ 7. Nc3 {
[pgndiagram] Sharp gambits such as this one were quite popular in the 19th
century.} d5 {Not a critical reply} (7... Nxe4 {"The best way of refuting a
gambit is to accept it"} 8. O-O Bxc3 {And now White has a choice} (8... Nxc3 9.
bxc3 Bxc3 10. Qb3 {Is very dangerous for Black}) 9. bxc3 {[pgndiagram] And the
tabiya of the variation is reached} (9. d5 $5 {Is probably playable,
especially if Black doesn't know what he is doing}) 9... d5 (9... Nxc3 10. Qe1+
) 10. Bd3) 8. exd5 {Now White has the initiative} Nxd5 9. O-O Be6 (9... Bxc3 {
Here Black can't go for the pawn} 10. bxc3 Nxc3 {loses to} 11. Qe1+) (9... Nxc3
{Again loses to} 10. bxc3 Bxc3 11. Qb3) 10. Bg5 Be7 (10... Qd7 {Was arguably
better} 11. Nxd5 Bxd5 12. Qb3 Bxf3 {[pgndiagram]} (12... Bxc4 13. Qxc4 O-O 14.
d5) 13. Qxf3 O-O {And Black will have to suffer, but his position is
definitely defensible}) 11. Bxd5 $1 {[pgndiagram] A very counterintuitive
exchange. You can feel the understanding Steinitz had for the game from moves
like these. His games are full of deep strategic ideas.} Bxd5 {The right
recapture} (11... Bxg5 {Is horrible for Black} 12. Bxe6 fxe6 13. d5 {And Black
is close to being lose} exd5 14. Re1+ Kf7 15. Nxd5 {[pgndiagram] With such a
king and undeveloped rooks, Black is doomed}) 12. Nxd5 Qxd5 $1 {Once again the
right choice} (12... Bxg5 {Is bad due to} 13. Re1+ {The most clear cut} (13.
Nxg5 {But not} Qxd5) (13. Nxc7+ {Wins a pawn} Qxc7 14. Nxg5) 13... Be7 14. Qb3
{[pgndiagram] And White has a winning position. Here it pays not to go for an
endgame, but to continue building up some pressure}) 13. Bxe7 Nxe7 14. Re1 {
[pgndiagram] If only Black could castle, he would have no problems whatsoever.
As it is, White has the initiative} f6 (14... Qd7 {Would allow the extra tempo
on the knight} 15. Ne5 Qd8 16. Qf3 {And White is winning}) (14... Qd6 {Would
likely transpose, although the queen on d6 is subjecto to knight attack from
e4 or c4} 15. Qe2 f6) 15. Qe2 Qd7 16. Rac1 $2 {[pgndiagram] So far the game
has been of a rather high quality. This move releases the tension} (16. d5 Kf7
17. Rad1 {With the idea of d6 push would maintain White's initiative}) 16... c6
$2 {And immediately Bardeleben returns the favour} (16... Kf7 {Was correct.
Black shouldn't have feared the potential sacrifice of the knight} 17. Ne5+
fxe5 18. dxe5 Qe6 19. Qf3+ Kg8 20. Rxc7 {[pgndiagram] Although this is highly
unpleasant, he should be able to unravel his forces and fight for the draw})
17. d5 $1 {[pgndiagram] A thematic clearance move.Now White is winning. The
way Steinitz concludes the game is highly spectacular} cxd5 18. Nd4 {The
knight is coming to e6 with great effect} Kf7 19. Ne6 Rhc8 (19... Nc6 {Was no
better, due to the weakness of d5 pawn} 20. Nc5 Qd6 (20... Qc7 21. Qh5+ {Wins
immediately}) 21. Nxb7 Qd7 22. Nc5 {And White retains the dominating position})
20. Qg4 {Now the g7 also is weak} g6 21. Ng5+ $1 {[pgndiagram] A highly
attractive finish and one of the most remarkable combinations in the history
of chess.} Ke8 22. Rxe7+ $3 {A fantastic blow. At first sight it seems that
White will overextend himself, because his back rank is weak. But Steinitz has
calculted everything precisely} Kf8 $1 {[pgndiagram] The best chance} (22...
Kxe7 23. Qb4+ {Wins} Ke8 24. Re1+ {[pgndiagram]} Kd8 25. Ne6+ {And Black has
to give up his queen due to mate on f8. And luckily for him, White has Re1 to
prevent back rank mate after he takes on e6} Qxe6 26. Rxe6 Rc1+ 27. Re1) 23.
Rf7+ $1 {The combination continues} Kg8 24. Rg7+ {Naturally, now Black can't
take the rook on g7 due to capture on d7 with check} Kh8 25. Rxh7+ {
[pgndiagram] Here Bardeleben left the game. Steinitz willingly demonstrated
the spectators what awaited his opponent had he continued the game} Kg8 26.
Rg7+ Kh8 (26... Kf8 27. Nh7+) 27. Qh4+ Kxg7 28. Qh7+ Kf8 29. Qh8+ Ke7 30. Qg7+
Ke8 31. Qg8+ Ke7 32. Qf7+ Kd8 33. Qf8+ Qe8 34. Nf7+ Kd7 35. Qd6# {[pgndiagram]
And at the end of the story, everything concludes with an epaulette mate.
Fantastic} 1-0

[Event "Steinitz - Chigorin World Championship "]
[Site "Havana CUB"]
[Date "1892.01.07"]
[Round "4"]
[White "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Black "Mikhail Chigorin"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C65"]
[PlyCount "57"]
[EventDate "1892.??.??"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 {[pgndiagram] The way of avoiding Berlin
even in the 19th century.} d6 5. c3 g6 {The fianchetto against d3 and c3 is
encountered even today on an occasion.} 6. Nbd2 Bg7 7. Nf1 {But I am not a big
fan of this move. White violates one of the basic principles of not moving the
same piece twice in the opening.  Although the position is somewhat slow and
closed enough, I think that he loses some of his flexibility; the knight might
take the alternative Nc3-Ne3 route in the future if neccessary.} (7. O-O {
Seems more natural}) 7... O-O 8. Ba4 $6 {But this loses another tempo} (8. Ng3
{Was more consistent} a6 9. Ba4 b5 10. Bc2 Re8 {And typical Ruy Lopez struggle
lays ahead}) 8... Nd7 $6 {[pgndiagram] Chigorin also moves his knight via c5
to e6, but this removes the knight from the kingside and loses time.} (8... d5
{Was possible, exploiting White's delay in the opening.} 9. Bxc6 {The point is
that White can't really capture the pawn on e5} bxc6 10. Nxe5 Re8 {And White
is too underdeveloped. Black has fantastic compensation and is probably even
close to winning.}) 9. Ne3 $6 (9. Ng3 {Was probably better, in order to
discourage the f5 idea. The point is that on g3 knight doesn't block the
bishop; the g5 square is defended} f5 10. exf5 gxf5 11. Bb3+ Kh8 12. Ng5 {
[pgndiagram] And Black is in trouble}) 9... Nc5 $6 {Chasing the bishop where
it wants to go} (9... f5 {Was now interesting}) 10. Bc2 Ne6 11. h4 $1 {
[pgndiagram] Great understanding of the game by Steinitz. He recognizes that
Black is not in control in the centre, so he begins a wing attack} Ne7 $2 {And
immediately there comes a mistake. Chigorin plays according to the principles
of the old school: attack on a flank should be countered by the centar blow.
However, White's centre is very stable here} (11... h5 {Was the way to go,
although they simply didn't move pawns in front of their king in those times.})
12. h5 d5 13. hxg6 fxg6 (13... hxg6 {Leads by force to a dominating endgame
for White} 14. Ng4 (14. exd5 Nxd5 15. Nxd5 Qxd5 {Is not that convincing any
more}) 14... dxe4 15. dxe4 Qxd1+ 16. Bxd1 Nc6 17. Nh6+ Bxh6 18. Bxh6 Re8 19.
Bc2 {[pgndiagram] And White is playing for two results only}) 14. exd5 Nxd5 15.
Nxd5 Qxd5 16. Bb3 {[pgndiagram] The b3-g8 diagonal is weak and Steinitz
immediately takes advantage of that} Qc6 17. Qe2 Bd7 18. Be3 Kh8 19. O-O-O Rae8
20. Qf1 $5 {Removing the queen from the bishop battery and preparing the d4
advance. This move is fantastic even if it objectively isn't the best} a5 $2 {
The final mistake.} (20... h5 {Black should have taken measures on the
kingside. This move counters White's prepared combination and allows Black to
successfuly defend}) 21. d4 $1 {Now Steinitz carries out another remarkable
combination} exd4 22. Nxd4 Bxd4 {[pgndiagram] The spectacular finish is near.
It is a great exercise to stop here and try to find the winning combination on
your own} 23. Rxd4 $1 Nxd4 24. Rxh7+ $3 {[pgndiagram] Black gets mated} Kxh7
25. Qh1+ Kg7 26. Bh6+ Kf6 27. Qh4+ Ke5 28. Qxd4+ {And here Chigorin resigned,
one move before checkmate} Kf5 29. g4# {[pgndiagram] A celebrated game that
demonstrated that in strategical positions Chigorin couldn't compete with
Steinitz on equal terms.} 1-0

[Event "London"]
[Site "London"]
[Date "1862.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Black "Augustus Mongredien"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B01"]
[PlyCount "57"]
[EventDate "1862.??.??"]

1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 Qd8 $5 {[pgndiagram] It is interesting to see
this line of Scandinavian in a game from 1862.} 4. d4 e6 {I am not an expert
on this variation, but I believe that Black should consider playing some other
moves and wait for an opportunity to pin the knight on f3. Here his light
squared bishop will remain a problem piece} (4... Nf6 5. Nf3 Bg4 {Comes to
mind. Although to be honest, I only have a vague idea about this variation
from the videos by IM John Bartholomew on youtube}) 5. Nf3 Nf6 6. Bd3 Be7 (6...
c5 {Transposes to the position from the French defense where Black is a tempo
down} 7. O-O cxd4 8. Nxd4) 7. O-O O-O 8. Be3 {[pgndiagram] I am not certain
about this move. It does develop a piece, but the bishop on e3 is not optimal.}
(8. Ne4 {Waiting with the bishop, seems to me to be  more in the spirit of the
position.} Nxe4 9. Bxe4 Nd7 10. c3 Nf6 11. Bc2 {[pgndiagram] And White retains
the advantage}) 8... b6 9. Ne5 Bb7 10. f4 {Steinitz goes all in, but Black
could have defended} Nbd7 (10... Nc6 {Attacking d4 and threatening Nb4, was
interesting} 11. Be2 (11. a3 Nxd4 12. Bxh7+ Kxh7 13. Bxd4 Kg8 {[pgndiagram]
And Black is better}) 11... Nb4 12. Bf3 Bxf3 13. Rxf3 c5 {And Black has
equalized. White has no attack}) 11. Qe2 Nd5 (11... c5 {Was probably better
than the game continuation}) 12. Nxd5 exd5 (12... Bxd5 13. f5 {Gives White a
nice attack}) 13. Rf3 {Now White's pieces enjoy excellent prospects} f5 {Not a
move made willingly} (13... Nf6 14. Rh3 {White threatens a sacrifice on h7} Qd6
(14... c5 15. Bxh7+ Nxh7 16. Qh5 {Is winning}) 15. c3 c5 16. f5 Rae8 17. Rg3 {
[pgndiagram] And White has a strong attack}) 14. Rh3 (14. Rg3 {Computer
indicates that this is the stronger continuation of attack.} g6 (14... c5 15.
Qh5) 15. Nxg6 {This is the point. White prevents g6 and includes his queen in
the attack as well}) 14... g6 {Black has to prevent the move Qh5} (14... Nxe5
15. fxe5 c5 16. Qh5 h6 17. Rg3) (14... c5 15. Qh5 h6 16. Rg3) 15. g4 $1 {Going
for it. The computer doesn't like this move, but for a human this is very
attractive} fxg4 $4 {[pgndiagram] And immediately there comes a big mistake,
which loses} (15... Nxe5 16. dxe5 (16. fxe5 f4) 16... d4 17. gxf5 (17. Bd2 Qd7)
17... dxe3 18. fxg6 {[pgndiagram] And here it is possible for Black to survive
with a series of only moves} Rxf4 $1 (18... Qd5 19. Bc4) 19. Rxh7 Qd5 20. Rh8+
Kg7 (20... Kxh8 21. Qh5+) 21. Rh7+ Kg8 {And in the end, everything ends with a
perpetual check}) 16. Rxh7 $1 {[pgndiagram] A brilliant conception and another
rook sacrifice on h7} Nxe5 (16... Kxh7 17. Qxg4 {And Black can't defend} Nxe5
18. fxe5 Qe8 19. Qh5+ Kg7 20. Qh6+ Kg8 21. Bxg6 {And wins}) 17. fxe5 Kxh7 (
17... Bg5 {Is another way of losing, in technical instead of the tactical way}
18. Bxg6 Rf3 19. Bf2) 18. Qxg4 {[pgndiagram] We have transposed to the
previously examined variation} Rg8 {Losing even faster than 18... Qe8} 19. Qh5+
Kg7 20. Qh6+ Kf7 21. Qh7+ Ke6 22. Qh3+ Kf7 23. Rf1+ Ke8 24. Qe6 Rg7 25. Bg5 Qd7
26. Bxg6+ Rxg6 27. Qxg6+ Kd8 28. Rf8+ Qe8 29. Qxe8# {[pgndiagram] A very nice
tactical bloodbath, although it is apparent that Steinitz's play was less
strategicaly founded (after all it was year 1862). Compared to the previous
two games, he went all in in this one, but the tactical finish was allowed
only by his opponent's mistakes; they didn't result from his strategical
superiority.} 1-0

[Event "London (England)"]
[Site "London (England)"]
[Date "1863.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Black "Augustus Mongredien"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B06"]
[PlyCount "43"]
[EventDate "1863.??.??"]

1. e4 g6 {I have chosen this game only because it featured the modern defence}
2. d4 Bg7 3. c3 {This move is death for every Modern player} b6 $5 {And with
this we have entered the uncharted waters.} 4. Be3 $5 {I think that
combination of Be3 and c3 is not good. The d4 square is safe enough. On e3
bishop might become a subject of the Ng4 in the future} Bb7 5. Nd2 d6 6. Ngf3
e5 {[pgndiagram] Generally speaking, the combination of the queenside
fianchetto together with the e5 advance should be condemned.} (6... Nd7 7. Bd3
Ngf6 {Might be wiser. Developing first and only then advancing}) 7. dxe5 dxe5
8. Bc4 {Now Black can't play Nf6 and has some tactical problems as well} Ne7 $2
{The losing move} (8... Nd7 {Was the best, trying to develop} 9. Ng5 Nh6 10.
O-O Qe7 11. b4 {And Black is on the back foot, but nothing is over}) 9. Qe2 $2
(9. Bxf7+ {Was winning immediately} Kxf7 10. Qb3+ Kf8 11. Ng5 Qe8 12. Ne6+ {
[pgndiagram] And White is winning}) 9... O-O $6 {another mistake. Castling
without the knight on f6 and with the rook on h1 is very dangerous} (9... Nc8 {
Is computer's idea, trying to place the knight on d6} 10. O-O-O Nd6 11. h4 {
And White is better, but not as much as in the game}) 10. h4 {[pgndiagram] A
familiar advance of Harry the h-pawn} Nd7 (10... h5 {Was no good here} 11. g4
hxg4 12. Nh2 {And White is going to break through}) 11. h5 {White has a
decisive attack} Nf6 12. hxg6 (12. Nxe5 {Was stronger} Nxh5 13. g4 Bxe5 14.
gxh5 {And Black is in deep trouble}) 12... Nxg6 $1 {Defending the e-pawn} (
12... hxg6 13. Nxe5) 13. O-O-O c5 14. Ng5 a6 $2 {A final mistake of the game}
15. Nxh7 $3 {[pgndiagram] Another sacrifice on h7 and another correct one} Nxh7
16. Rxh7 (16. Qh5 Nf6 17. Qxg6 {Was more precise, but White is winning all the
same}) 16... Kxh7 17. Qh5+ Kg8 18. Rh1 Re8 19. Qxg6 Qf6 20. Bxf7+ Qxf7 (20...
Kf8 {[pgndiagram] Also doesn't help} 21. Bxe8 Rxe8 22. Rh8+ Bxh8 23. Bh6+ Ke7
24. Bg5 {Is game over}) 21. Rh8+ {[pgndiagram]A finishing touch. White wins
the queen and the game} Kxh8 22. Qxf7 1-0

[Event "Anderssen - Steinitz"]
[Site "London ENG"]
[Date "1866.08.08"]
[Round "13"]
[White "Adolf Anderssen"]
[Black "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C65"]
[PlyCount "86"]
[EventDate "1866.07.18"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 d6 5. Bxc6+ bxc6 6. h3 g6 {[pgndiagram]
These Anti-Berlin lines with kingside fianchetto were championed successfully
by Steinitz} 7. Nc3 Bg7 8. O-O O-O 9. Bg5 h6 10. Be3 c5 11. Rb1 Ne8 12. b4 $1 {
[pgndiagram] A very modern interpretation of the opening for those years.
White recognizes that he has to play on the queenside and decides to undouble
the adversary pawns in order to open a file for his rook} cxb4 13. Rxb4 c5 14.
Ra4 $6 {Only this is slightly misguided. White removes his rook from the open
file} (14. Rb1 f5 15. Re1 f4 16. Bd2 {Is better, although from the practical
point of view, Black's position is much easier to play}) 14... Bd7 15. Ra3 f5
16. Qb1 Kh8 $6 (16... Nc7 $5 {Preventing Qb7, came into consideration} 17. Qb7)
17. Qb7 a5 18. Rb1 a4 19. Qd5 $2 {[pgndiagram] From this moment onwards,
White's game starts going downhill rapidly. His pieces are stuck on the
queenside and are unable to join the kingside and defend the king} (19. Qb6 {
Going for the queen exchange, was better. Black can't really avoid it} Qxb6 (
19... Qe7 20. Nd5 Qf7 21. Qb7 {And now Black's pieces are not coordinated}) 20.
Rxb6) 19... Qc8 20. Rb6 Ra7 21. Kh2 f4 $1 {[pgndiagram] Black finally decides
on this move. He will soon set his pawn chain in motion, and alraedy it is
very hard to suggest how White should counter that plan} 22. Bd2 g5 23. Qc4 Qd8
{Defending g5 and intending h5} 24. Rb1 $2 {Going from frying pan to fire} (24.
Nd5 {White had to seek tactical countermeasures. The point of this knight jump
is to open the diagonal for the slumbering bishop on d2} h5 25. Ra6 Rxa6 26.
Qxa6 g4 27. Ba5 Qb8 28. Nh4 {[pgndiagram] And White retains chances of
successful defence}) 24... Nf6 {But anyway, White's position is unpleasant} 25.
Kg1 (25. Nxa4 Qe8) 25... Nh7 $5 {h5-g4 is coming soon} 26. Kf1 $6 {White can't
escape with the king} (26. Qd5 {Was the last chance} Be8 {Keeping a4 defended}
27. Nb5 Ra6 28. Nh2 h5 {And Black has the initiative, but White can try to put
up some resistance}) 26... h5 27. Ng1 g4 {[pgndiagram] Now Black's attack is
simply irresistible} 28. hxg4 (28. f3 {Is also hopeless} gxh3 29. gxh3 Rg8)
28... hxg4 29. f3 Qh4 30. Nd1 Ng5 {[pgndiagram] All Black's pieces join the
attack and White doesn't have enough space to bring in his reserves.} 31. Be1
Qh2 32. d4 {This merely hastens the end, but White was doomed anyway. The rest
doesn't require any commentary} gxf3 33. gxf3 Nh3 34. Bf2 Nxg1 35. dxc5 Qh3+
36. Ke1 Nxf3+ 37. Rxf3 Qxf3 38. Nc3 dxc5 39. Bxc5 Rc7 40. Nd5 Rxc5 41. Qxc5
Qxe4+ 42. Kf2 Rc8 43. Nc7 Qe3+ {[pgndiagram] A fantastic win, demonstrating
Steinitz's superiority over his contemporaries in the strategical positions}
0-1

[Event "Steinitz - Blackburne"]
[Site "London ENG"]
[Date "1876.02.29"]
[Round "6"]
[White "Joseph Henry Blackburne"]
[Black "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C45"]
[PlyCount "132"]
[EventDate "1876.02.17"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 Qh4 {[pgndiagram] This variation of the
Scotch defence is nowadays considered dangerous. But Steinitz simply grabs the
pawn and defends it for the remainder of the game.} 5. Nb5 (5. Nc3 {Is the
modern move} Bb4 6. Be2 Qxe4 7. Ndb5 Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 Kd8 {[pgndiagram] And this
is the modern tabiya of the variation. Black has to be careful}) 5... Bb4+ 6.
Bd2 Qxe4+ 7. Be2 Kd8 {[pgndiagram] Black has given up a pawn for the right to
castle. Objectively speaking, White should have decent compensation for the
pawn, but it is not so easy to prove it.} 8. O-O Bxd2 9. Qxd2 (9. Nxd2 {
Already here, what could be more natural than bringing another piece in the
game?} Qf5 10. Nf3 d6 11. Nbd4 {[pgndiagram] And White's position is preferable
}) 9... a6 {Now Black has time to chase the knight away from d4} 10. N5a3 $6 (
10. N1c3 {Was better, not allowing Qd4} Qh4 11. Na3 d6 (11... b5 12. Nd5))
10... Qd4 11. Qg5+ Qf6 12. Qd2 $2 {[pgndiagram] Giving another pawn away is
too much} (12. Qc1 {Is ugly, but neccessary. It is clear that White has lost
the thread and that Black is taking over, but the game continues}) 12... Qxb2
13. Nc4 Qd4 $1 (13... Qxa1 14. Nc3 {Would be silly} Qxf1+ 15. Kxf1 Nge7 {And
Black's position is still better, but his undeveloped state gives White a lot
of counterplay}) 14. Qc1 $2 Nge7 $2 (14... Qxa1 {Was now possible} 15. Qa3 d6
16. Nc3 Qxf1+ 17. Kxf1 Nge7 {[pgndiagram] And the queen is sidelined on a3,
Black has played d6 and he has enough time to consolidate.}) 15. Nbd2 d6 {
[pgndiagram] Even so, Black has retained the advantage. Two pawns are too much}
16. Rd1 Be6 17. Qa3 Nd5 18. Nb3 Qc3 19. Bf1 (19. Nxd6 {Was the last chance}
cxd6 20. Qxd6+ Ke8 21. Rxd5 Bxd5 22. Qxd5 Rd8 23. Qf5 {And an exchange with
the open king gives some practical chances of salvation}) 19... Ndb4 {
[pgndiagram]Black has consolidated completely and now only needs to activate
his rooks} 20. Ne3 Re8 21. Rd2 {Desperation. White embarks on a faulty
combination} Bxb3 22. Rad1 Rxe3 $1 {[pgndiagram] A nice tactical refutation}
23. fxe3 Nxc2 24. Qc1 $2 (24. Qxb3 {Was the best, transposing into the lost
endgame} Qxb3 25. axb3 Nxe3 {And Black is winning. 4 pawns for the exchange
are simply too much}) 24... Qxe3+ 25. Kh1 Ba4 {[pgndiagram] Now Black is
easily winning (a piece and four pawns for the rook).} 26. Bc4 N2d4 27. Re1 Qf4
28. Rf1 Qh6 29. Qb2 Qe3 30. Bxf7 Bb5 31. Rfd1 Nf5 32. a4 Ne5 33. axb5 Nxf7 34.
Re2 Qh6 35. Qb3 axb5 36. g4 Nd4 37. Rxd4 Ra1+ 38. Kg2 Qf6 39. Rde4 Ne5 40. Rf2
Qg6 41. Ref4 c6 42. Qe3 Kc7 43. h3 h5 44. Rf5 hxg4 45. Rg5 {[pgndiagram]
Steinitz has complicated his task somewhat, but here he is clearly winning}
gxh3+ $4 {But suddenly, this is a huge mistake, after which Black is probably
not even better} (45... Qh6 $1 {Was easily winning}) 46. Kh2 Ra3 $2 (46... Qe8
{Was comparatively the best} 47. Rxg7+ Kb8 48. Qd4 Ra4 49. Qxd6+ Ka7 {And it
is doubtful whether Black can win this position}) 47. Qxe5 $1 dxe5 48. Rxg6 {
[pgndiagram] Now White is a full rook up and at least shouldn't lose} b4 49.
Rb2 (49. Rf7+ Kb6 50. Rgxg7 c5 51. Rxb7+ Kc6 52. Rgc7+ Kd5 {[pgndiagram] And
White has drawing decent drawing chances here}) 49... c5 50. Rf2 Rd3 51. Rc2 $2
{A step in the wrong direction, enticing Black to make a move he would like to
do anyway. Also, White pasivizes the rook} (51. Re2 {Forcing Black to defend
the pawn} Rd5 (51... c4 52. Rxe5 c3 53. Rc5+) 52. Rb2 {And then preventing the
b-pawn from advancing, was correct}) 51... b6 52. Re6 $2 (52. Rf2 {Was the
last chance} Rd6 53. Rxg7+ Kc6 54. Kxh3 c4 {Although even here, White's
position is unenviable}) 52... b3 {With this tempo, Black's pawns are simply
too strong} 53. Rb2 c4 54. Rxe5 Kc6 55. Rg5 Rd5 56. Rxg7 Kc5 57. Kxh3 Kb4 58.
Rb1 b5 59. Rg4 Rd2 60. Rg5 b2 61. Kg3 c3 62. Kf3 Kc4 63. Rgg1 Kb3 64. Ke3 Rd8
65. Rbf1 c2 66. Ke2 Ka2 {[pgndiagram] Despite the error in conversion, this
was a masterful defensive effort by Steinitz.} 0-1

[Event "Vienna"]
[Site "Vienna AUT"]
[Date "1873.08.12"]
[Round "7.2"]
[White "Adolf Anderssen"]
[Black "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C77"]
[PlyCount "90"]
[EventDate "1873.07.21"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 {One of the rare instances of Steinitz starting
with 3.. a6 instead of 3... Nf6 or 3... d6} 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. d3 {But still,
Anderssen chooses this move, leading to an Anti-Berlin, like position.} d6 6.
Bxc6+ bxc6 7. h3 g6 {[pgndiagram] And again we have the fianchetto and the
exhcange on c6, similarly to the previously examined encounter} 8. Nc3 Bg7 9.
Be3 Rb8 {This time Steinitz doesn't go for c5 but seizes the b-file himself}
10. b3 c5 11. Qd2 h6 {It is doubtful whether this move is necessary.} (11...
O-O {And it is doubtful whether White wants to exchange the dark squared
bishops; he can't follow it up with attack} 12. Bh6 Nh5 13. O-O-O f5) 12. g4 $2
{[pgndiagram] But this is completely uncalled for} (12. O-O {Was more natural,
leading to a balanced game} g5 $5 13. a3 g4 14. hxg4 Nxg4 15. b4 {[pgndiagram]
And the position is unclear}) 12... Ng8 $5 {Very strong play by Steinitz. He
starts moving his knight toward d4 immediately} (12... h5 {Also came into
consideration}) 13. O-O-O Ne7 14. Ne2 $6 {This knight is going nowhere.
Although the position is mildly unpleasant for White, notice how quickly
Steinitz will outplay his opponent} (14. Rdg1 Nc6 15. h4 Nd4 16. Qd1 Be6 17. h5
g5 18. Nd2 {Was correct} O-O 19. f3 c6 20. Na4 Qa5 21. Nc4 {[pgndiagram] And
White is slightly worse but he should be able to hold}) 14... Nc6 15. Qc3 {The
queen is completely missplaced here} Nd4 16. Nfg1 O-O 17. Ng3 Be6 18. N1e2 Qd7
19. Bxd4 cxd4 20. Qb2 a5 {[pgndiagram] Black has once again gained a strong
attack, while White's counterintuitive on the other wing is non existent} 21.
Kd2 (21. f4 {Was nevertheless better} Qe7 22. f5 Qg5+ 23. Kb1 Bd7 24. Rdf1 {
With at least some play}) 21... d5 $1 22. f3 Qe7 23. Rdf1 Qb4+ 24. Kd1 a4 25.
Rh2 c5 26. Nc1 c4 27. a3 Qe7 28. b4 c3 {[pgndiagram] White's queen is
completely sidelined and Black obtains free hands on the kingside. It is very
instructive to watch how Steinitz uses his greater control of space to quickly
shift his attention to the other side of the board} 29. Qa1 Qg5 30. Rff2 f5 31.
exf5 {[pgndiagram] Opening the kingside. White is completely lost} gxf5 32. h4
Qg6 33. Nxf5 $2 {Losing immediately} (33. gxf5 {Was slightly more prudent} Bxf5
34. Nxf5 Rxf5 35. Ne2 {Although even here White is doomed}) 33... Bxf5 34. gxf5
Rxf5 (34... Qg1+ 35. Ke2 e4 {Would be immediately decisive} 36. fxe4 dxe4 37.
dxe4 Rbe8) 35. Ne2 Rbf8 36. Qa2 Qf7 37. Rh3 Kh7 38. Ng1 (38. Kc1 {Would
prolong the resistance}) 38... Bf6 39. Ke2 Rg8 (39... e4 $1) 40. Kf1 Be7 41.
Ne2 Rh5 (41... Bxh4 $1) 42. f4 Bxh4 43. Rff3 e4 44. dxe4 Qg6 45. Ng3 Bxg3 {
[pgndiagram] An impressive victory, indicating once again the difference in
the level of understanding} 0-1

[Event "Steinitz - Zukertort World Championship"]
[Site "New York, NY USA"]
[Date "1886.01.11"]
[Round "1"]
[White "Johannes Zukertort"]
[Black "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "D11"]
[PlyCount "92"]
[EventDate "1886.??.??"]

1. d4 {It was rare to see queen's pawn moving on the first move in those times}
d5 2. c4 c6 {The Slav! In 1886!} 3. e3 Bf5 4. Nc3 e6 5. Nf3 Nd7 6. a3 $6 {A
sign of the times. Obviously there is a lot of theory and lot of possibilities.
I am sure I could write a good book on all the nuances and move orders here.}
Bd6 $5 {Provoking White's next move} 7. c5 $6 {Tempting but faulty. Black will
be able to break the bind by the timely e5 move} (7. Be2 {Is more natural} h6 {
Preventing Nh4 after Nf6} 8. O-O Ngf6 9. b3 {But then the whole point behind
a3 is gone}) 7... Bc7 8. b4 e5 $1 {Thematic in similar structures. White has a
bind on the queenside; Black has to break in the centre} 9. Be2 (9. dxe5 {
[pgndiagram] Was better here, not allowing e4 and freeing the d4 square for
the knight} Nxe5 10. Nd4 Bd7 11. f4 Ng6 12. Bd3 {With double edged position.
But this was already too modern for 1886}) 9... Ngf6 10. Bb2 e4 11. Nd2 h5 $5 {
Steinitz once again plays principled chess. He gains space on the kingside and
intends to mobilize his every single piece on that area of the board} 12. h3
Nf8 13. a4 Ng6 14. b5 Nh4 15. g3 Ng2+ $3 {[pgndiagram] To my mind this is a
fantastic move. Had such a conception been played in the 21st century,
everyone would assume it was computer preparation. However, it was 1886... 
Steinitz embarks on a modern knight sacrifice, for which he gains long term
compensation} 16. Kf1 Nxe3+ $1 {And here we go. Once again we see the concept
of space advantage utilized. White's pieces, stuck on the queenside will be
unable to come and defend White's king, who is stuck on the kingside} 17. fxe3
Bxg3 18. Kg2 Bc7 $2 {[pgndiagram] This is probably faulty retreat, as it gives
White enough time to organize} (18... h4 19. Nf1 (19. Qf1 {Is now not so good,
as the king can't escape} Be6) 19... Qb8 {Was strong} 20. Rb1 Bd7 $1 {
Defending c6} 21. Ba3 Rh6 {And White's position is highly unpleasant}) 19. Qg1
$2 {Returning the favour} (19. Qf1 {Gaining tempo on the bishop for the
vacation of the king was required} Be6 20. Kf2 Rh6 21. Ke1 {[pgndiagram] And
the king escapes}) 19... Rh6 20. Kf1 Rg6 21. Qf2 {[pgndiagram] Now White
doesn't have enough time. h3 pawn falls as well} Qd7 22. bxc6 bxc6 23. Rg1 {
White has to part with the h3 pawn} (23. h4 Bh3+ 24. Ke1 Bg3) 23... Bxh3+ 24.
Ke1 Ng4 {[pgndiagram] And just like that, Black is simply winning. Bear in
mind that Zuketort was the strongest player apart from Steinitz at that moment.
} 25. Bxg4 Bxg4 26. Ne2 Qe7 27. Nf4 Rh6 28. Bc3 g5 29. Ne2 Rf6 30. Qg2 {
[pgndiagram] White is getting steamrolled} Rf3 31. Nf1 Rb8 32. Kd2 f5 (32...
Bh3 {Was winning immediately} 33. Qxg5 Qxg5 34. Rxg5 Bxf1) 33. a5 f4 34. Rh1
Qf7 35. Re1 fxe3+ 36. Nxe3 Rf2 37. Qxf2 Qxf2 38. Nxg4 Bf4+ 39. Kc2 hxg4 40. Bd2
e3 41. Bc1 Qg2 42. Kc3 Kd7 43. Rh7+ Ke6 44. Rh6+ Kf5 45. Bxe3 Bxe3 46. Rf1+ Bf4
{[pgndiagram] One of the most underestimated games of all time. I have never
seen it before investigating for the article. Steinitz's conception and
position queen sacrifice was much ahead of his time.} 0-1

[Event "Steinitz - Zukertort World Championship"]
[Site "New Orleans, LA USA"]
[Date "1886.03.24"]
[Round "19"]
[White "Johannes Zukertort"]
[Black "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "D53"]
[PlyCount "58"]
[EventDate "1886.??.??"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 {[pgndiagram] Steinitz deviates from the Slav
which had brought him success earlier in the match} 4. Bg5 Be7 5. Nf3 O-O 6. c5
$2 {[pgndiagram] The best proof that queen's pawn openings were terra
incognita for Zukertort. This move gives Black immediate targets.} b6 7. b4
bxc5 $2 {Returning the favour} (7... a5 $1 {Was very strong} 8. a3 Ne4 9. Bxe7
Qxe7 10. Nxe4 dxe4 11. Ne5 axb4 12. axb4 Rxa1 13. Qxa1 f6 14. Nc4 Nc6 {
[pgndiagram] And Black is close to winning already}) 8. dxc5 $2 {The errors
continue} (8. bxc5 {Would give White a playable position} Ne4 9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10.
Qc2 Nxc3 11. Qxc3) 8... a5 9. a3 d4 $5 {[pgndiagram] Black's position is so
good, that virtually everything works for him.} (9... Ne4 {This thematic move
is once again strong}) 10. Bxf6 gxf6 11. Na4 e5 $2 (11... axb4 12. axb4 Bd7 {
Was winning quickly} 13. Nb2 Nc6) 12. b5 {Now White manages to advance his
pawns and is probably not even worse} Be6 13. g3 $2 {Probably it was wiser not
to allow c6} (13. e3 {undermining the centre was decent alternative} dxe3 (
13... c6 14. exd4) 14. Qc2 exf2+ 15. Kxf2 Nd7 16. Bd3 {[pgndiagram] And Black
is only slightly better}) 13... c6 14. bxc6 (14. b6 {Would lose the c5 pawn}
Na6 15. Qc2 Qd5 {And White is lost} 16. Rc1 e4) 14... Nxc6 {[pgndiagram] But
now once again White's position is highly unpleasant} 15. Bg2 Rb8 {Threatening
Bb3 and not allowing White to castle} 16. Qc1 (16. Nd2 {Was comparatively
better} Qc7 17. O-O f5 {Although even here Black retains a large advantage})
16... d3 {[pgndiagram] Now White gets murdered} 17. e3 e4 18. Nd2 f5 19. O-O
Re8 20. f3 Nd4 $1 {A nice touch.} 21. exd4 Qxd4+ 22. Kh1 e3 $1 {A cold shower}
(22... Qxa4 {Possibly Zukertort was hoping for immediate piece recapture} 23.
fxe4 fxe4 24. Nxe4 Qd4) 23. Nc3 Bf6 $1 {Another strong intermediate move} 24.
Ndb1 d2 {[pgndiagram] The pawn glide forward.} 25. Qc2 Bb3 26. Qxf5 d1=Q 27.
Nxd1 Bxd1 28. Nc3 e2 29. Raxd1 Qxc3 {[pgndiagram] A very uneven, irrational
encounter in which Zukertort lost virtually in the opening.} 0-1

[Event "Steinitz - Zukertort World Championship"]
[Site "New Orleans, LA USA"]
[Date "1886.03.29"]
[Round "20"]
[White "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Black "Johannes Zukertort"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C25"]
[PlyCount "37"]
[EventDate "1886.??.??"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. f4 exf4 4. d4 $1 {Steinitz has developed this audacious
gambit in his youth. It is interesting to see playing it in the most important
moment of his career - the World Championship match.} d5 {Playing d5 before
going for the check is worse, since White can take on f4 now as well} (4...
Qh4+ 5. Ke2 {[pgndiagram] As Kasparov writes, "Naturally, this gambit is
nonsense in the modern day chess, but back in the day, opponents had trouble
refuting it." The present game is the best example}) 5. exd5 {Allowing the
check, naturally} (5. Bxf4 Bb4 6. exd5 Qxd5 7. Nf3 {Is more normal}) 5... Qh4+
6. Ke2 Qe7+ 7. Kf2 Qh4+ 8. g3 fxg3+ 9. Kg2 {[pgndiagram] Actually, this way of
refuting the gambit is probably not the most energetic and Black has to look
for other possibilities instead of 4 (5).. d5} Nxd4 (9... Bd6 {Might be more
interesting} 10. dxc6 gxh2 11. Qf3 hxg1=Q+ 12. Kxg1 Qxd4+ 13. Be3 Qf6 {With c
omplicated position}) 10. hxg3 Qg4 11. Qe1+ $2 {[pgndiagram] This is a bad move
} (11. Bf4 {Would actually alow White to fight for the advantage} Bc5 (11...
Qxd1 12. Rxd1 Nxc2 13. Bd3 {Gives him massive lead in development and serious
compensation for the missing pawns}) 12. Nf3 Nxf3 13. Qxf3 Qxf3+ 14. Kxf3 Nf6
15. Re1+ Kd8 16. Bd3 {[pgndiagram] And White has sufficient compensation for
the pawn, but not more than that}) 11... Be7 12. Bd3 {It is important to
defend the c2 pawn} (12. Rh4 Nxc2) 12... Nf5 $2 {And very quickly there comes
a serious mistake. Rh4 was threatened and the move defends against that, but
it disrupts the coordination of the Black's pieces} (12... Kf8 13. Bf4 h5 {
Playing for the fast h4 was the right way to go}) (12... Nf6 13. Rh4) 13. Nf3
Bd7 (13... Kf8 {Was slightly more resillient} 14. Bf4 f6 15. Ne4 Ngh6 {And
White has to find the way to win}) 14. Bf4 {[pgndiagram] Already it is hard to
find a move for Black. Ne5 is a terrible threat} f6 (14... Nf6 15. Ne5) 15. Ne4
{Now Nf2 is on the table as well} Ngh6 $2 {A blunder in a lost position} (15...
O-O-O 16. Qa5) (15... Kf8 16. Nf2 Qg6 17. g4 {[pgndiagram] And White will win
material} Re8 18. Bxf5 Bxf5 19. Nh4) 16. Bxh6 Nxh6 17. Rxh6 $1 {Winning the
piece or the queen} gxh6 18. Nxf6+ Kf8 19. Nxg4 {[pgndiagram] An impressive
victory which allowed Steinitz to capture the crown.} 1-0

[Event "Carlsen - Karjakin World Chess Champion"]
[Site "New York NY USA"]
[Date "2016.11.11"]
[Round "1"]
[White "Magnus Carlsen"]
[Black "Sergey Karjakin"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "D00"]
[PlyCount "84"]
[EventDate "2016.11.11"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. Bg5 {There you have it. There are some players rated around 2300
who play the Trompovsky on a regular basis (and quite successfully) in Croatia)
} d5 {One of the main moves. Cox recommends 2...e6, but Karjakin has his own
opinion} 3. e3 {Prior to taking on f6 White waits a bit to see what Black will
do.} c5 4. Bxf6 {The logical idea in this opening... White gives up the bishop
to spoil the pawn structure.} gxf6 {Toward the centre is more logical, not to
isolate the d pawn} 5. dxc5 {This has all been played before.} Nc6 {This was
Giri's move in Caruana - Giri, London Classic, 2015} (5... e6 {Was Kramnik's
choice, but he quickly went astray} 6. Nf3 {And now instead of taking the pawn
with the bishop he played} Nd7 {planning to take with the knight, but there
followed} 7. c4 dxc4 8. c6 {With advantage to White, Carlsen - Kramnik, Tal
Memorial, 2013}) 6. Bb5 $5 {[pgndiagram]A new move, Caruana went c3 but didn't
achieve much} (6. Nc3 e6 7. Qh5 {Is the main line, that is scoring pretty well
for White to be honest (65% in my database)}) 6... e6 {Simple chess} (6... a6 {
Is also possible, but this way Black doesn't regain the pawn immediatelly} 7.
Bxc6+ bxc6 8. Nd2 e5 9. b4 a5 10. c3) (6... Qa5+ 7. Nc3 a6 {Would have
regained the pawn, but lost some time and White would enjoy that}) (6... Rg8 {
Is mentioned by GM Hess in his analysis, but it is better to reserve the right
to castle} 7. Nc3 e6 8. g3 {And with Qh5 coming it will be enjoyable for White}
) 7. c4 (7. b4 a5 8. c3 Bd7 9. Nf3 axb4 10. Bxc6 Bxc6 11. cxb4 b6) 7... dxc4 8.
Nd2 {Keeping the queens gives more chances.} Bxc5 9. Ngf3 O-O 10. O-O $1 {
Logical and natural} (10. Bxc6 bxc6 11. O-O Ba6 {Does compromise Black's
structure, but gives no chances of advantage, the bishops are very strong})
10... Na5 11. Rc1 {[pgndiagram] Up to here Magnus was playing instantly,
signalling that he was still in his preparation} (11. Qc2 {Was a possibility,
to capture on c4 as fast as possible without allowing queen exchange} Be7 12.
Nxc4 Qc7 {However also doesn't bring White alot}) 11... Be7 12. Qc2 (12. Nxc4 {
Taking on c4 doesn't bring much, but Black has to be somewhat careful} Qxd1 (
12... a6 $1 {[pgndiagram] Is very accurate computer suggestion} 13. Qxd8 Bxd8
14. Nd6 Be7 15. Nxc8 Raxc8) 13. Rfxd1 Nxc4 14. Bxc4 {With slightly more
pleasant position for White due to development lead}) 12... Bd7 $1 {Now Black
has more or less equalized} 13. Bxd7 Qxd7 14. Qc3 Qd5 (14... b6 {Was
interesting} 15. Nxc4 Rac8 16. Qd2 Qxd2 17. Ncxd2 {And equality is even more
clear}) 15. Nxc4 Nxc4 16. Qxc4 (16. Rfd1 {Is both GM Hess and computer
suggestion. White seizes the d file while the c4 knight isn't going anywhere.
However, it is doubtful whether that is advantageous to him} Qb5 17. Qxc4 Qxc4
18. Rxc4 Rac8 19. Rxc8 Rxc8 {And he doesn't have time to go Rd7 because of
bank rank problems}) 16... Qxc4 17. Rxc4 {[pgndiagram]Here it is time to take
a stop. Most of us would be faster to leave the board at this state than
Golden State Warriors fans are when their team is blowing the 3-1 lead in the
final. However I think it is very instructive how Karjakin eliminates even
these slight chances that Magnus had to create some problems. But why should
Magnus have chances at the first place. Well, White's pawn structure is better,
and he is first to seize the open file. However, amount of Black's weaknesses
and his good bishop easily deal with that.} Rfc8 {Exchanging a pair of rooks}
18. Rfc1 Rxc4 19. Rxc4 Rd8 20. g3 (20. g4 {Would put a pawn on a light square
and restrict the bishop somewhat, but would not change the evaluation}) 20...
Rd7 {Not allowing Rc7} 21. Kf1 f5 22. Ke2 Bf6 23. b3 Kf8 24. h3 h6 {The knight
wasn't going anywhere from f3, so it is time to reroute it} 25. Ne1 Ke7 26. Nd3
{When playing such endgames it is very hard, at least for me, to decide where
to advance the pawns, which pawns to advance, etc etc} Kd8 27. f4 {[pgndiagram]
This is the instance I don't understand completely. Now h pawn can never be
attacked by the rook, g pawn is somewhat weakened and stuff. Carlsen admitted
that he planned on going Ne5 and then winning some superior endgame, but as he
said, "it was a mirage"} h5 28. a4 {In any case White didn't miss big chances}
Rd5 {Keeping the rooks. Sometimes it is easier to steer for clarity, but often
exhcanges leave the pushing side satisfied.} (28... Rc7 {Would have been
something that White was aiming for} 29. Rxc7 Kxc7 30. Ne5 Bxe5 31. fxe5 Kc6
32. Kf3 {[pgndiagram] And i think that this pawn endgame is won for White}) 29.
Nc5 b6 30. Na6 Be7 31. Nb8 (31. b4 b5 32. axb5 Rxb5 {Is equal}) 31... a5 {
White was threatening Nc6 - Nxa7} 32. Nc6+ Ke8 33. Ne5 Bc5 34. Rc3 {Now there
really isn't very much going on, and players will be quick to agree a draw.}
Ke7 35. Rd3 Rxd3 36. Kxd3 f6 37. Nc6+ Kd6 38. Nd4 Kd5 39. Nb5 Kc6 40. Nd4+ Kd6
41. Nb5+ Kd7 42. Nd4 Kd6 {Very correctly played game and probing game from the
Champion. By going with the Trompovsky he basically avoided all Karjakin's
preparation and, as on numerous instances before, tried simply to play chess.
However, this time, it didn't quite work, but i doubt that Magnus will be
deeply depressed after this game, as he didn't miss serious chances, and he
has another game very soon anyway.} 1/2-1/2

[Event "Game two"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "2016.11.12"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Karjakin, Sergey"]
[Black "Magnus, Carlsen"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "C77"]
[PlyCount "82"]
[EventDate "2016.??.??"]

1. e4 {A somewhat expected start to the match by Karjakin.} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3.
Bb5 {So, we will have the Ruy Lopez after all. I think more people (myself
included) expected the Italian game more.} a6 {But this is the real surprise.
Black avoids the Berlin endgame and goes for the "old mainline" of the Ruy.} (
3... Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Nd6 6. Bxc6 dxc6 7. dxe5 Nf5 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 {Is the
modern tabiya of the so called Berlin endgame}) 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 (5...
Nxe4 {Would lead to the so called Open variation of the Ruy Lopez} 6. d4 b5 {
Note that now Nd6 is impossible, since it doesn't gain tempo on the bishop, so
there is no endgame. The result of the move 3..a6} 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5 {With
loads of theory}) 6. d3 {This solid approach has overtaken the historical main
lines recently.} (6. Re1 {Is the move known already in the 19th century.} b5 7.
Bb3 O-O {And in this position White can go} 8. d3 {In order to avoid the
Marshall attack} (8. c3 d5 {[pgndiagram] Is the theoretical and heavily
analized Marshall attack, played first in the game Capablanca - Marshall, New
York, 1918.})) 6... b5 7. Bb3 d6 {[pgndiagram]In this position White is at the
crossroads. His plan in this position is to play slowly and first develop his
pieces, and then eventually go for d4 under more favourable circumstances. I
think that this approach of controlable agression is dominant in the modern
treatment of the "Queen of openings".} 8. a3 {Karjakin chooses the more modest
pawn move. The idea of this move is to preserve the bishop from being
exchanged by Na5} (8. a4 b4 {And now black has ideas like Na5-Rb8-b3, for
instance. This leads simply to another type of position, and White choice is
most probably matter of taste and preference}) 8... O-O 9. Nc3 {Typical idea
is to jump with the knight on d5 and follow it up with c3-d4} Na5 10. Ba2 Be6
$5 {A modern move} (10... c5 11. b4 $1 {Typical Ruy Move} Nc6 12. Nd5 {Results
in a nice position for White}) 11. d4 $1 (11. Bxe6 fxe6 {And now d5 is
unavailable for White knight, and also f-file is opened} 12. d4 exd4 13. Nxd4
Qc8 {And with c5 coming and Nc4 black might as well consider taking the
initiative} 14. Be3 c5 15. Nde2 Nc4 {Okay, Be3 was stupid, but just for
illustration how quickly White's position can go wrong}) 11... Bxa2 12. Rxa2 {
So far the players have been following the game Topalov - Carlsen, GCT Rapid
Paris 2016., but Carlsen is again first to deviate} Re8 {Magnus is prepared.
This move is the first choice of the engines} (12... Nc6 13. d5 {And White was
better, although he eventually lost, Topalov - Carlsen, GCT Raprid Paris 2016.}
) 13. Ra1 {[pgndiagram]The opening phase is over. White has managed to occupy
the centre, but it doesn't bring him much joy. Black's pieces are fully
developed and he has simply a pleasant Ruy Lopez position. Maybe white
shouldn't have hurried with taking the rook back, but played b3 first, to
leave the a5 knight stranded, and maybe keep the possibility of doubling on
the a file after a4 somewhere in the future (however far-fetched this
generalisation is)} Nc4 14. Re1 Rc8 {I am not certain about the purpose of
this move, because Black goes c6 instead of c5 anyway} (14... Nb6 {Was maybe
more useful, in oder not to allow b3 to come with tempo} 15. b3 Nbd7 16. Bb2 c6
{With harmonious position}) 15. h3 (15. b3 Nb6 16. Bb2 Nbd7) 15... h6 {Both
sides are playing useful moves and improving their position} 16. b3 Nb6 17. Bb2
Bf8 18. dxe5 {[pgndiagram]Finally fixing the structure, but i don't see
another active plan for White} (18. Qd2 Nbd7 19. Rad1 c6 {And Black is very
solid, and the position is equal.}) 18... dxe5 19. a4 (19. Qxd8 {The computer
prefers the immediate queen exchange, but it is hard to imagine that White has
anything tangible here}) 19... c6 {Just patient play.} 20. Qxd8 {I don't think
that White can benefit from delaying or avoiding this} (20. Qe2 Qc7) 20...
Rcxd8 21. axb5 axb5 22. Ne2 (22. Na2 {With the Nb4 ideas, and hitting e5
doesn't bring anything} Nfd7 23. Bc3 Ra8 24. Nb4 {And now} f6 {[pgndiagram]And
the pawn is taboo} 25. Nxc6 $4 Rac8) 22... Bb4 (22... Nxe4 {Would have also
most probably end in a draw} 23. Bxe5 Bc5 24. Bd4) 23. Bc3 {There is not much
to comment on really. The game quickly peters to a draw} Bxc3 24. Nxc3 Nbd7 25.
Ra6 Rc8 26. b4 Re6 (26... c5 {Immediately was easier} 27. Nxb5 cxb4 28. Nd6 Re6
) 27. Rb1 c5 28. Rxe6 fxe6 29. Nxb5 cxb4 30. Rxb4 Rxc2 31. Nd6 Rc1+ {And here
the draw was agreed. Maybe Black could have tried the following line.} 32. Kh2
Rc2 33. Kg1 {And here the draw was agreed. Maybe Black could have tried the
following line.} Rc6 34. Nc4 Nxe4 35. Nfxe5 Nxe5 36. Nxe5 Rc1+ 37. Kh2 Nxf2 38.
Rb8+ Kh7 39. Rb6 Rh1+ 40. Kg3 Ne4+ 41. Kf4 Ng5 {With an extra pawn, albeit
hardly a convertible one.} 1/2-1/2

[Event "Game three"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "2016.11.14"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Magnus, Carlsen"]
[Black "Sergey, Karjakin"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "C67"]
[PlyCount "156"]
[EventDate "2016.??.??"]

1. e4 {The champion "changes hand" after playing the Trompovsky in the first
game. Will he head for Italian game, or face the possibility of another Berlin
endgame.} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 {Magnus played 3...a6 as Black here in game
three.} Nf6 {But Sergey isn't "risking it", and goes for the "currently
unbreachable" Berlin defence, which probably caused multiple spectators to
immediatelly switch to something more interesting. And since Mourinho's team
isn't playing any games today, probability of finding something more
interesting is considerably high.} 4. O-O {A first surprise, i think Magnus
prefered to avoid the endgame with d3.} (4. d3) 4... Nxe4 5. Re1 {And a second
surprise. Instead of exchanging queens and going the endgame, Magnus preferes
to play the symmetrical position resulting from the rook move.} (5. d4 Nd6 6.
Bxc6 dxc6 7. dxe5 Nf5 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 {Is a position I could probably write a
couple of books about. If I knew anything about it though. I promised myself I
will never play this with either colour. Like Svidler. I guess it means I will
never get chances to play in the World championship match. Like Svidler. :P})
5... Nd6 6. Nxe5 Be7 7. Bf1 Nxe5 8. Rxe5 O-O 9. d4 {[pgndiagram]The tabiya of
the variation. There are three reasonable moves for Black here.} (9. Nc3 {Is
apparently somewhat more drawish} Ne8 (9... Bf6 10. Re2 {Is also possible} b6
11. Re1 Re8 12. Rxe8+ Qxe8 13. d4 {And this is very similar to the game}) 10.
Nd5 Bd6 11. Re2 {This was the first instance of move Re2 appearing on the
board. In the New in Chess Magazine Maxime Vachier Lagrave (further referred
to as MVL) claimed that he discovered this move on a pocket set on a train.
Apparently there is something on trains that inspires creativity in people 
(Yes, another Harry Potter reference).}) 9... Bf6 {The most popular move.
Black's idea is to play Re8 next move and try to go with the knight on f5.} (
9... Ne8 {Is now allowing c4.} 10. c4 {From this position White scores 66%
according to my database.} Bf6 11. Re1 d5 12. cxd5 Qxd5 13. Be3 {And now the
position is no longer simetric.}) (9... b6 {Is interesting, but need more
exploration}) 10. Re2 $5 {The credit for this paradoxical maneouvre goes to
MVL (see above). At first point it looks completely ridiculous. But what is
the point behind it? White anticipates that most action will happen on the
e-file in the future. This move retains the possibility of playing Ra1-Re1 in
the very far future, after developing his queenside completely. Also, in the
case of Re8 White has Bf4 without worrying about d4 pawn. In the 8th game of
the 2013 Chennai match Carlsen played 10 Re1.} b6 {The best reaction to Re2,
in my opinion. Threatening Ba6 forces the rook to move again, since White
doesn't want to exchange light squared bishops immediately.} (10... Nf5 {To
illustrate the idea, consider the following moves} 11. c3 d5 12. Bf4 Be6 13.
Nd2 c6 14. Nf3 Qd7 15. Qd2 Nd6 16. Rae1 {Of course, play of both sides was
sub-optimal (White can probably first grab space on the kingside), but Re2!?
is very interesting idea in the position.}) 11. Re1 Re8 (11... Bb7 $5 {Maybe
was more precise}) 12. Bf4 $1 {[pgndiagram]Excellent move. Because of the move
b6 White can afford to play this developing move without worrying about d4.
That is some very deep 21st century opening subtlety.} Rxe1 13. Qxe1 Qe7 (13...
Bxd4 $4 {Would of course be very hard to expect in the World Championship
match.} 14. Bxd6 {And White wins.} cxd6 (14... Bxb2 {Also doesn't help} 15.
Bxc7 Qf8 16. Nd2 Bxa1 17. Qxa1 {With a won endgame for White}) 15. Qe4 Bxb2 16.
Qxa8) 14. Nc3 Bb7 15. Qxe7 Bxe7 {And for the third time in the match we have a
queenless endgame practically directly out of opening. White has a slight
advantage because his pieces are slightly better coordinated and he has more
space. However, Black doesn't have any weaknesses.} 16. a4 {Gaining more space
and threatening a5} a6 (16... h6 {To demonstrate that a5 is not something
Balck should allow, let's make a random move} 17. a5 {And suddenly it is not
easy for Black to make a move} Ne8 18. Nb5 {[pgndiagram]With unpleasant
pressure} Bd8 19. a6 Bc6 20. Re1 d5 21. Nc3) 17. g3 {I am not certain that
light squared bishop exchange is something White should aim for} (17. Bd3 g6
18. Re1 Bf6 19. Be5 {But probably this is also not much for White} Re8 20. Re3)
17... g5 {Now Black clearly equalizes} 18. Bxd6 Bxd6 19. Bg2 Bxg2 20. Kxg2 f5 {
I've commented the position somewhere around this moment with some Croatian
chessplayers and almost everybody was expecting a very quick draw and
expressing extreme boredom with the match course. I have to admit I even
started writing another lamenting post about how little interesting happens in
the match. Oh how i was wrong} 21. Nd5 Kf7 22. Ne3 Kf6 23. Nc4 Bf8 {
[pgndiagram]The last couple of moves haven't changed the evaluation of
position. The situation is similar a in the first game, where Carlsen's knight
is slightly superior to it's bishop's counterpart, but things should peter to
a draw quickly.} 24. Re1 Rd8 (24... d5 {Karjakin said after the game that he
should have played this immediatelly. And indeed, this makes Black's task
somewhat easier. Karjakin said he didn't consider 25 f4 properly} 25. Ne5 Bd6 {
And now} 26. f4 gxf4 27. gxf4 Rg8+ 28. Kf2 {[pgndiagram] And with the control
of the g-file, Black is equal}) 25. f4 $5 gxf4 26. gxf4 b5 {Good move, kicking
the knight and opening the a-file} (26... d5 27. Ne5 Bd6 28. Re3 {[pgndiagram]
Now White manages to swing his rook and stand slightly better, although here
also Black should probably equalize.}) 27. axb5 axb5 28. Ne3 c6 29. Kf3 Ra8 30.
Rg1 Ra2 $6 {First step in the wrong direction. Instead of being "active, Black
should have prefered Bh6.} (30... Bh6 {Preventing Rg5 and Rg8 and things
quickly peter to a draw.} 31. Rg3 Ra4 32. Rh3 Kg6 33. Rg3+ Kf6) 31. b3 c5 {
[pgndiagram]Mistakes come in pairs. This display of activity might have
backfired} 32. Rg8 $5 {More precise then Rg5} (32. Rg5 cxd4 33. Rxf5+ Kg7 34.
Ng4 b4 35. Rd5 d6 36. Rxd4 Rxc2 37. Rxb4 {[pgndiagram] And here White has less
chances.}) 32... Kf7 (32... Be7 33. d5 d6 34. Rh8 Kg6 35. Rb8 b4 {[pgndiagram]
Is unpleasant because black's bishop is severely restricted. However that
might have been better than to give a pawn}) 33. Rg2 cxd4 (33... Kf6 34. dxc5
Bxc5) 34. Nxf5 {Now the d4 is falling} d3 (34... Bc5 35. Ke4) 35. cxd3 Ra1 36.
Nd4 b4 {[pgndiagram] For a beginner it would seem that everything is clear.
Good knight and a pawn up. However, White's pawns are really weak, and Black
has some counterplay.} 37. Rg5 {The rook is most active along the 5th file} Rb1
38. Rf5+ Ke8 39. Rb5 {Karjakin was in time trouble prior to time control. Rh1
was best here, to win a pawn back, or tie White's rook. However, that would
have allowed White's pieces to activate} Rf1+ $2 {It is very easy to criticize
behind the keyboard, but this reminds me of a
"patzer-sees-check-patzer-gives-check", since this only improves white king.} (
39... Rh1 40. Ke4 Rxh2 41. Rb8+ Kf7 42. Rb7 Ke8 43. Nf3 {[pgndiagram] And
White has some chances}) 40. Ke4 Re1+ 41. Kf5 {After time control White is
seriously better now} Rd1 42. Re5+ $6 (42. Rb8+ $1 {And wherever the king goes,
it allows the knight redeployment.} Ke7 (42... Kf7 43. Nf3 {[pgndiagram] And
the pawn is taboo}) 43. Ke4 Re1+ 44. Kd5 Rf1 45. Nf5+ Kf7 46. Ke4 Re1+ 47. Ne3
{[pgndiagram]}) 42... Kf7 43. Rd5 Rxd3 (43... Ke7 $5 {Just keep the d pawns})
44. Rxd7+ Ke8 45. Rd5 Rh3 {[pgndiagram]From here up to the critical moment I
will give only a few brief comments on a couple of moves since now a
protracted maneuvering phase follows.} 46. Re5+ Kf7 47. Re2 Bg7 48. Nc6 Rh5+ (
48... Rxb3 $4 {[pgndiagram] The pawn is taboo and Black has to be careful a bit
} 49. Nd8+ Kg8 50. Re8+ Bf8 51. Ne6 {Is winning for White.}) 49. Kg4 Rc5 50.
Nd8+ (50. Nxb4 {Doesn't help} Rb5 51. Re4 h5+ 52. Kf3 Bf8 {And Black
eliminates the b-pawn, leaving a clear equality on the boad} 53. Nd3 Rxb3 {
[pgndiagram]}) 50... Kg6 51. Ne6 h5+ 52. Kf3 Rc3+ 53. Ke4 Bf6 54. Re3 {Up to
here Karjakin has slightly managed to reduce White's winning potential.} h4 {
[pgndiagram]Not neccessary.} (54... Rc2 55. h3 Kf7) 55. h3 (55. Nf8+ Kf7 56.
Nd7 {With Ne5 would improve the knight position a bit}) 55... Rc1 56. Nf8+ (56.
f5+ Kf7) 56... Kf7 57. Nd7 Ke6 58. Nb6 (58. Ne5 Bxe5 59. fxe5 {Is drawn}) 58...
Rd1 59. f5+ Kf7 60. Nc4 Rd4+ 61. Kf3 Bg5 $6 {This is probably the first bad
step(although far from obvious) which proves how difficult this "simple"
positions are.} (61... Kg7 {[pgndiagram] And it is hard for White to make some
progress.}) 62. Re4 Rd3+ 63. Kg4 {This tempo on the bishop is very important
and result of the 61th move.} Rg3+ 64. Kh5 Be7 $6 {[pgndiagram]The second bad
move, after which it is probably impossible to save the game any longer} (64...
Bd8 {Is something noone mentioned, and computer also ignored it at first. I
simply played it because I wondered what happens if i remove the bishop away
from attack. The long computer variation ends in a rook and knight vs rook
drawn position} 65. Nd6+ (65. Ne5+ Kf6 {And Kxf5 doesn't leave the bishop
hanging}) 65... Kg7 66. Re8 Bf6 67. Ne4 Rxh3 68. Rb8 Bc3 69. Rb7+ Kf8 70. Ng5
Rd3 71. f6 Bxf6 72. Nh7+ Ke8 73. Nxf6+ Kd8 74. Rxb4 h3 {[pgndiagram]} 75. Kg5
h2 76. Rh4) 65. Ne5+ Kf6 66. Ng4+ Kf7 67. Re6 $1 (67. f6 Bxf6 68. Rf4 Rxh3 69.
Rxf6+ Ke7 {[pgndiagram] Is drawn}) 67... Rxh3 68. Ne5+ Kg7 69. Rxe7+ Kf6 70.
Nc6 $2 {The first critical moment where Carlsen misses the win. The winning
idea consists of defending the b pawn with the knight from a secure position
while controlling the h pawn.} (70. Re8 $1 {[pgndiagram] Is far from obvious}
Kxf5 (70... Rxb3 71. Kg4 Rg3+ 72. Kf4 b3 {And here computer announces mate in
8, so we should probably believe him.}) 71. Nc6 Rh1 (71... Rc3 72. Nd4+ Kf6 73.
Kxh4 {[pgndiagram] And the knight is secure}) 72. Nd4+ Kf4 73. Re2 h3 74. Kh4
h2 75. Ne6+ Kf3 76. Rb2 Ke4 77. Nc5+ Kd5 78. Na4 Rd1 79. Rxh2 Rd4+ 80. Kg5 Rd3
81. Rb2 {[pgndiagram] And this, approximately 11 move variation, finished in
one of the possible winning constructions for White}) 70... Kxf5 $2 {Returning
the favour.} (70... Rc3 {Was relatively simple draw.} 71. Re6+ Kxf5 72. Nd4+
Kf4 73. Kxh4 Rd3) 71. Na5 Rh1 72. Rb7 $2 {[pgndiagram] The final mistake,
after which there is no going back.} (72. Rf7+ {After 7 hours it is not easy
to see the study-like winning idea. Carlsen said at the press-conference
afterwards that he didn't see a clear win.} Ke6 {The move that allows the
afore mentioned study-like idea, however all other moves lose as well.} 73. Rf2
h3 74. Kh4 Ra1 75. Nb7 Ra3 76. Rh2 {[pgndiagram]And the b pawn is indirectly
defended by knight.}) 72... Ra1 73. Rb5+ Kf4 74. Rxb4+ (74. Kxh4 $4 {It is
never too late to lose a chess game} Rh1#) 74... Kg3 75. Rg4+ Kf2 76. Nc4 h3
77. Rh4 Kg3 78. Rg4+ Kf2 {And here the draw was agreed. A fascinating struggle
at the highest level.} 1/2-1/2

[Event "Game Five"]
[Site "0:25:33-0:13:33"]
[Date "2016.11.17"]
[Round "5"]
[White "Magnus Carlsen"]
[Black "Sergey Karjakin"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "C54"]
[PlyCount "102"]
[EventDate "2016.11.11"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 {It was expected that this move will appear in the
course of the match. The Italian game was revived recently as a way for e4
player to avoid the Berlin defence.} Bc5 4. O-O {To be honest, I am not
familiar with all the subtleties and move orders in this opening. I think they
avoid early c3 because it allows Black to go d5 fast} (4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 O-O 6.
O-O d5 7. exd5 Nxd5 {[pgndiagram]Although here White has good results recently}
) 4... Nf6 5. d3 O-O 6. a4 d6 7. c3 a6 {The tabiya of the variation. Black
takes a moment to place his bishop to a7. Some time ago I tried analyzing the
Italian game for White, but I was unable to find a clear way to advantage.} 8.
b4 {The computer's first suggestion and one of the moves i looked at during
the aforementioned analysis.} (8. Bg5 {[pgndiagram] I think I have read
something that this pin is not so harmless as it was initially though.} h6 9.
Bh4 g5 10. Bg3 {There were many games where White sacrificed on g5} (10. Nxg5 {
[pgndiagram] And the play is extremely interesting. The computer even prefers
White} hxg5 11. Bxg5 Kg7 12. Nd2 {Whiet plays for the long term compensation.
This position should be examined more deeply before venturing over the board.})
10... g4 11. Nh4 Nh5 {[pgndiagram] Is some sort of modern tabiya which looks a
bit risky for Black, but has been played}) 8... Ba7 9. Re1 {[pgndiagram] I
think that Black meets any move by White with the same typical reaction. Ne7 -
Ng6 - c6} (9. Na3 Ne7 10. Nc2 c6 11. Be3 Ng6 12. Bxa7 Rxa7 13. Bb3 Bg4 14. h3
Bxf3 15. Qxf3 a5 {[pgndiagram] And White is only slightly better. Of course
this variation is only approximate and play of both sides can be improved.})
9... Ne7 $5 {Typical maneovre in the Italian game. Knight heads to g6 where he
defends e5 and possibly jumps to f4} 10. Nbd2 Ng6 11. d4 $5 {[pgndiagram] I
have never considered this move, but it is quite logical. Black has removed
the knight which has influenced the centre, so White uses the opportunity to
advance.} c6 {This is a very cunning move. Black is making a useful move,
while making White to play something sub-optimal.} (11... exd4 {[pgndiagram]
Commentary of the move c6 may become more clear if we consider the position
with immediate exchange} 12. cxd4 Nxe4 13. Nxe4 d5 14. Bd3 dxe4 15. Bxe4 {And
now Black is forced to play c6 anyway} c6 16. b5 {[pgndiagram] The point!
White doesn't play h3 here, but goes for b5, with advantage.}) 12. h3 {
Preventing Bg4} (12. b5 {Can now be strongly met by} d5 13. Bd3 exd4 14. cxd4
Bg4 {[pgndiagram]And Black has equalised} 15. Bb2 Nf4 16. Bf1 dxe4 17. Nxe4
axb5) 12... exd4 (12... d5 {Wasn't that good now} 13. dxe5 $1) (12... Re8 {
[pgndiagram] I am not sure what is White's plan if Black just plays quietly}
13. Bf1 d5 14. dxe5 Nxe5 15. exd5 {[pgndiagram] With equality. White's
advanced queenside pawns need care}) 13. cxd4 Nxe4 $6 {This trick is met
really often with the pawn on e4 and bishop on c4 and is worth remembering.
However, I highly doubt it brings Black clear equality in every case.} (13...
d5 14. exd5 cxd5 15. Bf1 Re8 {Seems to me more solid}) 14. Bxf7+ $5 {I quite
like when White players do this in the Bc4 variation of the Modern defence
that I often encounter as Black. However the move in the game lets White with
a better position as well. I think it is better for Black to avoid the Nxe4
altogether in this situation} (14. Nxe4 {[pgndiagram]} d5 15. Bd3 dxe4 16. Bxe4
Be6 17. b5 Bd5 18. bxc6 bxc6 19. Qc2 {[pgndiagram] And White doesn't have much
here either, but at least Black has some weaknesses and it is hard to imagine
that White will suffer as Carlsen did}) 14... Rxf7 15. Nxe4 d5 $1 {[pgndiagram]
This is forced, since else White would play it} (15... h6 16. d5 $1) 16. Nc5 h6
{Preventing Ng5 is always a good idea} 17. Ra3 $1 {A very original way to
include the rook into the game} Bf5 18. Ne5 $6 {Faulty move. Carlsen probably
missed or underestimated Black's 20th move} (18. Rae3 {[pgndiagram] This would
have been a logical continuation of the plan} Qf6 19. Ne5 Rff8 20. Nxg6 Bxg6
21. Qd2 {And here White has only slight lead in development} Rae8 {[pgndiagram]
However, the evaluation is close to equality.}) 18... Nxe5 19. dxe5 {Carlsen
probably thought that this is the only way to play for the win} (19. Rxe5 $5 {
Was interesting again though} Qd6 (19... Bb8 {[pgndiagram] Is risky} 20. Nxb7
Qf6 (20... Qf8 21. Nc5 Bxe5 22. dxe5)) 20. Rg3 Raf8 21. Bf4 Be4 22. Be3 {
[pgndiagram] With somewhat the better chances}) 19... Qh4 $1 {[pgndiagram]A
good move, creating fresh threats on the kingside} 20. Rf3 (20. e6 $2 {Is met
by} Bxe6) 20... Bxc5 $6 {There was no need to hurry and give up the bishop pair
} (20... Bg6 21. Rxf7 Bxf7 {[pgndiagram]}) 21. bxc5 {Now White is again in
control. The structure is similar to game four and b8 is again weak. However,
the position is close to equal due to lack of tangible weakness and opposite
coloured bishops.} Re8 {[pgndiagram]The maneuvering phase of the games ensues}
22. Rf4 Qe7 23. Qd4 Ref8 24. Rf3 Be4 25. Rxf7 Qxf7 26. f3 Bf5 27. Kh2 Be6 {
[pgndiagram] Black is very solid and it is hard for White to create something
serious. His plan should include pushing the kingside pawns, which might leave
his king weakened.} 28. Re2 Qg6 29. Be3 Rf7 30. Rf2 Qb1 31. Rb2 Qf5 32. a5 {
[pgndiagram]I am not sure whether this was neccessary since Black's king is
quite safe now on the queenside} (32. f4 Qh5 33. Rf2 {And trying to go g4 with
the black king on kingside might have been a better plan} Bf5) 32... Kf8 {
Black decides to act first and moves his king to the blocked queenside.} 33.
Qc3 Ke8 34. Rb4 g5 {[pgndiagram] preventing any action on the kingside by the
rook} (34... Kd8 {[pgndiagram]} 35. Rf4 Qg6 36. Rxf7 Qxf7 37. g4 {And the
dissapearence of rooks might be considered an achievement for White.}) 35. Rb2
Kd8 36. Rf2 Kc8 37. Qd4 {[pgndiagram] Objectively the game should end in a
draw. But Carlsen is persistent this time which soon lands him into trouble.}
Qg6 38. g4 $5 {A risky decision before time control. Opening the h file will
benefit Black.} h5 39. Qd2 Rg7 40. Kg3 Rg8 41. Kg2 {[pgndiagram] I am really
not sure about last two moves by White. Kg2 is a blunder, because it blocks
the rook's path to h file, This move was the result of the wrong notation of
the moves. Let me explian. The players receive bonus time after move 40.
Carlsen apparently though that only this is the move 40. Even World Champions
make mistakes as this in time trouble.} hxg4 42. hxg4 d4 $6 {Objectively not a
good move} (42... Qh6 $1 {Seizing the h file, was simple and good for Black}
43. Bd4 Rh8 44. Kf1 Qh1+ 45. Ke2 Qb1 {[pgndiagram]Is highly unpleasant for
White} 46. Qxg5 Rh1 {And I would always rather be Black, especially in
practical game.}) 43. Qxd4 Bd5 $6 {And this is another slip, because it allows
White to counter on h-file} (43... Rh8 {Was mentioned by Karjakin at the press
conference} 44. Qe4 Qh6 45. Kf1 Qh1+ 46. Ke2 Qa1 {[pgndiagram] Karjakin said
he forgot about this move. Again it is very hard to evaluate, and Black has to
play two pawns down. Imagine White king escapes?} 47. Bxg5 {However, I am not
certain Black has that much here. Objectively} Bd5 48. Qf4 $1 {A key move,
preparing Qc1 somewhere in the future.} Qb2+ 49. Ke1 Rh1+ 50. Rf1 Qa1+ 51. Qc1)
44. e6 $1 {Great resource, ruling out Rh8} Qxe6 45. Kg3 {[pgndiagram] White is
still slightly worse, but next Karjakin's move changes that} Qe7 {With this
the last train for pushingtown leaves.} (45... Kb8 46. Qc3 {This is the
computer sugestion. The idea is to put the bishop on e5} (46. Rh2 Ka7 {And
White's position is highly unpleasant. f3 will come under pressure}) 46... Ka7
47. Bd4 Rf8 48. Be5 {[pgndiagram] And Black has nothing. But it is really hard
to judge how would the game unfold}) 46. Rh2 {Now the game quickly peters into
a draw.} Qf7 47. f4 gxf4+ 48. Qxf4 Qe7 49. Rh5 Rf8 50. Rh7 Rxf4 51. Rxe7 Re4 {
The first game in which Karjakin managed to rise his head and obtain serious
chances. Again my match forecast ( I must be really boring by now) turns out
to be correct. Carlsen after missing serious chances in games three in four
tries to "win by force" and almost ends up being punished for it.} 1/2-1/2

[Event "Game Four"]
[Site "0:34:33-0:04:33"]
[Date "2016.11.15"]
[Round "4"]
[White "Sergey Karjakin"]
[Black "Magnus Carlsen"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "C88"]
[PlyCount "187"]
[EventDate "2016.11.11"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 {Here we go. Again no Berlin} 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O
Be7 6. Re1 {This time Karjakin goes for the "old" main line, without early d3}
b5 7. Bb3 O-O {An invitation to Marshall} 8. h3 {Nope sir, although this way
of avoiding Marshall doesn't rule the d5 break alltogether.} Bb7 9. d3 (9. c3
d5 $5 {Is a variation}) 9... d6 (9... d5 $5 {Is again possible here, although
with d3 instead of c3 it is favourable for White.}) 10. a3 {[pgndiagram] And
we have still reached something similar to the 2nd game. Black has many
reasonable options. Virtually every move is possible} Qd7 $5 {The 2nd most
popular move. Black connects his rooks and develops further} (10... Na5 11. Ba2
c5 {With typical Ruy play.}) (10... Nb8 {Along the Breyer lines is also
possible} 11. Nbd2) 11. Nbd2 $5 (11. Nc3 {[pgndiagram]My personal opinion is
that one of the advantages of not playing early c3 is the possibility to
transfer the knight via that square.} Rfe8 12. Nd5 Nxd5 13. Bxd5 Nd8 14. Bxb7
Nxb7 15. Be3 {[pgndiagram] Although with mass exchanges on d5 it is hard for
White to achieve something serious.}) 11... Rfe8 12. c3 {A rare continuation,
more often is the transfer of the knight.} (12. Nf1 Nd8 {Black can transfer
his knight as well.} 13. Ne3 Ne6 14. Nf5 Bf8 {[pgndiagram] With approximate
equality}) 12... Bf8 13. Nf1 (13. d4 $5 {[pgndiagram] would have been logical
continuation of the plan with c3} h6 14. Bc2 Rad8 15. Nb3 d5 {[pgndiagram] And
it seems to me that White's chances are existent} 16. Nxe5 Nxe5 17. dxe5 Rxe5
18. Bf4) 13... h6 14. N3h2 $6 {[pgndiagram] I don't like this move. The idea
is to go to g4, but Black is well prepared for this} (14. Ne3 {IS more logical
for me, preventing d5} Nd8 15. Nh2 {Only now} Ne6 16. Nhg4 {[pgndiagram]})
14... d5 $1 {Carlsen plays good chess and strikes in the centre immediately.}
15. Qf3 {The typical maneouvre in the Ruy, but Black is well prepared for this}
Na5 16. Ba2 dxe4 {[pgndiagram]This is good enough, giving White a e4 weakness,
that I will not even consider other escapades such as c5} 17. dxe4 Nc4 {I
think that it was here where Karjakin misscalculated.} 18. Bxh6 {This would be
a great move, if there wasn't a riposte by Black} Qc6 $1 {[pgndiagram]This is
what Karjakin missed. To my mind this is far from obvious, and i am not sure
how many players would find this.} (18... Nxe4 19. Rxe4 {Karjakin demonstrated
a nice variation here.} f5 $4 (19... Bxe4 20. Qxe4 gxh6 21. Ne3 {Leaves White
better}) 20. Rxc4 $1 {[pgndiagram] And White wins}) 19. Bxc4 $2 {And
immediately there comes a mistake. Karjakin was probably cursing himself for
not seeing 18 .. Qc6 and went into panic mode.} (19. Bc1 {[pgndiagram]} Nxe4
20. Ne3 {And nothing terrible for White is apparent} Ned6 {Svidler and Hansen
were analyzing similar stuff to this}) 19... bxc4 {[pgndiagram] Now all
endgames are bad for White. b2 is a chronic weakness} 20. Be3 Nxe4 21. Ng3 Nd6
(21... Qg6 {[pgndiagram] It was possible to retain the queens} 22. Nxe4 Bxe4
23. Qg4 Qc6 {But Carlsen's reply is good also}) 22. Rad1 Rab8 23. Bc1 f6 24.
Qxc6 Bxc6 {[pgndiagram] Once again we have a "queenless middlegame". Black's
chances are undoubtly better, his pieces are nice, he has a pair of strong
bishops, and b2 is weak. The plan for Black is to play on both wings, and use
pressure on queenside with push of the pawns in the centre and on the kingside.
} 25. Ng4 Rb5 {Another pleasing move. Black can go Bd5 or Rd5 in the future}
26. f3 (26. Ne3 g6 $1 {[pgndiagram]} 27. f3 f5 {Is strong for Black}) 26... f5
27. Nf2 Be7 28. f4 {[pgndiagram]Not wishing to be pushed of the board,
Karjakin tries active defence} (28. Nf1 Bh4 29. Ne3 g6 {Is highly unpleasant.
White can barely move.}) 28... Bh4 $1 29. fxe5 Bxg3 30. exd6 Rxe1+ 31. Rxe1
cxd6 {Exchanges don't easy White's position as Black is very active, and b2 is
still weak.} 32. Rd1 Kf7 {Now the king enters} 33. Rd4 {[pgndiagram]Attacking
c4, but Black has a tactical response It is always pleasant to see how top
level players manage to unite this little combinations with their strategical
goals.} Re5 {Threatening mate} (33... d5 {Would probably easen the grip a
little.}) 34. Kf1 Rd5 35. Rxd5 {Rook exchange is inevitable} (35. Rxc4 Bb5)
35... Bxd5 36. Bg5 Kg6 {Here comes the king.} 37. h4 Kh5 38. Nh3 Bf7 (38...
Bxh4 $2 {[pgndiagram] Would be to naive.} 39. Nf4+ Kg4 40. Bxh4 Kxf4 {Would
result in a draw.}) 39. Be7 Bxh4 {[pgndiagram] This was not strictly
neccessary, but it doesn't spoil anything.} (39... g6 {And again White can
hardly move}) 40. Bxd6 Bd8 41. Ke2 g5 {[pgndiagram] Now the kingside pawn-roll
comes} 42. Nf2 Kg6 $2 {As pointed by user "Eyal" on chessgames, this might be
Magnus' ultimate miss in the game} (42... Bd5 43. g3 g4 $1 {[pgndiagram] And
the knight is dominated} 44. Nd1 Bf3+ 45. Kd2 Bxd1 46. Kxd1 {[pgndiagram] And
now Black must be winning. Even Caruana tweeted about this variation}) 43. g4 {
I would always hesitate to push pawns in such a position, but Karjakin's
active defence manages to confuse Carlsen.} Bb6 44. Be5 (44. gxf5+ Kxf5 45. Nh1
{[pgndiagram] And with such a strong passer Black is probably winning (note
how black's two pawns restrict three white pawns on the queenside)}) 44... a5
45. Nd1 {[pgndiagram]Threatening Ne3, which would force f4, or taking on f4.
The critical moment of the game is reached.} f4 {This was condemned by almost
everyone. Magnus overlooked the possibility of the fortress, as he admitted
that he simply thought he has to breakthrough somehow. He will manage to get
his king on b3, but his bishops are unable to deal with the barricades on the
kingside by themselves.} (45... fxg4 {First, let's consider why taking the
pawn is bad} 46. Ne3 Kh5 47. Bg3 {And again there is a fortress on the board})
(45... Be6 {Most people claimed that bishop move should be winning. It is hard
to asses it however. This endgame is much more complex then it looks like. I
am not certain whether this wins. White has many options.} 46. Nf2 {
[pgndiagram] Most logical. Just waiting and asking Black what can he do} (46.
Ne3 f4 {Just wins the g4 and the game}) 46... Bd5 47. Bd4 Bc7 48. Be3 Be6 {
[pgndiagram]Now the threat of taking on g4 is real} 49. Kf3 {[pgndiagram] And
i don't see how Black can improve} (49. Bd4 fxg4)) 46. Bd4 {Now there is not
much to comment on. Position is drawn. It is always funny to look at the
computer's evaluation to see how it doesn't realize that it should be 0.0} Bc7
47. Nf2 Be6 48. Kf3 Bd5+ 49. Ke2 Bg2 50. Kd2 Kf7 51. Kc2 Bd5 52. Kd2 Bd8 53.
Kc2 Ke6 54. Kd2 Kd7 55. Kc2 Kc6 56. Kd2 Kb5 57. Kc1 Ka4 58. Kc2 Bf7 59. Kc1 Bg6
60. Kd2 Kb3 61. Kc1 Bd3 62. Nh3 Ka2 63. Bc5 Be2 64. Nf2 Bf3 65. Kc2 Bc6 66. Bd4
Bd7 67. Bc5 Bc7 68. Bd4 Be6 69. Bc5 f3 70. Be3 Bd7 71. Kc1 Bc8 72. Kc2 Bd7 73.
Kc1 Bf4 74. Bxf4 gxf4 75. Kc2 Be6 76. Kc1 Bc8 77. Kc2 Be6 78. Kc1 Kb3 79. Kb1
Ka4 80. Kc2 Kb5 81. Kd2 Kc6 82. Ke1 Kd5 83. Kf1 Ke5 84. Kg1 Kf6 85. Ne4+ Kg6
86. Kf2 Bxg4 87. Nd2 Be6 88. Kxf3 Kf5 89. a4 Bd5+ 90. Kf2 Kg4 91. Nf1 Kg5 92.
Nd2 Kf5 93. Ke2 Kg4 94. Kf2 {A fascinating game which proves how complex
endgame play can be. Fantastic.} 1/2-1/2

[Event "Game Six"]
[Site "0:54:33-1:23:33"]
[Date "2016.11.18"]
[Round "6"]
[White "Sergey Karjakin"]
[Black "Magnus Carlsen"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "C88"]
[PlyCount "64"]
[EventDate "2016.11.11"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 {For the third time in the match, The Champion
avoids the Berlin in favour of the Closed variation of the Ruy} 4. Ba4 Nf6 5.
O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. h3 {And once again Karjakin avoids the
invitation to Marshall. Although this move doesn't rule out the d5 advance, as
we will see.} Bb7 9. d3 d5 $5 {[pgndiagram]Here we go. In analysis to game
four i mentioned that this move is possible. In my database this was first
played in the game Quesada P. - Nielsen P.H., Capablanca Memorial Elite 2007.
It is indicative because Nielsen is Carlsen's second.} 10. exd5 Nxd5 11. Nxe5 (
11. Nbd2 {[%emt 0:00:11] [pgndiagram] When I saw 9..d5!? I remembered the
brilliant game Ivanchuk - So, Tata Steel 2015 which went  Nbd2} Qd7 12. Nxe5
Nxe5 13. Rxe5 Nf4 14. Nf3 Nxg2 $3 {[pgndiagram] And Black went on to win})
11... Nd4 $5 {This move is also very well known in this position} 12. Nc3 Nb4
$5 {[pgndiagram] The point. Black's play revolves around weak c2 square. You
will see that in many variations that square is the key.} 13. Bf4 {Natural
developing move} (13. a3 Nxb3 14. cxb3 f6 {Leads to equality}) (13. Ne4 {
[pgndiagram] Threatening c3 is computer's first choice. It was played only 4
times in 2500+ domain, with White scoring 1 win and 3 draws.} Nxb3 14. axb3 Qd5
15. Nf3 f5 {[pgndiagram]And apparently Black has adequate compensation for the
pawn} 16. Neg5 Bd6) 13... Nxb3 14. axb3 c5 $1 {[pgndiagram] The novelty of the
game. Black seizes space and control of d4} 15. Ne4 (15. Rc1 $5 {[pgndiagram]
Is very interesting computer suggestion, defending c2 square and freeing the
queen} Rc8 16. Qd2 Re8 {And White retains some advantage}) 15... f6 $1 {
[pgndiagram]} (15... f5 {Immediate push was also possible. However, that would
leave the knight on e5 untouched. Control of central squares was probably
something Carlsen was trying to avoid.}) 16. Nf3 f5 17. Neg5 (17. Ned2 {Was
slighly more passive} Re8 18. Be5 (18. Ne5 $2 Qd4) 18... Bf6 19. Rc1 {
[pgndiagram] And the position is equal}) 17... Bxg5 18. Nxg5 h6 {[pgndiagram]
Black can easily parry the fork threat} 19. Ne6 Qd5 $1 {Fantastic preparation
by Carlsen in my opinion} 20. f3 Rfe8 21. Re5 (21. Nc7 {[pgndiagram] Doesn't
work} Qd4+ 22. Kh1 Rxe1+ 23. Qxe1 Qxf4 24. Nxa8 Nxc2 {[pgndiagram] I have no
doubt that Carlsen would find all this.} 25. Qe8+ Kh7 {[pgndiagram] And now
White doesn't have time to move the rook} 26. Rf1 (26. Rd1 Bxf3 $3 {
[pgndiagram] What a sacrifice}) 26... Qd6 $1 {Attacking the d pawn} 27. Rd1 Qg3
{[pgndiagram] And believe it or not - Black is winning}) 21... Qd6 $1 {
[pgndiagram] Another computer move. Black places himself into pin} 22. c3 (22.
Re1 Qd7 {And White knight doesn't have a square} 23. c3 (23. Nxc5 Qd4+) (23.
Nc7 {[pgndiagram] Doesn't work} Rxe1+ 24. Qxe1 Nxc2) 23... Nd5 24. Nxc5 Qc6 {
[pgndiagram] And in complications Black is better. Two White pieces are hanging
}) (22. Re2 {Defending c2, is better!?} Qd7 23. Nc7 $1 {[pgndiagram] Now this
move is possible} Rxe2 24. Qxe2 Rc8 25. Re1 {And White remains in control. The
knight is taboo.} Rxc7 $2 26. Bxc7 Qxc7 27. Qe8+ Kh7 28. Re6 {[pgndiagram] And
White wins.}) 22... Rxe6 23. Rxe6 Qxe6 24. cxb4 cxb4 {[pgndiagram] The
opposite coloured bishops and the pawn structure now lead to a draw.} 25. Rc1
Rc8 26. Rxc8+ Qxc8 27. Qe1 Qd7 28. Kh2 a5 29. Qe3 Bd5 30. Qb6 Bxb3 31. Qxa5
Qxd3 32. Qxb4 Be6 {[pgndiagram] And the draw was agreed. Impressive piece of
opening preparation by the Champion.} 1/2-1/2

[Event "Game Seven"]
[Site "0:21:33-1:04:33"]
[Date "2016.11.20"]
[Round "7"]
[White "Sergey Karjakin"]
[Black "Magnus Carlsen"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "D27"]
[PlyCount "66"]
[EventDate "2016.11.11"]

1. d4 {In his 2nd White game in a row, Karjakin finally "changes hand" and
gives up the e4 move.} d5 2. c4 c6 {So, the champion has prepared a Slav for
this match.} 3. Nc3 Nf6 {In my database there are 14 games where Magnus has
had this position on the board. Last time he play the Slav was 4 years ago at
the Tata Steel Chess tournament 2012.} 4. e3 {This is the move he has
encountered most often. 9 times in 14 games} a6 $5 {[pgndiagram]But i guess
this must have come as a surprise to Karjakin. The last time Magnus played
this was against Ponomariov in Wijk aan Zee 2007. Almost 10 years ago.} 5. Bd3
{I think this is a somewhat rare move. With this move Black instantly
transposes in a position similar to Queen's gambit declined lines, where White
has played e3. I am not entirely certain, but I think that current state of
theory says that e3 variation of the queen's gambit is pretty harmless for
Black.} (5. Nf3 {Is much more popular, after which Black usually goes either 5.
..b5 or 5...Bf5.}) 5... dxc4 6. Bxc4 e6 {[pgndiagram] Black's plan is to play
c5-b5-Bb7 in the next couple of moves. I think that there is no way for White
to stop this and that Black will have no trouble equalizing.} (6... c5 $6 {
Immediate advance would mean a pawn sacrifice} 7. dxc5 Qxd1+ 8. Kxd1) 7. Nf3 c5
8. O-O (8. a4 {[pgndiagram] Is the only way to prevent Black's plan, but a4
move always leaves the b4 square weak.} Nc6 9. O-O cxd4 10. exd4 (10. Nxd4 Bd7)
10... Bb4 {And Black has equalised.}) 8... b5 9. Be2 (9. Bb3 {[pgndiagram]Is
more popular move. The idea is to vacate the e2 square for the queen.} Bb7 10.
Qe2 Nbd7 11. Rd1 {And the line continues.} Qb8 (11... Qc7 12. e4 cxd4 13. Nxd4)
12. e4 cxd4 13. Nxd4 Bd6) 9... Bb7 {[pgndiagram] I think that this position is
not dangerous for Black and that he has equalized.} 10. dxc5 {There is nothing
else useful for White.} (10. b3 {[pgndiagram] Trying to develop the bishop
would be met by the pressure on d4} Nc6 11. Bb2 cxd4 12. Nxd4 Nxd4 13. exd4 {
[pgndiagram] And he may only be worse.}) 10... Nc6 $5 {[pgndiagram] Again a
novelty by Carlsen. The champion seems to be excellently prepared.} (10... Qxd1
11. Rxd1 Bxc5 {[pgndiagram] This position should probably be OK for Black as
well, but in recent 2500+ games White has managed to score a couple of
victories.}) 11. Nd2 $6 {Similar maneovre occurs when the Queens are exchanges,
but here it is not so effective.} (11. Qxd8+ {Has a hidden point.} Rxd8 12. b4
$1 {[pgndiagram] It is not easy to find this at board, and moreover, since
even my Stockfish indicates it instantly, it must have been taken into account
by Carlsen in his preparation.} Nxb4 13. a4 Bxc5 14. axb5 axb5 15. Nxb5 O-O {
[pgndiagram] And the liquidation on the queenside leads to an equal endgame})
11... Bxc5 12. Nde4 Nxe4 13. Nxe4 Be7 {[pgndiagram] The opening has finished.
Black has equalized without problems. He has an advantage in development, a
little more space and no weaknesses.} 14. b3 Nb4 15. Bf3 O-O (15... f5 $5 {
[pgndiagram] Was an interesting move to try and fight for advantage} 16. Nd2
Bxf3 17. Nxf3 Qxd1 18. Rxd1 Bf6 19. Nd4 Rc8 {[pgndiagram] And here Black
retains some pressure.}) 16. Ba3 Rc8 $2 {[pgndiagram] But this is a bad move.
It is brave to criticize the World Champion, but this is uncharacteristic for
him. Instead of trying to utilize his slightly better position, he goes for a
position with opposite coloured bishops where he will be a pawn down.} (16...
Bd5 17. Ng3 Bxf3 18. Qxf3 Nd5 {[pgndiagram] Leads to equality}) (16... Rb8 17.
Qxd8 Rfxd8 18. Rfd1 {[pgndiagram] And here also draw is the most probably
result, but only Black can play for the win.}) 17. Nf6+ $1 {[pgndiagram]
Entering the tactical complications favourable for White} Bxf6 18. Bxb7 Bxa1
19. Bxb4 Bf6 (19... Qxd1 {[pgndiagram] After this Black wouldn't have to give
up the pawn. But he would still be somewhat tied up} 20. Rxd1 Rc7 21. Bxf8 Rxb7
22. Bb4 Bf6 23. Rc1 h5 24. Rc8+ Kh7 25. Rc6 Ra7 26. Kf1 {[pgndiagram] But even
in this approximate variation it is hard to imagine that he will lose.}) 20.
Bxf8 Qxd1 21. Rxd1 Rxf8 22. Bxa6 (22. b4 {[pgndiagram] Some comentators on
chessgames recommended this, but it is also doubtful that even here White has
anything.} g5 23. Bxa6 Rb8 24. Kf1 Rb6 25. Bc8 Kf8 {[pgndiagram] And i doubt
that White can win this}) 22... b4 {[pgndiagram] Entering the opposite
coloured bishop position with one pawn less. However, since black's b pawn is
controllin White's two queenside pawns, White is unable to create a passed
pawn.} 23. Rc1 {There is nothing to comment on from here. The draw was soon
agreed.} g6 24. Rc2 Ra8 25. Bd3 Rd8 {[pgndiagram] Black only has to avoid
playing Bc3 which would allow a4 in reply. While the pressure on a pawn exists,
there is nothing to worry about.} 26. Be2 Kf8 27. Kf1 Ra8 28. Bc4 (28. Rc4 {
[pgndiagram] Here Bc3 is possible} Bc3 29. a4 $4 {It is never to late to lose..
} bxa3 30. Rxc3 a2) 28... Rc8 29. Ke2 Ke7 30. f4 h6 31. Kf3 Rc7 32. g4 g5 33.
Ke4 Rc8 {From my point of view, this was the most dull game so far. After once
again displaying great preparation, which hasn't been considered his forte,
Carlsen avoided the most fighting continuations (which is considered his forte)
and satisifed himself with a draw.} 1/2-1/2

[Event "Game Eight"]
