1. e4 {I like this decision by Karjakin. He doesn't chicken after the victory,
but continues to playing principaly.} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 {So, for the 3rd
time in the match we have the Closed Ruy Lopez on the board.} 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O
b5 6. Bb3 Bc5 {[pgndiagram] A new variation. Instead of offering Marshall as
in games 2 and 4, Magnus goes for the Neo - Arkhangelsk variation. I think
that in modern times the move 6...Bc5 is played exclusively instead of the old
6... Bb7. It is curious to see what Garry Kasparov wrote about this in his
"Revolution in the 70s": "Apparently, Malanyuk was the first to conclude that
Black should not hurry with 6... Bb7, and he began emplyoing the improved
variation with 6.. Bc5" I am not versed enough into all the nuances to
understand why this is so to be honest.} 7. a4 {This 7th move is more rare
than c3.} (7. c3 d6 8. d4 {Is more topical} Bb6 9. a4 Bb7) 7... Rb8 {The main
reply to 7... Ra4.} (7... Bb7 8. d3 (8. c3 {[pgndiagram]} d6 9. d4 Bb6 {
Transposes to the main line examined under 7 c3.}) 8... b4) (7... Nxe4 $4 8.
axb5) 8. c3 (8. Nxe5 {[pgndiagram] Is the alternative.} Nxe5 9. d4 Bxd4 10.
Qxd4 d6 {[pgndiagram] And White's advantage is minimal, Topalov - Nakamura,
Norway Chess, 2015}) 8... d6 (8... Nxe4 {[pgndiagram] Is not to be advised} 9.
d4 exd4 10. cxd4 Bb4 11. d5 Na5 12. Bc2 Nf6 13. Re1+ $1 {[pgndiagram] A key
move} Bxe1 14. Qxe1+ Kf8 15. Qxa5 {With decisive advantage to White}) 9. d4 {
