difference does it make whether Queen is on c2 or d3?} Bd8 38. Re4 Nf6 39. Re5
Bc7 40. Bb5 Qd8 {[pgndiagram] And here is the difference. With the queen on c2
Rc5 threatens the bishop on c7,} 41. Rc5 Ra7 42. Bf4 {[pgndiagram]} Nd5 43.
Bxc7 Nxc7 {And White is slightly better,}) 36. Qd3 Ra1 37. Rxa1 Rxa1+ 38. Kg2 {
The culminating point of the game.} Ne7 $2 {[pgndiagram] I think it is correct
to call this move a blunder. I think that it might have cost Magnus the match
and probably his title as well.} (38... Bd8 {Was one of the ways to keep
defending comfortably} 39. Re4 Nf6 40. Re5 Bc7 41. Bb5 Qd8 {[pgndiagram] And
when compared with the position with the queen on c2, the bishop on c7 is not
hanging. Therefore, Black can get his draw rather easily.} 42. Rc5 Qd6) 39.
Bxf7+ $2 {It is hard to criticise this move, but apparently it is not the best.
} (39. Qb3 {[pgndiagram] Was very unpleasant} Nf5 40. Bxf7+ {And Carlsen would
have extremely hard choice to face just before the time control.} Qxf7 {I
think this is better way to try and save the game} (40... Kg7 41. Rh3 Qe7 42.
Bg8 $3 {[pgndiagram] And White is winning} h5 43. d5 {But as svidler said,
"Good luck finding this at the board"}) 41. Qxf7+ Kxf7 42. Rxh7+ Kg8 $1 (42...
Ke6 43. Rxc7 Nxd4 44. Rg7) 43. Rxc7 Nxd4 {[pgndiagram] And I am not certain
about the evaluation. Peter Svidler in his recap of the game said that with
the king cut off, the endgame should be convertible.}) 39... Kxf7 40. Qc4+ (40.
Rxh7+ $5 {[pgndiagram] Nice, but not the best} Kg8 41. Qxg6+ Nxg6 42. Rxd7 {
And White doesn't have winning chances}) 40... Kg7 41. d5 {[pgndiagram] The
point of the combination. White threatens Bc3 and Bh6 as well} Nf5 (41... Ra3
42. Bh6+) (41... g5 {Was crazy engine suggestion} 42. Bc3+ (42. Bxg5 Ng6) 42...
Kg6 43. Qd3+ Qf5 44. Re4 Ra4 {[pgndiagram] But it is no wonder why Magnus
didn't go for this}) 42. Bc3+ Kf8 43. Bxa1 Nxh4+ 44. Qxh4 Qxd5 {[pgndiagram]
Unfortunately for Karjakin, this combination has left him in a position where
he doesn't have serious winning chances} 45. Qf6+ (45. Qxh7 $2 Qg5+ {And
perpetual is immediate} 46. Kh3 Qf5+) 45... Qf7 46. Qd4 {[pgndiagram]
Naturally, Karjakin is entitled to play this position for a win, but I think
losing such a position at this level isn't to be expected} Ke8 47. Qe4+ Qe7 48.
Qd5 Bd8 49. Kf1 Qf7 50. Qe4+ Qe7 51. Be5 Qe6 52. Kg2 Be7 53. Qa8+ Kf7 54. Qh8
h5 55. Qg7+ Ke8 56. Bf4 Qf7 57. Qh8+ {[pgndiagram] White simply doesn't have
targets} Qf8 58. Qd4 Qf5 59. Qc4 Kd7 (59... Kf8 {[pgndiagram] Is also not
losing} 60. Bh6+ Ke8 61. Qg8+ Kd7) 60. Bd2 Qe6 61. Qa4+ Qc6 $1 {Only here did
Carlsen have to find the only move} (61... Kd8 $2 {[pgndiagram] Would be awful}
62. Ba5+ Kc8 63. Qe8+ Kb7 64. Bb4) 62. Qa7+ Qc7 63. Qa2 Qd6 64. Be3 Qe6 65.
Qa7+ Ke8 66. Bc5 Bd8 67. h3 Qd5 68. Be3 Be7 69. Qb8+ Kf7 70. Qh8 Qe6 71. Bf4
Qf6 72. Qb8 Qe6 73. Qb7 Kg8 74. Qb5 Bf6 {[pgndiagram] And finally a draw was
agreed. A fascinating clash where Karjakin played almost like a machine. But
to be honest, Carlsen's opening choice didn't leave him with many
opportunities to go wrong. At least that's my humble 2175 impression} 1/2-1/2

[Event "Game ten"]
[Site "0:08:33-0:04:33"]
[Date "2016.11.24"]
[Round "10"]
[White "Magnus Carlsen"]
[Black "Sergey Karjakin"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C65"]
[PlyCount "149"]
[EventDate "2016.11.11"]

{To be honest, I was anticipating 1 Sf3 at some point by Magnus, as Kasparov
played in the 24th game in the Seville match. But probably Magnus decided that
the "time" for such drastic measures hasn't yet arrived.} 1. e4 e5 {Of course,
there is no reason for Karjakin to deviate from the Berlin} 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5
Nf6 4. d3 $1 {I think that this is what Carlsen should have played from the
start, as the resulting positions are fully comapatibile with his style.} Bc5
5. c3 {[pgndiagram] For me it is always interesting to discover some nuances
in the move orders. White doesn't want to allow Nd4} (5. O-O Nd4 {Gives Black
this option} 6. Nxd4 Bxd4 7. c3 Bb6 {[pgndiagram] And from this position
Magnus lost his latest long time control game. Carlsen - Aronian, European
teams, 0-1. Probably it was noted by Karjakin's team}) 5... O-O 6. Bg5 $5 {And
this is already very rare.} (6. O-O {Is much more often} d6 7. Bg5 {
[pgndiagram] Most often White players wait for Black to play d6, so there
isn't any Be7 available for Black.}) 6... h6 7. Bh4 {[pgndiagram] It is
interesting to stop and consider this position for a moment.} Be7 {It is
interesting to note that the bishop retreat was Magnus' choice when he faced
this position with Black in Shirov - Carlsen, Biel 2011. Also, here is his
comment: " Once the white bishop is on h4, this loss of tempo is considered to
be justified"} (7... g5 8. Bg3 d6 9. Nbd2 {Is how the game Anand - Topalov,
Wch Saint Louis 2005 proceeded. Topalov managed to neutralize the White pieces,
but probably Karjakin didn't want to commit himself with the kingside pawn
advance so early.}) 8. O-O (8. Nbd2 {Is how the aforementioned game with
Shirov went. Magnus won that encounter as well.} d6 {And White prolonged
castling, but I am not certain about that "subtle" method} 9. Nf1) (8. Bxc6
bxc6 9. Nxe5 {[pgndiagram] doesn't win a pawn due to a standard trick} Nxe4 $1)
8... d6 9. Nbd2 Nh5 $1 {A good move. exchaning the bishop and eyeing the f4
square.} 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 11. Nc4 $1 {[pgndiagram]I like this way of maneouvering
the knight to e3 while keeping the centre static for the time being.} (11. d4 {
Was too rushed} Nf4 12. Re1 Qf6 {And black gets good counterplay}) 11... Nf4
12. Ne3 Qf6 {[pgndiagram] Maybe somewhat too cautious.} (12... f5 {Without
underestimating anyone, I think that many lower rated players would play this
"natural" move instantly I like it as well, opening the f-file and creating
breathing space for Black's pieces} 13. g3 fxe4 14. dxe4 Nh3+ 15. Kg2 Qf6 {
[pgndiagram] And Black has counterplay} 16. Qe2 Ne7 17. Ng1 $1 Nxg1 18. Rxg1 {
[pgndiagram] And in this computer variation White retains a very slight plus.
But the position is not a dead draw yet.}) 13. g3 $1 {Since white doesn't have
his light squared bishop, this might seem a little risky. But probably sooner
or later he would have to get rid of the strong BLack knight on f4.} Nh3+ 14.
Kh1 $6 {[pgndiagram] But this is indeed a bit too cautious} (14. Kg2 {Was
perfectly possible. Maybe Carlsen feared some Nf4 tactics in the future} Qg6
15. Nh4 Qg5 16. Nef5 {And White is better} Nf4+ 17. Kh1) 14... Ne7 15. Bc4 $6 {
Missing a very nice reply from black.} (15. Qe2 {Was probably better, due to a
deep computer tactic}) 15... c6 (15... b5 {[pgndiagram] While I was following
the game live on chessbomb, the computer screamed for this move to be played.
I didn't quite understand it, but it really isn't that hard} 16. Bb3 (16. Bxb5
Rb8 {Is the point. Black get's his pawn back and activates hsi rook} 17. Bc4
Rxb2 18. Kg2 {[pgndiagram] And Black's activity is worth at least for equality}
) 16... a5 17. a4 b4 {[pgndaigram] And Black has seized some space on the
queenside}) 16. Bb3 $1 {The idea of this move is to sidestep the d5 advance in
the future} (16. a4 $5 {Preventing queenside expansion once and for all, but
allowing the centre break}) (16. d4 {[pgndiagram] Was once again too fast} exd4
17. cxd4 Re8 18. e5 dxe5 19. dxe5 Qg6 {[pgndiagram] And Qe4 is anoying threat}
20. Nh4 Qe4+ 21. Qf3 Qxf3+ 22. Nxf3 {[pgndiagram] Balck is probably even
slightly better here}) 16... Ng6 {Both sides are maneouvering for the moment}
17. Qe2 a5 18. a4 Be6 $1 {[pgndiagram] A good move and a critical moment of
the early part of the game.} 19. Bxe6 $2 {Probably it is too harsh to
accompany this move with a question mark, but it could have led to a forced
draw. Which would be terrible result for Magnus at this stage of the match.} (
19. Bc2 {[pgndiagram] First of all, this simple bishop retreat would have kept
the game going} d5 {The logical continuation of Black's game.} 20. d4 dxe4 21.
Bxe4 Rae8 {[pgndiagram] And White is probably slightly better here. And more
importantly, a complicated struggle follows}) (19. Ra3 {[pgndiagram] Secondly,
the maneovre of the rook was the suggestion of both the engine and the
Gustafsson - Svidler commentating duo} Bxb3 20. Rxb3 Rab8 21. Nd2 {And White
is much better. Black probably shouldn't exchange on b3}) (19. Nd2 {
[pgndiagram] Finally, this is the move that Wesley so claims is the best in
the position} Bxb3 20. Nxb3 Rfe8 21. Rae1 {And once again White has good
prospects, Black has to be careful about the a-pawn and White will probably
advance in the centre sooner or later}) 19... fxe6 20. Nd2 {[pgndiagram] The
first critical moment} d5 $2 {Missing a very nice tactic and indicating that
nerves are also taking its toll on the challenger, and not just the champion} (
20... Nxf2+ $1 {[pgndiagram] This move is perfectly possible, as it would
either lead to a draw, or to a position where White would be "playing with
fire" if he was to continue.} 21. Kg1 {The only way to continue} (21. Kg2 {
Would run into} Nh4+ 22. Kg1 {The knight cannot be taken} (22. gxh4 $2 Qg6+ {
[pgndiagram] And Black is winning} 23. Ng4 Nxg4) 22... Nh3+ {With perpetual
check}) 21... Nh3+ 22. Kg2 Nhf4+ $1 {[pgndiagram] Karjakin said that he missed
this move in his preliminary calculations} 23. gxf4 Nxf4+ 24. Rxf4 exf4 25. Nc2
{[pgndiagram] And I think there is no doubt that Black shouldn't be worse here.
Of course, White has some practical chances, but I think that Carlsen wouldn't
risk this much at this point and would settle for a draw earlier.}) 21. Qh5 $2
{This time it is Carlsen's turn to go wrong and allow the challenger another
opportunity to draw the game immediately} (21. f3 $1 {Was very solid and much
more in Carlsen's style. From this point White can gradually improve his
position}) 21... Ng5 $2 {[%emt 0:00:20] Missing the chance to force a draw
once again. But too be honest this one was much harder to see than the one on
move} (21... Nxf2+ {[pgndiagram] Is perfectly viable here. However, the follow
up is not that obvious} 22. Kg2 {The only move} (22. Kg1 $2 {Is faulty due to
a knight fork tactic} Qg5 23. Qxg5 Nh3+ {[pgndiagram] And Black remains a
healthy pawn ahead}) 22... Qf7 {Threatening Nf4+ or Nh4 check} 23. Kg1 {And
now the key move is} Qf6 {[pgndiagram] While commentating live Peter Svidler
said that it is very hard to consider such a paradoxical computer move during
the preliminary calculations. Black simply threatens Qg5 and Nh3 again, and
there is no good way for white to avoid that}) 22. h4 $6 {Sometimes chess is
amazing. Instead of this obvious move, white had one much subtler} (22. Rae1 {
This was Svidler's find during the live feed (bear in mind that he was
analysing without the engine). The point ill be revealed soon} Nf3 23. Ng4 {
And now black must play Qg5 and play inferior endgame, because} Qf7 24. Nxf3
Qxf3+ 25. Kg1 Ne7 26. Re3 {[pgndiagram] And amazingly, Black's queen is trapped
}) 22... Nf3 23. Nxf3 Qxf3+ 24. Qxf3 Rxf3 {[pgndiagram] The crisis is over.
White has managed to get another position where play is "only on one goal".
Doubled e-pawns are a constant headache} 25. Kg2 Rf7 (25... Raf8 26. h5 Ne7 {
[pgndiagram] And now White can almost always win the exchange} 27. Nf5 (27.
Rad1 {With Nf5 or Ng4 next is probably eeven more precise}) 27... exf5 28. Kxf3
fxe4+ 29. Ke2 exd3+ 30. Kxd3 {[pgndiagram] And White is probably close to
winning, although a technical task still remains}) 26. Rfe1 $6 {Another slight
oversight} (26. Ng4 h5 27. Nh2 {Was probably better} Raf8 28. b4) 26... h5 $6 {
And again returning the favour} (26... Raf8 {[pgndiagram] Would earn some time,
because White would have to deal with the threat to the f-pawn} 27. Nd1 (27.
Re2 {Which is what Magnus probably planned, would enter into tactical counter}
dxe4 {I think that if Black plans to go Nf4, he should first exchange on e4.
The reason will be soon apparent} (27... Nf4+ {[pgndiagram] However, here the
machine finds the strong counter, so therefore taking on e4 is probably better}
28. gxf4 exf4 29. Nf5 $1 exf5 30. exd5 cxd5 31. Kf3) 28. dxe4 Nf4+ {
[pgndiagram] Now this works, since Black won't have the weakness on d5 after
Nf5} 29. gxf4 exf4 30. Nf5 exf5 31. e5 g5) 27... d4 28. Ra3 {And Black's
position is better than after what happened in the game}) 27. Nf1 {[pgndiagram]
Now everything is in order once again. White has alot of ideas. He can play
both in the centre with d4 and on the queenside with b4, when favourable
circumstances arive. More importantly, Black doesn't have any active
counterplay} Kf8 (27... Raf8 {Would only prolong, but not hinder White's plan}
28. Re2 Rf3 29. Ne3 R3f7 30. Nc2) 28. Nd2 Ke7 29. Re2 {[pgndiagram] There is
no reason to hurry I think} (29. Rab1 {Is something computer wants to do} Kd6
30. b4 axb4 31. Rxb4 {[pgndiagram] And here White has a pleasant position} Nf8
32. d4 exd4 33. cxd4 Ra7 34. Nf3 {[pgndiagram] And in this approxiamte
