axb4 39. cxb4 Nh6 40. Nb3) (38. c4 {[pgndiagram] Is now not so good} c5 39. Nb3
d4 {And this tempo move is hitting the rook here}) 38... Nh6 $6 {[pgndiagram]
Natural and uncommital move, heading for the f-pawn, but nevertheless not the
best} (38... c5 {Setting the queenside pawns in the motion and grabbing some
space was good. White would have to forget about b4 forever} 39. Nb3 b6 40. Rf6
Rg8 41. Nd2 Nd8 42. Re3 Kc6 {[pgndiagram] And Black's position is much more
resilient than the one with the b4-a5 White pawn duo}) 39. Rf6 Rg7 40. b4 $1 {
[pgndiagram]Finally White plays this breakthrough} (40. c4 {In the given
position this was a possible path, because of the king position} Nf5 (40... c5
41. Nb3 {And now d4 is not gaining a tempo}) (40... dxc4 41. Rd1 {Wins the e6
pawn}) 41. Nb3 {[pgndiagram]} b6 42. c5 Kc7 43. cxb6+ Kxb6 44. Rc1 {[pgniagram]
And White is close to winning. Black has many many many weaknesses}) 40... axb4
41. cxb4 Ng8 42. Rf3 Nh6 43. a5 {[pgndiagram] White has managed to form the
ideal pawn construction. Black is confined to back two ranks} Nf5 44. Nb3 {
[pgndiagram] Black can again only wait. White's plan is to rearange his pieces
and deliver the b5 breakthrough under favourable circumstances. The time has
not arrived for that yet, though} Kc7 45. Nc5 {[pgndiagram] Weasley So was
very critical of this move} (45. Rc1 {So claimed that this would win for White.
} Kb8 (45... g5 {This pawn push was examined repeatedly by Svidler and
Gustafsson, but with the knight on c5. As we will see later, there they could
never make it work because of Nxe6 tactic. But it also shows that top
grandmasters are always looking for counterplay possibilities} 46. hxg5 Rxg5
47. Rf4 h4 48. g4 h3+ 49. Kh2 Ne7 {[pgndiagram] And White's position is close
to winning.}) 46. a6 $1 {After this he may well be right} Rc7 47. Rfc3 bxa6 48.
Rxc6 Rxc6 49. Rxc6 Kb7 50. Na5+ Ka7 {[pgndiagram] And this position should be
considered a technical task for a player of Magnus' calibre.}) 45... Kb8 (45...
g5 {Doesn't work} 46. Nxe6+ Rxe6 47. Rxf5) 46. Rb1 {Preparing the future b5
break} Ka7 47. Rd3 Rc7 {[pgndiagram] Black has to wait passively} (47... g5 $6
48. hxg5 Rxg5 49. b5 cxb5 50. Rxb5 Re7 51. Rdb3 Rgg7 52. R3b4 {[pgndiagram]
And Black is even more passive than in the game. a6 is also coming soon} Rh7
53. a6 bxa6 54. Rb6 {And Black is lost}) 48. Ra3 Nd4 49. Rd1 Nf5 {[pgndiagram]
Black has to keep his knight near f5} (49... Nb5 {Is preventing the b5 break,
but now the punch comes from the other side} 50. Rf3 Rg7 51. Rf6 {[pgndiagram]
And although here computer shows enormous advantage, I haven't been able to
find anything definite for White. Probably transfering the knight on f4 is the
best idea} Nc7 52. Nd3 (52. f4 {Is computer's suggestion, but I don't see how
White can make progress after this} Ree7 53. Kf3 Re8 54. Ke3 Ree7 55. Ra1 Re8)
52... Ree7 53. Nf4 Ref7 54. Nxe6 Nxe6 55. Rxe6 {[pgndiagram] With a won endgame
}) 50. Kh3 {There is absolutely no need to hurry. The stakes are high} Nh6 51.
f3 {Now White can aim for g4 or b5 or Nd3-Nf4} Rf7 {[pgndiagram]} (51... Nf7 {
Attacking the e-pawn, was interesting} 52. Nd3 Nh6 53. Nf4 Rg7 54. a6 b6 55.
Rc1) 52. Rd4 Nf5 {[pgndiagram]} (52... Rf5 {Was a possibility. White would
ideally avoid commiting with f4} 53. Re3 Nf7 54. f4 (54. Nd3 g5 {And Black
gets some counterplay at least} 55. hxg5 Nxg5+ 56. Kg2 b6) 54... Nh6 55. Ra3
Rf7 {And it is not clear how White intends to breakthrough} 56. Rd2 Nf5 57. Rb2
Nd4 {[pgndiagram] And b5 is impossible for the moment}) 53. Rd2 Rh7 54. Rb3
Ree7 $6 {It is very difficult to understand all the nuances of this position} (
54... Rf7 $1 {[pgndiagram] Was a better option. Black would like to play Rc6
after the b5 breakthrough. The move played by Karjakin doesn't allow that
since e6 would fall} 55. b5 {White should probably deffer this, but we will
demonstrate Black's idea} cxb5 56. Rxb5 Rc7 57. Rdb2 Ree7 58. R2b4 Ne3 {
[pgndiagram] And black has equalized}) 55. Rdd3 (55. b5 {Was now possible} cxb5
56. Rxb5 Rhg7 57. Rdb2 {[pgndiagram] And you can see how superior White's
position is compared to one after 54... Rf7} (57. g4 $5 {This is an important
moment. Here Black can met g4 by Ne3. When the White rook is on d3 Ne3 is
impossible and Black stands clearly worse. See analysis after 56th move} Ne3)
57... Ne3 58. f4 Nc4 59. R2b3 Rgf7 60. Kg2) 55... Rh8 56. Rb1 Rhh7 $2 {And
this is the decisive mistake White was waiting for. To me it is not even
remotely clear what difference does it make compared to the earlier play,
except that Black cannot put a rook on c file after b5} (56... Nh6 57. b5 cxb5
58. Rxb5 Rc8 {Is the point. Black would like to put a rook on c file after
White has opened with b5}) 57. b5 $1 {[pgndiagram] Compared to the position on
the 55th move, White's rooks are on d3 and b1 instead of d2 and b3.} cxb5 58.
Rxb5 d4 (58... Rhg7 59. g4 {And now there is no Ne3. Chess is really a
fantastic game.}) 59. Rb6 {[pgndiagram] Now e6 falls, and White is clearly
winning} Rc7 60. Nxe6 Rc3 61. Nf4 {[pgndiagram] It is instructive to watch how
Magnus converts his advantage} Rhc7 62. Nd5 {[pgndiagram] Carlsen takes a very
practical decision to exchange a pair of rooks and therefore clarify the
situation} (62. Rxg6 Rxd3 63. Nxd3 Rc3 64. Rf6 Ne7 65. Nf4 Rxf3 {[pgndiagram]
Was good, but more complicated and computer solution}) 62... Rxd3 63. Nxc7 {
[pgndiagram]} Kb8 (63... Rd1 64. Nb5+ Kb8 (64... Ka8 65. Rf6 {[%emt 0:00:05]
And mate in}) 65. a6) 64. Nb5 Kc8 {Moving from the pin} (64... Rxf3 65. a6) 65.
Rxg6 Rxf3 66. Kg2 {Very precise. Bringing the king closer} (66. e6 $2 {
[pgndiagram] It is never to late to spoil a chess game} Kd8) (66. Nd6+ {
Immediately is not so good} Nxd6 67. Rxd6 Re3 68. e6 Re4 69. e7 Rxe7 70. Rxd4
Re5 71. Ra4) 66... Rb3 67. Nd6+ Nxd6 68. Rxd6 {[pgndiagram]And now White
managed to put his rook on the ideal d5 square} Re3 $6 (68... Rb5 69. e6 Kc7
70. e7 Re5 71. Rxd4 Rxe7 72. Rd5) (68... Kc7 {Was the best defensive try} 69.
Rxd4 Rb5 70. Re4 Rxa5 71. Kf3 {[pgndiagram] And White should still be winning,
although some technique is still required}) 69. e6 Kc7 70. Rxd4 Rxe6 71. Rd5 {
[pgndiagram] Karjakin could have resigned here. The White rook finds itself on
an ideal square, defending a5 and attacking h5} Rh6 72. Kf3 Kb8 73. Kf4 Ka7 74.
Kg5 Rh8 75. Kf6 {[pgndiagram] Next is Kg7 and White will win. A game of
enormous significance, both for this match and for the chess history.
Exploiting the drawing chance on move 20 would put Carlsen in a desperate
situation, where he would have only two more games to save his title. However,
"fortune" follows the brave, and Magnus probably deserved to have at least
equal score at this point of the match. It will be interesting to follow the
two remaining games, since Magnus will probably get huge psychological boost
because of this result.} 1-0

[Event "Game Eleven"]
[Site "New York, NY USA"]
[Date "2016.11.26"]
[Round "11"]
[White "Sergey Karjakin"]
[Black "Magnus Carlsen"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "C77"]
[PlyCount "67"]
[EventDate "2016.11.11"]

{In the last White game of the regular part of the World Championship,
Karjakin sticks to his main move.} 1. e4 e5 {Naturally, Carlsen also replies
with what he knows the best.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 {And for the 4th time in
the match Carlsen says no to Berlin.} 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 {[pgndiagram]
Repeating the Closed Ruy Lopez from the games 2 and 4 and 6.} (5... b5 {In the
9th game Carlsen deviated and played the Neo-Arkhangelsk variation} 6. Bb3 Bc5
{But he experienced serious problems in that game}) 6. d3 {Karjakin once again
avoids the Marshall counterattack} b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. a3 {Repeating the variation
from the 2nd game.} O-O 9. Nc3 {So far everything as in game two. Karjakin
obviously had some idea in mind. However, Carlsen once again forestalls him
and deviates first.} Be6 $5 {[pgndiagram] A nice idea. Black offers a bishop
exchange which would ruin his pawn structure. But because of his mobility in
the centre and control of d5, he judges that it is not disadvantageous for him}
(9... Na5 {Would be repetition of game two}) 10. Nd5 $5 {A typical idea. White
refuses to go along with Black's plans, but jumps on the d5 square while it is
available} (10. Bxe6 fxe6 11. Bd2 d5 {Is apparently ok for Black}) 10... Nd4 $1
{Carlsen played this instantly. This move forced the exchange on d4, leaving
Black with a mobile pawn chain on the queenside. The player who played this
relieveing move most often was Peter Svidler. There are 3 games from 2013 year
in my database where Svidler plays Black in this position.} (10... Bxd5 {Is
not a good idea} 11. exd5 Na5 12. Ba2 {And White is simply better}) (10... Na5
{Would result in an inferior position for Black} 11. Nxf6+ Bxf6 12. Bxe6 fxe6
13. b4 Nc6 14. Be3 {And White is clearly better}) 11. Nxd4 {[pgndiagram]
Nothing else really brings any advantage} (11. Nxe7+ Qxe7 12. Nxd4 exd4 13. Bg5
Bxb3 {And Black has probably equalized. And he even went on to win. Ivanchuk -
Svidler, Thessaloniki, 2013}) 11... exd4 12. Nxf6+ (12. Nxe7+ {[%emt 0:00:11]
Would leave to variations examined after  Nxe7}) 12... Bxf6 13. Bxe6 fxe6 {
[pgndiagram] It is time to pause and evaluate the result of the opening. Black
has a doubled pawn on the d-file, but it is hard for White to attack it or to
play against it. Furthermore, after the potential d5 in the future it is easy
to get rid of that "weakness" On the other hand, White has a kingside pawn
majority. So he will play on that wing.} 14. f4 {Making a useful move while
keeping his queen on the starting square for the moment and retaining the
option of the quick g4 push} (14. a4 {[pgndiagram] Both Anand and Dominguez
Perez played this move against Svidler in the 2013} Qd7 (14... c5 {Was
Svidler's choice against Anand} 15. Qg4 Qd7 16. axb5 axb5 17. Bd2 {And he
immediately equalized}) 15. b3 c5 16. Qg4 Qf7 (16... b4 $5 {Is a possible
improvement}) 17. Bd2 c4) 14... c5 {[pgndiagram] Apparently a novelty of the
game. Black creates a "triangle" in the centre, and has ideas about pushing
further in the future.} (14... e5 {Is another idea} 15. f5 d5 16. Qe2 c5 17. g4
c4 {And Black's counterplay on the queenside probably balances White's push on
the kingside}) 15. Qg4 {[pgndiagram]Typical move for this variation} (15. Rf3 {
Is now met by} d5 16. e5 Be7 17. Rh3 g6 {And White' attack is nonexistent})
15... Qd7 16. f5 $6 {I think this is creating tension too early. White is not
sufficiently developed} (16. Bd2 Rf7 17. b3 Raf8 {Shouldn't pose Black much
problems either, though. It seems like whole variation is relatively harmless})
16... Rae8 {[pgndiagram] Black had sevelar ways of reaching equality} (16...
exf5 17. Rxf5 Rf7 18. Bd2 Be5 {Is probably most clear cut way to equality}) (
16... e5 $5 {Is also a possibility}) 17. Bd2 {Finally completing the
development. Karjakin prefers to keep the tension in the centre for the time
being} (17. fxe6 Qxe6 18. Qxe6+ Rxe6 19. Bd2 Be5 {Is equal}) (17. a4 d5 18.
axb5 axb5 {And Black has no problemos}) 17... c4 $5 {[pgndiagram] Sharpening
the play. Magnus sets his queenside pawns in the motion} (17... exf5 {Was once
again easy path to equality} 18. Rxf5 Rf7 19. Raf1 Be5) 18. h3 $6 {This is
probably a bit too slow in a position like this. White's idea is to defend the
queen and prepare the future h3-g4} (18. fxe6 Qxe6 19. Qxe6+ Rxe6 20. Bb4 Rd8
21. Rf5 {Is probably equal as well, though}) 18... c3 $5 {[pgndiagram] Trying
to set concrete problems for White.} (18... e5 $5 {Was another interesting
idea. Trying to close the centre in order to play on the queenside.} 19. Qf3
Rc8 20. g4 h6 21. Rac1 cxd3 22. cxd3 Qf7 {And after this approximate variation
Black should be slightly better. White's attack on the queenside is long in
coming}) 19. bxc3 d5 $1 {Carlsen probably prepared this move already when
considering 18...c3!?. It came as clear surprise to Karjakin, who now had to
consider multiple options and captures.} 20. Bg5 {Karjakin choses a save path}
(20. fxe6 {[pgndiagram] First of all, this obvious capture may lead to some
problems} Qxe6 21. Qxe6+ Rxe6 22. exd5 Re2 23. Rf2 Rxf2 24. Kxf2 Bg5+ 25. Ke2
Bxd2 26. Kxd2 Rf2+ 27. Ke1 Rxg2 {[pgndiagram] And it is not at all easy to
evaluate this position} 28. Kf1 Rh2 29. Kg1 Re2 {And the position is equal.} (
29... Rxc2 $4 {Would be terrible} 30. d6 Kf7 31. Re1) 30. a4 Kf7 31. axb5 axb5
32. d6) (20. exd5 {[pgndiagram] Would be very bad decision} dxc3 $5 {A great
move. It leads to same variation as immediate capture on f5, but it is really
beautiful. To be honest I haven't seen mentioned by either GM Hess in his
chesscom analysis, or by GM So in his chessbase analysis.} (20... exf5 21. Qf3
dxc3 22. Be3 {Would transpose of course}) 21. Be3 (21. dxe6 Qa7+) 21... exf5
22. Qf3 Qd6 {And Black has some chances to press for the win}) (20. c4 {
[pgndiagram] Finally, this move is also possible. White admits c pawn will be
lost anyway and gets rid of it right away.} dxe4 $1 {The only move for Black
that doesn't land him into a worse position} (20... bxc4 {Is now bad} 21. Bb4
Rf7 22. fxe6 Qxe6 23. Qxe6 Rxe6 24. exd5 {And now White is suddenly better})
21. Bb4 exf5 22. Qxf5 Rf7 23. cxb5 axb5 24. dxe4 Qxf5 25. exf5 {[pgndiagram]
And after the dust has settled, an equal endgame is on the board}) 20... Bxg5
21. Qxg5 dxe4 22. fxe6 Rxf1+ 23. Rxf1 Qxe6 24. cxd4 e3 $5 {[pgndiagram]
Carlsen avoids the immedate draw and tries to use his last chance - the passed
e-pawn.} (24... exd3 25. cxd3 Qe3+ 26. Qxe3 Rxe3 {Is an easy draw}) 25. Re1 h6
{Creating some breathing space for the king with tempo is always a good idea}
26. Qh5 e2 27. Qf3 {[pgndiagram] White has enough resources to deal with the
e-pawn} (27. Kf2 $4 {Would be a terrible blunder} Qe3# {And White gets mated})
27... a5 {Carlsen's idea is b4-a4-a3-a2-a1-e1Q. Of course, he will have none
of that} 28. c3 {Forestalling Black's idea} Qa2 $5 {The last try. White has to
reckon with Qd2 ideas} 29. Qc6 $1 {Precise defence that soon leads to
perpetual check} Re6 {[pgndiagram] There is no much difference in the position
of the rook.} (29... Re7 30. Qc8+ Kh7 31. Qf8 Qd2 32. Qxe7 Qxe1+ 33. Kh2 {
Would lead to the same perpetual that appeared in the actual game}) 30. Qc8+
Kh7 31. c4 {And now the draw is practically forced} Qd2 32. Qxe6 Qxe1+ 33. Kh2
Qf2 34. Qe4+ {And here the draw was agreed. Another very correct game by both
players where it was again Carlsen who brought the excitement at the board. He
seemed very confident after his win, but Karjakin also played very precisely.}
1/2-1/2

[Event "Tiebreaks game 1"]
[Site "0:02:33-0:08:33"]
[Date "2016.11.30"]
[Round "13.1"]
[White "Sergey Karjakin"]
[Black "Magnus Carlsen"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "C77"]
[PlyCount "74"]
[EventDate "2016.11.11"]

1. e4 {It probably made alot of sense for Karjakin to stick to his main
opening move in the tiebreaks as well.} e5 {Of course. Magnus plays this
almost exclusively} 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 {And the dispute in the Closed Ruy
from the first part of the match continues in the tiebreaks as well.} 4. Ba4
Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. d3 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. a3 {Karjakin once again chooses the a3-d3
setup} O-O 9. Nc3 {[pgndiagram] This position has been reached twice in the
match. In the game two Magnus went 9 ... Na6 here, while in the game eleven he
deviated with 9... Be6} Nb8 {Once again Carlsen deviates and goes for this
"Breyer-like" maneuvre.} (9... Na5) (9... Be6) 10. Ne2 {Karjakin goes for the
very controlled knight move.} (10. a4 {[pgndiagram] Was Caruana's choice} b4
11. Nd5 Nxd5 12. Bxd5 c6 13. Bb3 {And Black has equalized}) (10. Ng5 $5 {
[pgndiagram] Is Gajewski's agressive idea.} Nc6 {The simplest move. Black
heads for the weakened d4 immediately. This paradoxical move is very hard to
find over the board.} (10... h6 11. f4 {[pgndiagram] Is the point} exf4 (11...
hxg5 12. fxg5 {Is dangerous for Black}) 12. Nxf7 Rxf7 13. Bxf4) 11. Ba2 Nd4 12.
Ne2 {[pgndiagram] And White shouldn't have any advantage, Anand - So, Gashimov
Memorial 2015}) 10... c5 {Grabbing the centre. I think that after 10 Ne2 Black
shouldn't have serious problems.} 11. Ng3 Nc6 12. c3 {[pgndiagram] Black has
enough space, and I am not certain what White's plan in this position should
represent. Black has clear idea of playing on the queenside} Rb8 (12... a5 {
Was perfectly possible at once}) (12... Be6 {Is how the game Malakhov - Ding,
China Championship Teams 2015 went}) 13. h3 a5 (13... Be6 {Is not the best
move, since White manages to go b4 himself, hindering Black's play somewhat}
14. Bxe6 fxe6 15. b4) 14. a4 b4 15. Re1 {[pgndiagram] Black has simply
equalized} Be6 16. Bc4 (16. Bxe6 {Would only help Black, since it would
control the f5 square, and increase Black's pawn mass in the centre}) 16... h6
(16... Bxc4 {Is also not the best, since White can get rid of the doubled
pawns anytime by cxb4. Furthermore, the pawn on c4 can't be attacked, and it
only puts d5 firmly under White's control.}) 17. Be3 Qc8 {[pgndiagram] To be
honest, I didn't quite understand why Carlsen didn't go for more natural Qc7.
Edit: After analysing a bit, I think the point of this move is to have f5
square under control, in case of d5 advance} (17... Qc7 18. Nd2 d5 19. exd5
Nxd5 {And Black is OK}) 18. Qe2 (18. Nd2 {I don't see much difference in this
variation compared to Qc7} d5 19. exd5 Nxd5 20. Bxd5 Bxd5 {And here also Black
stands well. Maybe slight difference is that here f5 square is under control})
18... Rd8 {[pgndiagram] Preparing d5} 19. Bxe6 fxe6 $6 (19... Qxe6 {Would have
been slightly better I think. Because after that d4 wouldn't have been
possible.} 20. Nf5 (20. d4 exd4 21. cxd4 Nxe4) 20... Bf8 {And Black is under
control}) 20. d4 {[pgndiagram] Now White manages to advance in the centre,
leaving him slightly better} bxc3 {This exchange is useful} (20... cxd4 21.
cxd4 exd4 22. Bxd4 {And the rook on b8 is dumb}) 21. bxc3 cxd4 22. cxd4 exd4
23. Nxd4 {[pgndiagram]} (23. Bxd4 {This counter intuitive move was interesting}
Nd7 {Taking on d4 is not good for Black, the White knight gains b5 and e6 is
weak} 24. Nh5 Bf8 25. Rac1 {[pgndiagram] And White has some pressure}) 23...
Nxd4 24. Bxd4 Rb4 {Now Black manages to stabilize, and his activity
compensates for his slightly weakened pawn structure.} 25. Rec1 Qd7 $1 {
[pgndiagram] Care is required. Attacking the a4 pawn is safe way to equalize} (
25... Qb7 26. Bc3 {Loses the a pawn}) 26. Bc3 Rxa4 27. Bxa5 Rxa1 28. Rxa1 {And
now dead draw is on the board.} Ra8 29. Bc3 Rxa1+ 30. Bxa1 Qc6 31. Kh2 Kf7 32.
Bb2 Qc5 33. f4 Bd8 34. e5 dxe5 35. Bxe5 Bb6 36. Qd1 Qd5 37. Qxd5 Nxd5 1/2-1/2

[Event "Tiebreas game three"]
[Site "0:00:33-0:07:33"]
[Date "2016.11.30"]
[Round "13.3"]
[White "Sergey Karjakin"]
[Black "Magnus Carlsen"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C77"]
[PlyCount "76"]
[EventDate "2016.11.11"]

{The final act of the play started with the fascinating struggle in game three
} 1. e4 {Naturally, Karjakin sticks to what he knows the best.} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6
3. Bb5 a6 {And for the final time in this match we have the opportunity to
enjoy in the Closed Ruy Lopez.} 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. d3 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. a3
O-O 9. Nc3 {[pgndiagram] This position can rightfully be considered a tabiya
of this match. The position was reached in games two and eleven of the regular
part of the match, and also in the first game of the tiebreaks.} Na5 {Carlsen
repeats his play from the game two.} (9... Be6 10. Nd5 {Was the game eleven}) (
9... Nb8 {Was the game one of the tiebreaks. Karjakin has been unable to prove
advantage in any of the given lines.}) 10. Ba2 Be6 11. b4 {[pgndiagram]
Karjakin is first to deviate this time.} (11. d4 Bxa2 12. Rxa2 {My opinion is
that this move order from the 2nd game is still critical and probably White's
best try for advantage}) 11... Nc6 $1 {And Carlsen immediately plays a novelty.
} (11... Bxa2 {In my database I have 4 2650+ games where this position has
been reached. All 4 games witnessed this bishop exchange before the knight
retreat} 12. Rxa2 Nc6 13. Bg5 {And White probably has some slight advantage,
although all 4 games ended in a draw.}) 12. Nd5 {As explained in the analysis
of the game eleven, White often jumps at d5 when it is available} Nd4 {
[pgndiagram]And this jump is also familiar from the game eleven} (12... Bxd5
13. exd5 Nb8 14. c4 {It is not a good idea to leave White with a strong pawn
on d5 and two bishops}) 13. Ng5 {The idea is to hit the bishop on e6.} Bxd5 {
Most straightforward} (13... c6 {Is also possible though.} 14. Nxe6 Nxe6 15.
Nxf6+ Bxf6 16. Be3 d5 {And Black is probably equal, although with the two
bishops White's chances are slightly better.}) 14. exd5 Nd7 {[pgndiagram]
Gaining some tempo on the White knight} (14... Nxd5 {Doesn't work} 15. Nxf7
Kxf7 16. Bxd5+ Ke8 17. Bxa8) 15. Ne4 f5 16. Nd2 {[pgndiagram]} (16. c3 {Was
not pointless, and maybe this intermezzo was a better move.} fxe4 17. cxd4 exd3
18. dxe5 Nxe5 19. Bb1 {And White probably retains some slight advantage}) 16...
f4 {Neccessary, to create an escape route for the knight.} 17. c3 Nf5 {
[pgndiagram] It is time to pause and take a look at the result of the opening.
White should probably be slightly better, because of the weakened light
squares in the black structure. However, Black has more space on the kingside,
and in the games with lower time control parrying attacks is considerably
harder then in normal over the board play.} 18. Ne4 {Perhaps it was better to
place the knight on b3, in order to prevent a5 ideas in the future} (18. Nb3) (
18. Re1 $5 {Also has a nice tactical point behind} Qe8 19. d4 $1 exd4 (19...
Qg6 20. Bb1 {And it's a pin}) 20. cxd4 Nxd4 21. Ne4 Nf5 22. Bxf4 {And White is
considerably better}) 18... Qe8 {Now the queen swings over effectively} 19. Bb3
(19. d4 Qg6 20. Bb1 {Now this is not pin due to knight on e4} Nh4) 19... Qg6 {
[pgndiagram] Now White has to take some care on the kingside.} 20. f3 Bh4 21.
a4 {Creating some play on the queenside} (21. Bd2 Nf6 22. a4 {Is very similar})
21... Nf6 {Bringing the last piece to the kingside.} (21... a5 $1 {Was
immediately possible}) 22. Qe2 (22. Nxf6+ {Is perhaps not advisable} Qxf6 23.
Bd2 Bg3 {And the removal of the knight allows installment of the strong g3
bishop} 24. Qe2 Qh4 25. h3 Qd8 {And I think only Black can play for the win in
this position}) 22... a5 $3 {[pgndiagram] To my primitive chess mind this is a
fantastic move. After ganging his pieces on the kingside, Carslen changes
direction and makes a pawn move on the other side of the board.} 23. axb5 (23.
bxa5 {Was inferior} Rxa5 24. Ra2 {There is nothing better really} bxa4 25. Rxa4
Rxa4 26. Bxa4 Nxd5) 23... axb4 24. Bd2 {A nice calm move} (24. Rxa8 Rxa8 25.
cxb4 Nd4 {And White's pawn structure is in ruins, while Black will regain his
pawn soon after the capture on b5.}) 24... bxc3 25. Bxc3 {[pgndiagram] After
White's precise replies Black still has a slight edge} Ne3 {Perhaps this was a
bit too quick.} (25... Bg3 {Was a possibility here} 26. Rxa8 {White has to be
precise} (26. hxg3 $2 {Is probably not advisable} fxg3 {And White is living on
the edge} 27. Rxa8 Rxa8 28. Ra1 Rxa1+ 29. Bxa1 Qh6 {And he will most probably
have to give back the piece on g3}) 26... Rxa8 27. Ra1 Rxa1+ 28. Bxa1 Nxe4 29.
dxe4 Qh5 30. h3 Ne3 {And at the end, Black still has some slight advantage})
26. Rfc1 Rxa1 27. Rxa1 Qe8 {[pgndiagram] Striking the b5} 28. Bc4 Kh8 {Moving
out of the way, so that the knight is not pinned once it captures on d5} (28...
Nfxd5 {Is too hasty} 29. Qa2 Qf7 {And Black doesn't have an advantage}) 29.
Nxf6 {Removing the threat to the d5 pawn in the more radical way.} (29. Qa2 {
Defending the d5 pawn was another possibility} Qg6 {Black can continue to play
for compensation} 30. Kh1 Rb8) 29... Bxf6 $5 {[pgndiagram] According to the
computer this is not the best move. But I think that this is the move that won
Carlsen.. the match.} (29... gxf6 {Opening the g-file, with the pressure on g2,
is better from the chess truth perspective} 30. Kh1 {This is suboptimal move,
but I think it is instructive to see how precise White has to be. He probably
has to find the series of only moves.} (30. Bd2 {Is the best chance} Nc2 31.
Ra7 Nd4 32. Qf1 Nxb5 {Although even here Black has serious winning chances})
30... Rg8 31. Rg1 Bg3 {And Black is winning}) 30. Ra3 {Defending the bishop} e4
$3 {[pgndiagram] To my mind this is an amazing move. Such a pawn breakthrough
is surely unpleasant to face when already low on time. Black simply gives up a
pawn for a complete Black square domination. Objectively position is still
equal, but now White has to find some precise move in order not to lose, while
Black is hardly risking anything.} 31. dxe4 Bxc3 32. Rxc3 Qe5 {[pgndiagram]
The difference in activity of the pieces compensates for the material deficit.}
33. Rc1 $6 (33. Qd2 {Was probably easier} Ra8 34. Bf1 Ra1 35. Rc1 h6 {And the
position is probably equal}) 33... Ra8 34. h3 $6 {Karjakin makes the most
obvious moves} (34. Qd2 Ra1 35. Bf1 g5 {And Black can play forever, but
White's position shouldn't be breachable}) 34... h6 35. Kh2 Qd4 36. Qe1 $6 {
[pgndiagram] And only this can be called a mistake. Because after this move
White's defensive task becomes much more hard} (36. Bd3 Ra1 37. Rxa1 Qxa1 38.
Qd2 {And White is very much in the game}) 36... Qb2 {Now it suddenly gets very
unpleasant.} 37. Bf1 Ra2 38. Rxc7 $4 {[pgndiagram] And White cracks under the
tension and commits a decisive blunder.} (38. Rb1 {Was now aboslutely
neccessary.} Qf6 {The idea is to go Qg5 next. White has to find only moves to
survive.} 39. Be2 {Even Peter Svidler in his commentary said that this
self-pinning move is very hard to find over the board. Especially in a rapid
game}) 38... Ra1 {And suddenly it is game over as White loses material.} 0-1

[Event "Tiebreaks game four"]
[Site "0:01:33-0:00:33"]
[Date "2016.11.30"]
[Round "13.4"]
[White "Magnus Carlsen"]
[Black "Sergey Karjakin"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B55"]
[PlyCount "99"]
[EventDate "2016.11.11"]

1. e4 {It seems like the number of 1 e4 games in this match confirms the
Fischer's evaluation of that move : "Best by test!"} c5 {Of course! When you
need to win a game, Sicilian is the most logical choice.} 2. Nf3 d6 {"Najdorf,
please?"} 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. f3 {[pgndiagram] And you will have none of
that. This variation is the so-called Prins Variation. It occurs here and
there on the highest level, but the main thing is that it is hardly a serious
winning try if Black is content with a draw.} e5 {This move I like to see when
playing e5} (5... e6 {Since my good friend and co-author of this blog, FM
Matej Blazeka has showed me this opening, I have been using it regularly with
decent results. Therefore, I hate the variation with e6 much more. I think
that going for the fast d5 is the best thing Black can do, since it will make
the f3 pawn look plain stupid} 6. c4 Be7 7. Nc3 O-O 8. Be3 Nc6 9. Be2 d5 {
[pgndiagram] And Black has equalized} 10. exd5 exd5 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. cxd5) 6.
Nb3 Be7 {[pgndiagram]} (6... a5 {Too be honest, if Black wants to play with a5,
it makes more sense to do so immediately. The point is that White can't go for
Maroczy bind now} 7. c4 $2 (7. Nc3 {Is the correct approach}) 7... Nxe4 $1 {
[pgndiagram]} 8. fxe4 Qh4+ 9. Kd2 a4 {And White's knight has no retreat
squares available}) (6... d5 {Is another theme} 7. Bg5 Be6 8. Bxf6 gxf6 9. exd5
Qxd5 10. Qxd5 Bxd5 11. Nc3 {And in this endgame only White can play for the
win, although the majority of games end up as a draw.}) 7. c4 a5 8. Be3 {
[pgndiagram] I think that White simply gets a good version of the Maroczy now}
a4 9. Nc1 {Such "ugly" knight maneovres are typical of the entire variation.}
O-O 10. Nc3 Qa5 11. Qd2 Na6 $6 {[pgndiagram] Not the best.} (11... Be6 12. Be2
Rc8 {Pressuring the c4 square, wuld have forced a concession from White}) 12.
Be2 Nc5 13. O-O {[pgndiagram] White has a very pleasant position, he has
succesfully carried out all the main ideas of the system without being
hindered.} Bd7 14. Rb1 {The b4 plan is very logical, disrupting the piece
omposition on the queenside.} Rfc8 15. b4 axb3 16. axb3 {It is clear that
Karjakin's had an off day and that he can hardly expect to win this game.} Qd8
17. Nd3 {[pgndiagram]The best way of disturbing Black's best piece, the knight
on c5} (17. b4 Na4 {Was a bit too hasty. The White's knight would ideally like
to use b4 as a jumping point.}) 17... Ne6 18. Nb4 {[pgndiagram] Seizing
control of the d5 square} Bc6 (18... Nf4 {Is computer's first choice. Such a
recommendation by the engine is a clear sign that Black's position is dubious}
19. Bxf4 Qb6+ 20. Be3 Qxb4 21. Rfd1 {And White is still smooth sailing}) 19.
Rfd1 {[pgndiagram] Black simply doesn't have a single active idea} h5 {This is
a bit of desperation} (19... Nd4 $1 {Was not a bad chance} 20. Bxd4 exd4 21.
Qxd4 d5 $1 22. Nxc6 (22. Nbxd5 $2 Bxd5 23. Nxd5 $2 Bc5 {Loses the queen}) 22...
Rxc6 23. b4 {And White remains in control}) 20. Bf1 {[pgndiagram] Carlsen
plays rather safe and slow, occupation of the d5 was perfectly possible,} h4
21. Qf2 {Again ignoring d5} (21. Nbd5) 21... Nd7 22. g3 {This was perhaps also
unnecessary. White plans to play the bishop on h3, but this gives Black time
to latch on some ideas.} (22. Kh1) (22. Rd2) 22... Ra3 (22... Bg5 {Was
apparently the best. Usualy in this structure it is favourable to exchange the
dark squared bishops for black} 23. Rxd6 $2 {Taking the pawn is very bad idea.}
Bxe3 24. Qxe3 Qe7 {And Black suddenly breaths}) 23. Bh3 Rca8 24. Nc2 {
[pgndiagram] The White knights are doing a wonderful job in keeping the black
rooks out} R3a6 25. Nb4 Ra5 {Naturally, there will be no repetition} 26. Nc2 (
26. f4 {Was very interesting, but rather unnecessary in the given match
situation} hxg3 27. hxg3 exf4 28. gxf4 Rh5 {And suddenly play is much more
concrete and tactical than it was a couple of moves ago}) 26... b6 (26... R5a6
{Is objectively the best, but of course, Karjakin couldn't agree to a draw in
this game}) 27. Rd2 Qc7 28. Rbd1 {[pgndiagram] White is probably close to
winning at this point} Bf8 29. gxh4 {Simplifying the position and gaining a
pawn in the process.} Nf4 30. Bxf4 exf4 31. Bxd7 Qxd7 32. Nb4 {[pgndiagram]} (
32. Qxb6 {Was perfectly possible}) 32... Ra3 33. Nxc6 Qxc6 34. Nb5 {
[pgndiagram] The classic example of the -good knight vs bad bishop} Rxb3 {
Karjakin tries to mix things up with an exchange sacrifice, and practically
speaking it is not a bad decision. The problem is that both Carlsen and his
position are too strong.} (34... Ra1 {Trying for the d5 break, was interesting}
35. Kh1 {And that is the end of the demonstration} (35. Kg2 d5)) (34... d5 {
Immediately doesn't work} 35. Nxa3 Bc5 36. Rd4) 35. Nd4 Qxc4 36. Nxb3 Qxb3 {
[pgndiagram] Here White should be technically winning. Moreover, Karjakin was
already below two minutes ata this point} 37. Qe2 Be7 38. Kg2 Qe6 39. h5 Ra3
40. Rd3 Ra2 41. R3d2 Ra3 42. Rd3 Ra7 {[pgndiagram] Naturally, under normal
circumstances Karjakin would have agreed to draw without hesitation. However,
under normal circumstances Magnus would have never offered a repetition in
this position} 43. Rd5 Rc7 44. Qd2 Qf6 45. Rf5 Qh4 46. Rc1 Ra7 47. Qxf4 {Now
soon comes the explosive finish} Ra2+ 48. Kh1 Qf2 {At this point almost
everyone was expecting Magnus tu go for Qg3 and play it safe. But he finishes
the game and the match in style.} 49. Rc8+ $3 {[pgndiagram] Not that hard to
see, but still, choosing a stylish instead of a safe continuation requires
some nerves and bravery.} Kh7 (49... Bf8 50. Rxf8+ Kxf8 51. Rxf7+) 50. Qh6+ $3
{One of the most beautiful checkmate pictures I have ever seen. Even more
beuatiful when the importance of the game and the identity of the opponent are
considered. Not a bad present for your 26th birthday.} 1-0

[Event "Round 1"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "2018.03.11"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Aronian, Levon"]
[Black "Ding, Liren"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "A18"]
[PlyCount "43"]
[EventDate "2018.??.??"]
[SourceDate "2018.03.11"]

1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 e6 3. e4 d5 4. e5 d4 5. exf6 dxc3 6. bxc3 Qxf6 7. d4 {
[pgndiagram] Well known position on GM level. Aronian played it as black in
2014 against Grischuk and it ended 1:0. It has many similarities with this
game.} b6 {7... e5 and c5 are the main lines here, but I guess we could write
a good book about this opening anyway} 8. h4 $5 {The idea of Garry Kasparov
from the Sinquefield Rapid and Blitz 2017. It is amazing that Gazza's ideas
are topical well after his retirement. Pushing flank pawns was always a
favourite idea of his.   This move is objectively not the strongest , but
black has to play precisely. It's clear that this position Aronian had on his
board at some point while preparing for the game since this move was played
instantly.} Bb7 9. Bg5 {The point - White intends to gain a few tempi chasing
the queen away.  It also includes a pawn sacrifice} Qf5 10. Bd3 Qa5 {Most
logical and best moves.} 11. Kf1 $1 {[pgndiagram] While this move might not be
quite obvious (especially to newer players) it's kinda logical. It's moving
king from e1 do a5 diagonal stoping the potencial check after Qc3, defending
pawn on g2 and moving the king from centre.} (11. Ne2 {Would simply lose the
g2 pawn} Bxg2) 11... Nc6 $1 {A very good decision by Ding} (11... Qxc3 {Was
incredibly risky and more importantly, Aronian certainly prepared for it.} 12.
Ne2 {And the queen is lacking good squares, for instance} Qb4 (12... Qa5 13. c5
{With the threat of Bd2} Ba6 {The only move} 14. Bd2 Qa3 15. Be4 c6 16. Rh3 {
[pgndiagram] And White has a huge lead in development}) 13. Rh3 {Taking
"worthless" pawn on c3 is not worth the time it gives white to develop. And
once again Black's position is incredibly shaky - all White pieces are
participating in the game}) 12. Rb1 {Insisting on a pawn sacrifice, but maybe
it is not required} (12. Ne2 {Was safer}) 12... f6 $1 {Kicking the bishop away
and preparing for e5} (12... Qxc3 {Now Black might have taken the pawn,
because after} 13. Ne2 Qa3 14. Rh3 h6 $1 {[pgndiagram] There is no Be4 move in
the position. Still, White does have compensation for the pawn}) (12... h6 $6 {
Is inferior} 13. Bd2 O-O-O {And White is better - f6 is definitely more useful
for Black}) 13. Bd2 O-O-O 14. a4 e5 {[pgndiagram] Critical moment in the game.}
15. c5 $1 {Maybe slightly optimistic, but typical for Aronian} (15. Rh3 {Would
enter a long and forced variation} exd4 16. cxd4 Bb4 17. d5 Bxd2 18. Rb5 Qa6 (
18... Qc3 19. Ne2) 19. dxc6 (19. Qxd2 Ne5 {Is worse}) 19... Bxc6 20. Qxd2 Qxa4
(20... Bxb5 21. axb5 Qa1+ 22. Qe1) 21. Rb1 Qxc4 22. Ne2 Be4 23. Bxc4 Rxd2 24.
Rc1 {[pgndiagram] And after all the fireworks, White remains a piece up. Still,
this endgame is far from won - three connected passed pawns are dangerous}) (
15. Ne2 {Was probably the safest way} Qa6 16. Qc2 Na5 17. c5 Nc4 {When White
retains some advantage}) 15... Bxc5 $1 {[pgndiagram] Exclamation mark is not
for this obvious capture, but for the follow up} (15... bxc5 16. Ne2 {Gives
White a strong attack - Qb3 is a huge threat} (16. Qb3 Ba6 17. Qe6+ Rd7) 16...
Be7 17. Qb3 Ba6 18. Qe6+) 16. Rb5 Qa6 17. Rh3 Bxd4 $1 {Fantastically
calculated piece sacrifice - Black is fighting for the initiative} 18. Be2 {
[pgndiagram] Best moves from both players.} (18. cxd4 Rxd4 {Gives Black plenty
of compensation for the piece} 19. Ra5 (19. Ne2 Qxa4 20. Qxa4 Rxa4 $15 {Black
has 4 pawns for a bishop. He's slightly better and it's easier to play this
position as black.}) 19... Nxa5 20. Bxa6 Bxa6+ 21. Ke1 Rhd8 $19 {[pgndiagram]
And Black, despite having only a rook and three pawns for the queen, has a
decisive initative}) 18... Rd6 $2 {a mistake} (18... Ba8 {Was the best move
here - which would put Aronian in a rather tough spot} 19. cxd4 Nxd4 20. Rb2
Qb7 {[pgndiagram] The point - Black queen can retreat to b7. The position is
double edged, but I think it is easier to play the Black pieces and all three
results are very much possible}) (18... Bxc3 {Does not work} 19. Rxc3 Nd4 20.
Rbc5 {And wins}) 19. Rb1 $2 {Aronian fails to exploit his chance. Nerves
possibly?} (19. Rb2 {Defending d2, was quite strong} Qa5 20. cxd4 Qd5 21. dxe5
Nxe5 22. Nf3 Rhd8 $16 {[pgndiagram] This was the best way to play for a win.})
19... Qa5 20. Rb5 (20. cxd4 {Is now impossible} Qd5 21. dxe5 Nxe5 {[pgndiagram]
And both d2 and g2 are hanging - Black is almost winning here}) 20... Qa6 21.
Rb1 Qa5 22. Rb5 {[pgndiagram] Game ended by repetition.} 1/2-1/2

[Event "Candidates Round Two"]
[Site "Berlin GER"]
[Date "2018.03.11"]
[Round "2"]
[White "Alexander Grischuk"]
[Black "Wesley So"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C84"]
[WhiteElo "2767"]
[BlackElo "2799"]
[PlyCount "87"]
[EventDate "2018.03.10"]
[SourceDate "2018.03.11"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. d3 {Nowadays, this is
the main move in the Ruy Lopez. White players have swiched to it in order to
avoid Marshall attack} b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Bb7 {More or less standard
Spanish position has arisen} 10. Nbd2 Re8 {[pgndiagram] I thought Black should
play this only after Ng5 has been ruled out} (10... h6 {Is more typical}) 11.
Ng5 Rf8 {The drawback of the previous move. I guess Black argues he will gain
a tempo on h6} (11... d5 {I one of Ivanchuk's masterclasses, I have seen him
talking about his game about Vugar Gashimov. He mentioned Ng5 is often met by
d5, but here it doesn't work (it didn't work for Ivanchuk as well)} 12. exd5
Nxd5 13. Nxf7 Kxf7 14. Qf3+ {[pgndiagram] And Black is busted} Ke6 15. c4 {And
here the only move is} (15. a4 {Simply increasing the pressure, might be even
stronger}) 15... Nd4 16. cxd5+ Bxd5 17. Qh5 {[pgndiagram] But Black can't
survive with a king like that}) 12. Re1 (12. f4 {Another thing I thought about
Ng5 is that it can be followed up by f4. Alas, it seems I was wrong} exf4 13.
Rxf4 h6 {This is the problem. White can't take with the bishop on f4, so his
rook will be sidelined} 14. Ngf3 g5) 12... d5 {When I was streaming, I was
critical of this move, and it seems not without reason. So opens the positions
too late and gains the inferior version of the open Italian} (12... h6 {Was
more consistent} 13. Ngf3 Na5 14. Bc2 c5 {[pgndiagram] And Black has a normal
position}) 13. exd5 Nxd5 14. Ndf3 $1 {[pgndiagram] Grischuk didn't like this
move, but the computer approves of it} Qd7 $6 (14... h6 {Was once again more
consistent. After} 15. Ne4 Nf6 16. Nxf6+ Bxf6 17. Be3 {[pgndiagram] Black has
inferior, but defensible position}) 15. d4 $1 {Another strong move, striking
in the center instantly} (15. Nxe5 {Is of course stupid due to} Nxe5 16. Rxe5
f6) (15. a4 {Was interesting, but after} f6 16. Ne4 Na5 {White loses part of
his advantage}) 15... exd4 16. cxd4 $6 {The first slight inaccuracy by
Grischuk.} (16. Qd3 {Gaining a tempo first, was possibly more accurate. The
play continues} Nf6 17. cxd4 Nb4 18. Qb1 $1 {[pgndiagram] Possibli this idea
was missed by Grischuk. White still has the Nxf7 threat}) 16... h6 {Now Black
managets to get this move and is able to offer some exchanges} 17. Ne4 Rfe8 $2
(17... Nf6 {Was possible, but So was probably worried about the d5 advance} 18.
d5 Na5 19. Ne5 {And Black is also in trouble}) (17... Rad8 {Not removing the
defender of the f7 square, was probably the most precise move in the position})
18. Bd2 $5 {Simply developing and anticipating Black's next move} Nf6 19. Rc1
$1 {[pgndiagram] Strong play by Grischuk. The rook joins the game with almost
decisive effect. Just look at the subsequent pendulum via c5} Nxe4 {There is
nothing else} (19... Nxd4 20. Bxf7+) 20. Rxe4 Bf6 21. Rg4 $1 {[pgndiagram] One
of the best Grischuk games I have seen. Suddenly White has threats on the
kingside} Kh8 (21... h5 22. Rf4 {And White is threatening an exchange
sacrifice on f6, and f7 pawn. Say after} Rad8 23. Ng5 {He is already winning} (
23. Rc5 {Is also strong})) (21... Rad8 {Trying to get the d4 pawn, was
possible. After} 22. Bxh6 Nxd4 23. Nxd4 Bxd4 24. Qd2 {[pgndiagram] White has
the better chances, but Black is alive and kicking}) 22. Rc5 $1 {Creating the
idea of Rh5 with decisive threats} Rad8 $2 {The final mistake, but it is
already hard to offer the alternative} (22... Ne7 {Is the computer defence -
trying to get the knight to the defence of the king and creating the threat of
taking on f3. It could have made White's life more difficult} 23. Qc1 $1 {All
the same} Bxf3 24. gxf3 Nf5 25. Bxh6 $1 {[pgndiagram] Still works, but it is
much harder to calculate} gxh6 26. Rd5 $1 {Inducing the queen to go to e6
first!! Amazing geometry} (26. Rxf5 Re1+) 26... Qe6 (26... Qc8 27. Qf4) 27.
Rxf5 (27. Qf4 $4 {It is never too late to lose a game of chess} Qe1+ 28. Kg2
Re2 $1)) 23. Qc1 $1 {[pgndiagram] This is decisive} Nxd4 24. Nxd4 Re4 (24...
Bxd4 25. Bxh6 {And wins}) (24... Be4 25. Bxh6 Qxd4 26. Be3 {And wins}) 25. Rxc7
Qxd4 26. Be3 {[pgndiagram]} Rxg4 27. hxg4 Qe4 28. f3 {[pgndiagram] The only
move, but sufficient. The rest is, as they say, matter of technique} Qb4 29.
Rxb7 Bxb2 30. Qf1 f6 31. Qf2 Be5 32. f4 Bd6 33. g5 Qe4 34. Qf3 Qb1+ 35. Qf1 Qe4
36. Qf3 Qb1+ 37. Kf2 Bb4 38. Qe2 Qe4 39. Qf3 Qb1 40. Kg3 fxg5 41. Kh2 Qf5 42.
Rf7 Qg6 43. fxg5 Bd6+ 44. Kh3 {[pgndiagram] A fantastic game by Grischuk and a
terrible start to the tournament by Wesley So.} 1-0

[Event "World Championship Candidates"]
[Site "0:32:33-0:18:33"]
[Date "2018.03.12"]
[Round "3"]
[White "Levon Aronian"]
[Black "Vladimir Kramnik"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C65"]
[PlyCount "62"]
[EventDate "2018.03.10"]
[SourceDate "2018.03.11"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 Bc5 5. Bxc6 dxc6 6. O-O Qe7 7. h3 {
[pgndiagram] There is no doubt that Levon expected the quiet Spanish game.} (7.
Nbd2 {Had he known what awaits him, he would have possible waited a bit} Rg8 {
Now doesn't quite work} 8. Nc4 Bd6 9. Be3 Nh5 10. Nfxe5) 7... Rg8 $1 {
[pgndiagram] A strong strong move from Kramnik. It seems that the addition of
Anish Giri in his team has made him even more lethal in the opening. This move
is extremely hard to meet over the board and it virtually "refutes" the whole
7 h3 system} 8. Kh1 {Aronian tries to play it cautiously} (8. Nbd2 {Is now too
late} g5 9. Nc4 g4 {And Black has a strong attack}) 8... Nh5 (8... h6 {My
engine prefers to start with h6, but please stop me from being pretentious -
Kramnik must have known what he is doing}) 9. c3 {Consistent in a way -
meeting the flank attack with a central breakthrough. Still, White is too slow}
(9. Bg5 {Is what engine is insisting on} f6 10. Bd2 {And now Black can't go g5
immediately due to Nxe5} Qf7 {He might prepare it first, but still} (10... g5
11. Nxe5) 11. Nc3 g5 {[pgndiagram] And Black has a strong attack and a
position that is easy to play}) 9... g5 10. Nxe5 $1 {[pgndiagram] The point of
Aronian's play, but this is met with a strong riposte} (10. d4 exd4 11. cxd4
Bb6 {Is highly problematic for White - Black's attack is coming}) 10... g4 $3 {
A fantastic move - Black has strong mating threats and White (already) has to
find the only moves to survive} 11. d4 (11. hxg4 {White can't really take on g4
} Qh4+ 12. Kg1 Ng3 {With checkmate on the next move}) (11. Nxg4 Bxg4 12. hxg4
Qh4+ 13. Kg1 Ng3) 11... Bd6 12. g3 {Once again the only move} (12. Nxg4 Bxg4
13. hxg4 Qh4+ 14. Kg1 Qh2#) 12... Bxe5 13. dxe5 Qxe5 {[pgndiagram] Black has
regained his pawn and still has attacking chances} 14. Qd4 {The only move,
defending the e4 pawn. White would very much like to exchange queens here} Qe7
{I don't care about the engine suggestions, but only a madman would exchange
the queens here} (14... Qxd4 15. cxd4 gxh3 {Is the engine suggestion, but it
would be a great relief for White to reach this position}) 15. h4 (15. Be3 {
Was possibly more resistant, preventing c5 and trying to develop. After} gxh3
16. Nd2 Be6 {[pgndiagram] White might have chances to survive}) 15... c5 $1 {
Dislodgind the queen} 16. Qc4 {White pins the f pawn and prevents black from
putting the bishop on the long diagonal} (16. Qe3 {Is met strongly by} b6 {And
e4 will fall}) (16. Qd3 Bd7 {And the bishop comes to c6} 17. Bg5 f6) 16... Be6
$1 (16... Bd7 {Now didn't work due to} 17. Bg5 {And the f pawn can't move}) 17.
Qb5+ $6 {The start of the wrong plan. White wants to block the long diagonal,
but he misses Black's other response} (17. Qd3 Bd7 {And Black will get the
bishop to c6 anyway, though}) 17... c6 18. Qa4 $2 {[pgndiagram] Putting the
queen too far away} ({Preventing f5 for the moments, was nevertheless better.
After} 18. Qd3 Rd8 19. Qe3 Bc4 (19... f5 {Now doesn't work} 20. exf5 Bd5+) 20.
Re1 Rd3 {[pgndiagram] Black is winning}) 18... f5 $3 {A deadly move that costs
White the game} 19. Bg5 (19. exf5 Nxg3+ {Is the point of Black's play} 20. fxg3
Bd5+ 21. Kg1 Qe2 {[pgndiagram] And Black wins} 22. Rf2 Qe1+) 19... Rxg5 $3 {
[pgndiagram] One sacrifice after another. My friend agadmator christened this
as "The Immortal Berlin game" and he may well be right} 20. hxg5 f4 {Black
simply has a decisive attack} 21. Qd1 Rd8 22. Qc1 fxg3 23. Na3 Rd3 24. Rd1 Bd5
{[pgndiagram] And this is simply a flashy finish} 25. f3 gxf3 26. exd5 Qe2 27.
Re1 g2+ {[pgndiagram] And here Levon resigned, as after} 28. Kg1 f2+ 29. Kh2
g1=Q+ 30. Rxg1 f1=Q+ 31. Kh1 Qeg2# {[pgndiagram] He gets checkmated in a
spectacular fashion. What a game by Vladimir Kramnik!!} 0-1

[Event "Candidates Round 5"]
[Site "0:57:33-0:09:33"]
