1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 {This wasn't expected. As far as I know Grischuk is not a
Gr�nfeld player. But did he really want to go for the King's Indian?} 3. f3 {
Aronian doesn't ask that question and goes for early f3.} c5 (3... d5 {Would
go to Gr�nfeld}) (3... Bg7 {Would lead to King's Indian S�misch}) 4. d5 d6 5.
e4 {So in the end, some sort of Benoni with f3 has arisen} e6 {[pgndiagram]
And after this, we will definitely have a Benoni} 6. Nc3 exd5 7. cxd5 Bg7 8.
Nge2 Nbd7 {[pgndiagram] A logical move, but also the first novelty of the game.
} (8... a6) (8... O-O) (8... Nh5 {Have all been played before}) 9. Ng3 h5 {
Typical idea when White plays Ng3} 10. Be2 Nh7 {Not sure how deeply Grischuk
prepared, but this look seemingly violated all opening principles, moving the
same piece twice, etc. However, there is a concrete idea behind it - Black
prevents Bg5} (10... O-O 11. Bg5) (10... h4 11. Nf1 Nh5 {With a similar idea,
seems more consistent, but Grischuk arguably looked at this line before. For
instance the computer suggest} 12. g4 {Which might be very strong}) 11. Bf4 (
11. O-O {Seems more natural, but it loses material} Bd4+ $1 {And now White has
to give up the exchange since} 12. Kh1 h4 {And there is no typical Nh1 move.
White is lost}) 11... Qe7 12. Qd2 h4 13. Nf1 {[pgndiagram] Ideally, White
would want to send this knight on the h1-f2-d3/g4 route, but Grischuk's
cunning Nh7 has prevented him from carrying it out} g5 14. Be3 Ne5 {Great play
by Black so far - now he has secured the e5 for the knight as well. He can
consider f5 in the future} 15. g3 $5 {Immediately breaking the structure} Bd7
$2 (15... g4 $1 {Pointed out by computer, is more energetic, but it is not
clear whether it is better than the move in the game} 16. f4 Nf3+ 17. Bxf3 gxf3
18. e5 dxe5 19. d6 Qd7 20. fxe5 Bxe5 21. O-O-O {[pgndiagram] And the position
is incredibly complex, but Black has some difficulties with development, so I
guess White's chances are preferable.  If you are Alpha Zero}) 16. gxh4 {Now,
suddenly, White createst a strong initiative} gxh4 $2 (16... g4 {Is again
computer's suggestion, but it seems it was better to play it on the previous
move, if Black wanted to}) 17. Rg1 f5 18. f4 $1 {[pgndiagram] Amazingly, it
would appear Black is already close to being lost. Such is the price of a
wrong move in a Benoni} Ng4 19. e5 dxe5 20. d6 Qe6 {Preventing Nd5, but
allowing Nb5} (20... Qf7 {Would be met by} 21. Nd5 exf4 22. Bxf4 {And Black is
clearly worse} (22. Nc7+ {Might be even stronger})) 21. Nb5 $1 {Exploiting the
fact that the queen is on e6} (21. Nd5 {Would be now met by} Qxd6) 21... Rc8
22. Nc7+ Rxc7 23. dxc7 $1 {[pgndiagram] Now Aronian should be winning, but
amazing things start to happen} exf4 24. Rd1 (24. O-O-O {Was mentioned as the
best move, but it is really hard to tell the difference - the lines are
incredibly complicated} fxe3 25. Nxe3 (25. Qxd7+ {Now, this doesn't work, as}
Qxd7 26. Bb5 Bxb2+ $3 {Turn the tables}) 25... Bh6 26. Bb5 $3 {[pgndiagram] A
key move White had to envision - Black is simply busted. Say after} Bxb5 (26...
Bxe3 27. c8=Q+ Kf7 28. Qxh8 Bxd2+ 29. Rxd2 Bxb5 30. Qxh7+ {[pgndiagram] Is
also winning for White}) 27. Qd8+ Kf7 28. Qxh8) (24. Bxf4 {Would also probably
retain the advantage - sometimes playing prosaically is quite appropriate.})
24... Ng5 $1 {An amazing resource, that works in Black's favour - all because
the king is on e1} (24... fxe3 25. Qxd7+ Qxd7 26. Bb5 {And now the bishop
can't take on b2}) 25. c8=Q+ Bxc8 26. Qd8+ Kf7 27. Qc7+ $2 {[pgndiagram]
Aronian has clearly lost the thread of the game, and makes a move that for the
first time, changes the evaluation in Black's favour} (27. Qxg5 {What can be
more natural than taking the piece?} fxe3 28. Rxg4 $1 {A key move that leads
to a draw} (28. Bxg4 fxg4 29. Qf4+ Ke8 {Is not so clear cut}) 28... fxg4 29.
Qf4+ {[pgndiagram]And despite being three pawns up, Black can't avoid the
perpetual} Ke7 (29... Ke8 30. Bb5+ {Would even lose} Ke7 31. Qc7+) ({Or} 29...
Qf6 30. Bc4+ Ke8 31. Bb5+ Kf7) 30. Qg5+ Kf7 31. Qf4+) 27... Kg8 $2 {An
inexplicable blunder, that might have cost Grischuk the game} (27... Qe7 28.
Bc4+ Kf6 29. Qxe7+ Kxe7 30. Bxc5+ Ke8 {[pgndiagram] And only Black can play
this position for the win}) 28. Rd6 $1 {[pgndiagram]Now Black loses the bishop
on c8 - or does he?} (28. Rxg4 {According to Aronian, was easier win - and he
might well be right} fxg4 29. Rd6 Qf5 30. Bd3 {[pgndiagram] And wins}) 28...
Qf7 29. Qd8+ $4 {The most inexplicable move in the game at first glance.
However, at the press conference, it was explained that White can win only in
study like fashion} (29. Qxc8+ Kh7 30. Qxc5 Ne4 {And here Aronian couldn't
find a move Peter Svidler found in his live analysis of the game. Are you
ready?} 31. Rxg4 $3 {Simply magical} Nxc5 32. Rxh4+ Kg8 33. Rd8+ Bf8 34. Rxh8+
Kxh8 35. Bxc5 {[pgndiagram] And Black wins}) 29... Qf8 30. Bxf4 (30. Qxg5 {Was
still possible} Qxd6 31. Bc4+ Kh7 32. Rxg4 fxg4 33. Qh5+ Bh6 34. Qf7+ Bg7 {
[pgndiagram] And things end in a perpetual check}) 30... Ne6 {[pgndiagram] And
now suddenly Black is very much in the game} 31. Bc4 {Forced} Qxd8 32. Rxd8+
Kh7 33. Rxh8+ Bxh8 34. Bd6 Ng5 35. Rg2 Ne4 36. Bb8 Bd4 37. h3 Ne5 38. Bd5 Nd3+
39. Ke2 {[pgndiagram] And in this exciting position, player unfortunately went
for the three-fold repetition. In the chessbrah live stream, Yasser Seirawan
repeatedly stated how regretable that is - he was rather curious whether Black
would go for the b2 or h3 pawn} Nc1+ 40. Kd1 Nd3 41. Nd2 Nf6 42. Bf3 {
[pgndiagram] And in this exciting position, players mentaly agreed to a draw
unfortunately went for the three-fold repetition. In the chessbrah live stream,
Yasser Seirawan repeatedly stated how regretable that is - he was rather
curious whether Black would go for the b2 or h3 pawn A fantastic, mind
boggling encounter} 1/2-1/2

[Event "Round 4"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "2018.03.14"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Grischuk, Alexander"]
[Black "Ding, Liren"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "D43"]
