1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 c6 5. Bg5 h6 6. Bh4 {Allowing the
Anti-Moscow gambit. Grischuk once said he is addicted to this gambit.} dxc4 7.
e4 g5 8. Bg3 b5 9. Be2 Bb7 10. O-O Nbd7 11. Ne5 Bg7 12. Nxf7 {[pgndiagram]
This has been played in a historic encounter Topalov - Kramnik, Corus, 2008,
in which Bulgarian star won the game. In the interview right after the game,
Grischuk said: " Nf7 is an idea of Alpha 0, stockfish says it's very stupid.
Black is in big danger here. "} Kxf7 13. e5 Nd5 14. Ne4 Qb6 15. Nd6+ Ke7 16. a4
(16. Bg4 {Has been played by Kramnik, but since then, remedies have been found}
) 16... Raf8 17. Bf3 a6 18. Bxd5 cxd5 19. axb5 axb5 20. Kh1 Bc6 {[pgndiagram]
Up to this moment, Grischuk was still in his preparation. He expected Ding to
play Ba6 following with Qc2.} (20... Ba6 {Might be indeed better. After} 21. f4
b4 {White has a number of ideas - fxg5 or Qe2}) 21. f4 $5 {A strong move} gxf4
$2 {[pgndiagram]  Already a decisive mistake. This is the cricital moment in
the game. Grischuk can win on spot.} (21... h5 {Was interesting, but White has
the strong} 22. Be1 $1 (22. f5 h4 23. Be1 b4 {Is the wrong move order} 24. Qc2
(24. f6+ Nxf6 25. exf6+ Bxf6) 24... Bxe5) 22... b4 23. Qc2 {[pgndiagram] And
Black can't take the d4 pawn. Despite being a full piece up, his position is
hard to play}) 22. Rxf4 $2 {Grischuk returns the favour} (22. Bh4+ {Was now
simply winning. The lines are long, but forced, and we will reproduce them
without further comments} Nf6 (22... Bf6 23. Qg4 Qxd4 (23... Rhg8 24. exf6+
Kxd6 25. Qxf4+ e5 26. dxe5+ Nxe5 (26... Kc7 27. e6+ Kb7 28. f7 Rg7 29. e7) 27.
Rfe1 Re8 28. f7) 24. Bxf6+ Nxf6 25. Qg7+ Kd8 26. exf6 Qxf6 27. Nb7+ {
[pgndiagram] While calculating 21...g:f4, Ding missed Nb7, after which he can
only resign since mate is unstoppable} Bxb7 28. Qxb7) 23. Qg4) 22... Rxf4 23.
Bxf4 Kd8 (23... Rf8 {It's a bit more precise to attack white's bishop first.}
24. Bg3 (24. Qg4 {Is now not possible due to} Qxd4 25. Qxg7+ Kd8 26. Bxh6 Qxb2
{[pgndiagram] And Black is much better}) 24... Kd8 25. Qg4 Kc7 {[pgndiagram]
And Black is doing fine}) 24. Qg4 Rf8 25. Bd2 Kc7 26. h3 (26. Ba5 Qxa5 27. Rxa5
Rf1#) (26. Ne8+ {Was possible, diverting the rook} Rxe8 27. Ba5 Qxa5 28. Rxa5 {
[pgndiagram] But Black has three pieces for the queen and his position is
easier to play}) 26... b4 27. Qxg7 Qxd4 28. Bxb4 Qxb2 29. Ba5+ Kb8 {
[pgndiagram] Here Grischuk said he though he would be able to deal with the g2
pawn} 30. Rg1 $1 {A very precise move - defending g2} (30. Re1 {Would be losing
} c3 31. Qe7 d4 {And White has to lose another tempo which proves to be crucial
} 32. Rg1 Qa2) 30... c3 (30... Qxe5 {Is not to be recommended} 31. Rb1+ Ka7 32.
Qxe5 Nxe5 33. Bc7 {[pgndiagram] And Black's king is in trouble}) 31. Qe7 c2 32.
Bd2 Qxe5 33. Bxh6 Rg8 34. Nf7 Qc3 (34... Qf6 {[pgndiagram] After many great
moves from both sides, this is first inperfect in a while. Qf6 forces white to
exchange queens and to go in worse endgame.} 35. Qb4+ Kc8 36. Qd6 e5 37. Qxf6
Nxf6 38. Nxe5 {[pgndiagram] Although it is doubtful how series Black's chances
are}) 35. Qd6+ Kb7 36. Qxe6 d4 {[pgndiagram] The players find the correct
moves until the end and the draw is the most fair result} 37. Nd6+ Kb6 38. Ne4
Nc5 39. Qxg8 Nxe4 40. Kh2 d3 41. Be3+ Kb5 42. Qb8+ Kc4 43. Qc7 Qf6 44. Rf1 Qd6+
45. Qxd6 Nxd6 46. Rf6 Kd5 47. Rxd6+ Kxd6 48. Kg3 Kd5 49. Kf2 Kc4 50. Bd2 Kb3
51. Ke3 Bxg2 {[pgndiagram] A mind-boggling encounter. Great game played really
well by both players!} 1/2-1/2

