1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O {
[pgndiagram] In today's time most GM's think that black can easily drawn in
Marshall Attack ( So-Ding 3rd round of 2018. Candidates ). Accordingly,
strongest players are switching to 8.a4 since it's more ambitious than
Marshall for white.} 8. a4 (8. c3 d5 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. Rxe5 c6)
8... b4 (8... Bb7 {Also a possibility, thou 8...b4 is more critical this days.}
9. d3 d6 10. Nc3 Na5 11. Ba2 b4 12. Ne2 c5 13. Ng3) 9. a5 d6 10. d3 Be6 (10...
Bg4 11. Nbd2 Nd4 {Is also playable, say} 12. Ba2 c5 13. h3 Bh5) 11. Bxe6 fxe6
12. Nbd2 Rb8 13. Nb3 Qc8 {[pgndiagram] While black has clear idea of
repositioning his knight to push c5, white can play this position in more than
one way.} 14. h3 (14. Qe2 Nd8 15. d4 exd4 16. Nbxd4 c5 17. Nb3 e5 18. Nbd2 {
Caruana- Carlsen St. Louis 2017. White's plan is to reposition his knight on
c4, pressuring d6 pawn and to develop his dark square bishop.}) (14. d4 {
Immediate push in the center is probably not that critical} exd4 15. Nbxd4 Nxd4
16. Nxd4 c5 17. Nf3 {And Black should be fine}) 14... Nd8 15. Be3 c5 16. Nbd2 {
Moving the knight to c4 - the defensive duty on b3 has been fullfilled} Nc6 17.
c3 {[pgndiagram] At this point, Levon is most likely out of his preparation
and he decides to go for the most logical and natural option} Rb5 18. d4 (18.
Nc4 Nd7 {With the idea of pushing d5.}) 18... exd4 {Slight mistake by Aronian.
In general it's better to exchange as many pawns as possible in this type of
position.} (18... bxc3 19. bxc3 exd4 20. cxd4 c4 {[pgndiagram] Black threatens
to play d5} 21. e5 $5 {Entering a maelstorm of complications} (21. Nxc4 Nxe4
22. Nb6 Qe8 23. d5 Nc3 24. Qd3 Nxd5 25. Nxd5 Rxd5 26. Qxa6 Bf6 {[pgndiagram]
And although White's a-pawn looks dangerous, Black can deal with it and should
be fine. Of course, it is hard to calculate and evaluate all this during the
game}) 21... dxe5 22. Nxc4 exd4 23. Nxd4 Nxd4 24. Qxd4 Bc5 {With completey
equality}) 19. cxd4 Nxa5 {For the price of pawn, white desynchronized black's
pieces what will at the end cost Aronian the game.} 20. dxc5 dxc5 21. Ra2 {So
played this move instantly, it's safe to assume his preparation went this far.}
(21. Qc2 {White would like to exchange knights on c4 and pressure black's weak
pawns, but this is met tactically with} c4 22. Nd4 b3 23. Qd1 Bb4 24. Nxb5 axb5
$11 {After this line, black is down an exchange but his pawns are not weak
anymore and he has great d3 outpost for his nights}) 21... Qb7 22. b3 {
[pgndiagram] While objectively this position is balanced, it's much easier to
play White.} Kh8 $6 (22... Nxe4 {Is ill advised} 23. Nxe4 Qxe4 24. Bg5 Qb7 25.
Rd2 $18 {[pgndiagram] And White is close to winning} Bxg5 (25... Qa7 {Seems
like the only move}) 26. Nxg5) (22... Nd7 {Immediately might be more precise.
A loss of tempo is critical}) 23. Qc2 Nd7 24. Rea1 Bd8 25. Nc4 {All White's
pieces occupy ideal posts} Nxc4 26. Qxc4 Bf6 27. Rd1 Qc6 28. Rad2 {[pgndiagram]
White is better, despite the missing pawn. The price of one loss of tempo in a
2800 game} Nb6 (28... Ne5 29. Nxe5 Bxe5 30. f4 Bc7 31. Rd7 $16) 29. Qc2 Qc7 $2
{[pgndiagram]Unfortunately for Aronian this was in practical terms, mistake
that lost him game. It was important to leave a queen on a protected square.} (
29... Qc8 30. e5 Be7 31. Bg5 (31. Nd4 {Is now not possible, of course}) 31...
Qe8 32. Bxe7 Qxe7 33. Rd6 Nd5 $14) 30. e5 Be7 {It is still impossible to take
on e5} (30... Bxe5 31. Ng5) 31. Nd4 $5 {A strong rejoinder, winning an exchange
} Rc8 (31... Qxe5 {Offered better practical chances.} 32. Nxb5 axb5 33. Re2 Qc3
34. Qe4 Qxb3 35. Ree1 $18 {[pgndiagram] Athough even here, Black has a
difficult time holding. White has many threats, the key one being Qb7}) 32.
Nxe6 $5 {I think it is very unpleasant to deal with such a move} (32. Nxb5 {
Would have eased Black's play, although this might be the strongest}) 32...
Qxe5 {[pgndiagram]} 33. Nf4 (33. Rd6 $3 {Was an amazing possibility. Craziest
engine move i have seen in a long time.} Bxd6 (33... c4 {The only move} 34. Bd4
{Only move} Qxd6 35. Bxg7+ Kg8 36. Rxd6 Bxd6 37. Qd1 Rd5 38. Bd4 {Only move
again.} Kf7 39. Ng5+ {And again.} Rxg5 40. Bxb6 Ke7 41. Qf3 {It turns out
after this quiet move, black pieces are badly positioned. While this position
is objectively winning for white there would still be quite a lot of technique
needed to win it.}) 34. Ng5 {The point - the bishop is diverted and Black
can't defend both Nf7+ and Qxh7 mate} Qh2+ 35. Kf1 g6 {[pgndiagram] And here,
there is a good question - what next.} 36. Rxd6 Qh1+ 37. Ke2 Qa1 38. Qd2 {
[pgndiagram] And despite being an exchange down - White has a winning attack
here. Some lines go} c4 39. Rd8+ Kg7 (39... Rxd8 40. Qxd8+ Kg7 41. Qe7+ Kg8 42.
Qxh7+ Kf8 43. Qf7#) 40. Rxc8 Nxc8 41. Qd7+ Ne7 42. Qxe7+ Kh6 43. Qxh7#) 33...
Rf8 $2 {[pgndiagram] The losing mistake} (33... Kg8 {Best move. Stops many
tactics white has in this position.} 34. Re2 Bf8) (33... c4 34. bxc4 Nxc4 {Is
not possible due to} 35. Qxc4) 34. Re2 Qc3 $2 {Mistakes often come in pairs} (
34... Bf6 {Was still better , although after} 35. Qa2 Ra8 36. Bd4 Qxf4 37. Qxa6
{Tactics work in White favour}) 35. Qb1 $6 (35. Qa2 {Was stronger. Quite
unexpected that So missed this move. After this black can resign on spot.} a5 (
35... Ra8 36. Ne6 {Is incredibly strong} Ra7 37. Bf4 {Black's position is in
ruins}) 36. Bc1 Qf6 37. Re6 Qf7 38. Qe2 {[pgndiagram] And Black loses material}
) 35... Qf6 36. Bc1 c4 $2 {A final mistake in a difficult position} 37. bxc4
Nxc4 {[pgndiagram] Nd5 and Qe4 also wins.} 38. Re6 Qg5 39. Ng6+ Qxg6 40. Rxg6
hxg6 41. Qe4 {[pgndiagram] The rest is, as they say, matter of technique} Bf6 (
41... Ne5 42. f4 Bc5+ 43. Kh1 Nf7 44. Qxg6 {Clear ideas here are Bb2 and Rd5
to finish the game.}) 42. Qxc4 b3 43. Ba3 Rfb8 44. Rb1 b2 45. h4 Ra5 46. Qd3
Rd8 47. Qb3 Rc8 48. Qb7 Rd8 49. Qb3 Rc8 50. Qb4 Rb5 51. Qg4 Rc3 52. Bxb2 Rxb2
53. Rxb2 Rc1+ 54. Kh2 Bxb2 55. Qxg6 {[pgndiagram] Unfortunately for black weak
king, awkwardly placed pieces and 3 white pawns are too much for him to handle.
} Ra1 56. g4 a5 57. Qh5+ Kg8 58. Qb5 Ba3 59. Qe5 Rd1 60. Qe6+ Kh7 61. Qe4+ Kh8
62. Qa8+ Kh7 63. Qxa5 Bd6+ 64. Kg2 Rd4 65. Qf5+ Kh8 66. Qh5+ Kg8 67. g5 Kf8 68.
Qg6 Be7 69. Qf5+ Ke8 70. Kh3 Rd6 71. Qh7 Kf7 72. f4 Rd4 73. Qf5+ Ke8 74. Qe5
Rb4 75. Kg4 Kf8 76. Qf5+ Ke8 77. Qe6 Rd4 78. Qe5 {Amazing game by So ! From
opening preparation to endgame technique. For Aronian, on the other hand, this
game probably means the end of his hopes in this tournament.} *

