1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 dxc4 5. e4 Bb4 {A brave opening choice by
Caruana and correct psychological decision - playing for the win against a
player who is out of form in this tournament} 6. Bg5 h6 {[pgndiagram] Playable,
but risky} (6... c5 {Is the main line}) 7. Bxf6 Qxf6 8. Bxc4 c5 9. O-O cxd4 10.
e5 Qd8 11. Ne4 {Levon is also playing ambitiously} (11. Nxd4 O-O 12. Rc1 Bd7 {
Is safer for White, although Black's position is quite solid}) 11... O-O 12.
Qe2 Be7 13. Rad1 Qc7 14. Bd3 {Indicating he is only interested in attack} (14.
Rxd4 Nc6 15. Rd2 Nxe5) (14. Ng3 {With the intention of taking on d4, or with
some Nh5 ideas, might be playable}) 14... Nd7 (14... Nc6 15. Ng3 {Is inferior})
15. Rc1 $6 {[pgndiagram] Probably too risky} (15. Ng3 Nc5 {The point of
development to d7} 16. Nxd4 Nxd3 17. Rxd3 {With a level game.}) 15... Qa5 $1 {
Now White loses another pawn} 16. g4 $2 {But this is simply not justified} (16.
Ned2 Nc5 $1 (16... Qxa2 17. Qe4 g6 18. Qxd4 {Might be better for White}) 17.
Bb1 d3 18. Qe3 Rd8 {With a complex, double-edged position}) 16... Nxe5 {Now
Black simply picks up another pawn} 17. Nxe5 Qxe5 18. f4 Qa5 19. g5 {
[pgndiagram] Of course, Caruana has to be careful - White's attack is not
without venom. But if Black defends correctly, he can deal with it without
problems} Qd8 $2 {An inaccuracy} (19... g6 {Immediately was maybe more precise}
20. gxh6 (20. h4 Bd7 (20... h5 $5) 21. gxh6 Rac8 {And Black is now fully
developed}) 20... Kh7 {And h4 is not possible}) 20. h4 Bd7 21. gxh6 g6 (21...
Bxh4 22. hxg7 {Is just losing}) 22. h5 {[pgndiagram] Now White manageds to
break the structure with the help of this move} Kh8 (22... Kh7 {Is now worse}
23. Qg2 Rc8 24. Nf6+ Bxf6 25. hxg6+) 23. Kh2 $2 (23. Qg4 {Or}) (23. hxg6 {Was
enough to maintain the balance}) 23... Bc6 24. Rf3 $2 {[pgndiagram] The losing
mistake - White simply wants too much out of position} (24. hxg6 fxg6 25. Qg4
Bxe4 26. Bxe4 Rf6 {And Black would retain some advantage, but opposite
coloured bishops and Black's weak king give White some hope}) (24. Rf2 Qd5)
24... Bd6 $6 {Once again not the best} (24... Qd5 {Was the strongest, bringing
the queen to the 5th file}) 25. Qf2 $6 (25. Nxd6 {It was better to get rid of
this bishop} Qxd6 (25... Bxf3 26. Qe5+ f6 27. Qxe6 {But I guess it is very
hard to evaluate the consequences here - White is fine})) 25... Bc7 26. Kh3 (
26. hxg6 fxg6 {Is now not ideal}) (26. Qg3 {Would force Black to find the
accurate} Qe7 27. hxg6 (27. Rf2 {Keeps the game going}) 27... fxg6 {And now
White can't capture on g6 due to} 28. Qxg6 Bxf4+) 26... Qe7 27. Ng5 $2 {
[pgndiagram] The final mistake} (27. Rg1 {Would maintain the balance} e5 (27...
Rg8 {Might be better, retaining some chances}) 28. f5 g5 29. f6 Qe6+ 30. Rf5 {
And White is fine}) 27... e5 $6 (27... gxh5 {Would win on the spot - White has
no attack} 28. Qxd4+ e5) 28. Rxc6 bxc6 29. Nxf7+ Rxf7 30. hxg6 {A great
conception by Aronian - offering some practical chances} Rf6 31. g7+ Kg8 32.
Bc4+ $2 {[pgndiagram] A pity, after displaying some inventiveness and
creativity, Aroian violates the "patzer sees a check, patzer gives a check"
principle} (32. Qh4 {Immediately was stronger} e4 {Is now met with} (32... exf4
33. Bc4+ Kh7 34. Bd3+) 33. h7+ Kxg7 34. Rg3+ Kh8 35. Rg8+ Rxg8 36. hxg8=Q+ Kxg8
37. Bc4+ Kg7 38. Qg5+ Kh7 39. Qg8+ {[pgndiagram] And things end with a
perpetual check}) 32... Kh7 33. Qh4 {Now White has nothing and can easily
resign} e4 34. Rg3 Bxf4 35. g8=Q+ Rxg8 36. Bxg8+ Kh8 37. Rg7 Qf8 {[pgndiagram]
A wonderful defensive effort by Caruana and another Candidates game in which
Aronian's nerves failed to cope with the tension.} *

