1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. g3 {The Catalan is slowly becoming the main opening of
this tournament} Bb4+ 4. Bd2 Be7 5. Bg2 d5 6. Nf3 O-O 7. O-O c6 8. Qb3 {
[pgndiagram] Caruana already played this against Wesley So in early rounds} b6
{So preferred Nbd7} 9. Nc3 Ba6 {The line chosen by Ding is less ambitious than
So's play, but also more solid - White is forced to take on d5} 10. cxd5 cxd5
11. Ne5 Bb7 {Covering the weak c6 square} (11... Nbd7 12. Nc6 {Is awkward} Qe8
13. Nb5) 12. Rfc1 Nc6 $6 {Slightly provocative, but Ding played it quickly.
Black tries to solve his opening problems directly} (12... Nfd7) (12... Nbd7 {
Are probably both playable as well}) 13. Nxd5 $1 {[pgndiagram] Of course,
Caruana plays the most testing continuation} (13. Nxc6 Bxc6 {Is just big
nothing for White}) 13... Nxd4 {Entering tactical complications} 14. Nxe7+ Qxe7
15. Qc4 Bxg2 16. Qxd4 Rfd8 $1 {A key move Black had to envision beforehand} 17.
Qf4 $1 {[pgndiagram] Yet another correct decision} (17. Qb4 Qxb4 18. Bxb4 Bb7
19. Rc7 Ba6 20. Rac1 (20. Nxf7 {Was amazingly, played before} Nd5 21. Nxd8 Nxc7
22. Nc6 Bxe2 {And the game ended in a draw - Gleizerov - Farago, Oberwart, 1996
}) 20... Nd5 21. Rxf7 Bxe2 {With equality} (21... Nxb4 22. Rcc7 {Is of course
losing})) 17... Bb7 18. Bb4 Qe8 19. Rc7 Nd5 20. Qxf7+ Qxf7 21. Rxf7 Ba6 22. Ba3
Bxe2 23. Rc1 {[pgndiagram] The forced variation that started with 12 .. Nc6
ends here. White is slightly better, but more importantly, he can play on
without any risk. Black's weak e6 pawn and White's active pieces are slightly
problematic} Bh5 (23... h6 {Was arguably more prudent - it is not clear what
White's next move is}) 24. Rb7 {The rook is annoying here} Be8 25. Kg2 a5 26.
h4 Rdb8 27. Rxb8 Rxb8 28. g4 {[pgndiagram] Caruana imrpoves his position
little by little} b5 29. b3 b4 {This seems so natural, but it might actually
be stronger to wait with the queenside expansion} (29... h5 30. f3 Ra8 31. Bb2
a4 {Might be the right way to go}) 30. Bb2 a4 31. h5 {Now White gains even
more space on the kingside and restricts Black's bishop} Ra8 32. Kg3 Kf8 ({
Black can't actually do anything along the a-file since} 32... axb3 33. axb3
Ra2 34. Rc8 {Is actually losing} Kf8 35. Bd4) 33. Nf3 Bd7 34. Rc4 Kg8 35. Ne5
Bb5 36. Rc5 Be8 37. Nc4 Bd7 38. Ne5 Be8 39. Bd4 Kf8 40. f3 {[pgndiagram] The
position is still highly unpleasant for Ding. White has more active pieces,
better structure and the pressure is mounting.} Ke7 41. Kh4 {Intending to
provoke h6 in the future, though it is not clear whether White should do that}
(41. Nd3 {White would like to get his knight to c5 somehow, but he can't do so
directly} g6 42. hxg6 Bxg6) (41. Nc4 {Might be more subtle} g6 42. hxg6 Bxg6
43. Rb5 axb3 44. axb3 Bc2 45. Rb7+ {And White has serious advantage now} Kd8
46. Nd6 Bxb3 47. g5) 41... Kf8 42. Kg5 h6+ 43. Kh4 Kg8 44. Kg3 Kh7 {The Black
king feels safe on h7} 45. Nd3 Kg8 46. Rc1 axb3 47. axb3 Kh7 48. Nc5 $1 {
[pgndiagram] Still, Caruana has definitely made some progress and now his
knight is a beast. The next stage is to push the kingside pawns even further
and to try reaching either g7 or e6 pawns} Bf7 49. Re1 Ne7 50. Bb2 (50. Nxe6 {
Would be maybe too hasty} Re8 51. Bc5 Nc6 52. Nf8+ Kg8 53. Rxe8 Bxe8 {And it
is unclear whether White's extra pawn is enough}) 50... Nc6 (50... Ra2 $5 {
Comes to the same} 51. Nd3 (51. Re2 Nc6 52. f4 {Also maintains the pressure}))
51. f4 {Maybe it was better to wait with this move} Ra2 52. Nd3 $6 {
[pgndiagram] And here, it was arguably wiser to avoid e5 alltogether} (52. Re2)
(52. Ba1) 52... e5 $5 {This trade seems favourable for Black - he gets rid of
one weakness and creates a passed pawn of his own} 53. fxe5 Bxb3 54. e6 Ra7 $2
{[pgndiagram] Amazingly enough, this might be the losing move} (54... Ne7 {
Blocking the pawn with the knight and maintaining the active rook, seems better
} 55. Rc1 $5 {A tricky try} Bxe6 $1 56. Rc7 Rxb2 57. Nxb2 Nd5 {[pgndiagram]
And Black shouldn't lose this position}) (54... Bc4 {Is probably also enough
for a draw} 55. e7 Nxe7 56. Rxe7 Bxd3 57. Rxg7+ Kh8 58. Bd4 b3 59. Rb7+ Kg8 60.
Rxb3 {[pgndiagram] And Black probably shouldn't lose this position, although
Whtie can try}) 55. Nc5 $1 {Now Black is in dire straits once again} Bc4 (55...
Ba2 {GM Bojkov claims this was the only defence} 56. Nd7 Ra8 57. Rf1 Nd8 (57...
Bxe6 {Loses to} 58. Nf8+ Rxf8 59. Rxf8 {And with the bishop targeting the g7
pawn, Black is unable to hold, in contrast to the previously examined position
where he had a knight (see note to Black's 54 move)}) 58. e7 Nf7 {[pgndiagram]
Here he stops his analysis, but let's continue it further} 59. Ne5 $1 Kg8 60.
Ra1 $1 {A key move, with the threat of Rxa2, forcing b3 and blockade of the
bishop} b3 (60... Bd5 61. Rxa8+ Bxa8 62. e8=Q+) 61. Rd1 Re8 62. Nc6 {With the
threat of Rd8} Ng5 63. Kf4 $1 Kf7 64. Kf5 {And Black is losing. The threats
are Rd8 and Bxg7} Ne6 65. Ne5+ Kg8 (65... Kxe7 66. Ba3+ {Is the beautiful point
}) 66. Kxe6) 56. Nd7 Bb5 57. Nf8+ $2 {[pgndiagram] Missing the win} (57. Re3 {
Is a very hard move to find. The idea is to transfer the rook to the f-file}
Bc4 58. Rf3 Bb5 59. Nf8+ Kg8 60. Ng6 {[pgndiagram] And White will win}) 57...
Kg8 58. Ng6 Ne7 $2 {Returning the favour} (58... Ra8 {Preventing all the
backrank ideas, was sufficient.} 59. Rd1 Rd8 {[pgndiagram] And Black is in
time to hold this position}) 59. Ra1 $1 {[pgndiagram] Ding said he missed this
and that he was lucky to have Nc6 in the position} Nc6 (59... Rxa1 60. Nxe7+
Kf8 61. Ng6+) 60. Rd1 {The point - White has gained time to transfer the rook
to the d-file} Kh7 61. Rd5 Ba4 (61... Bc4 62. Rc5) 62. Kf4 $2 {[pgndiagram]
The final miss of the game} (62. Rd2 $3 {Caruana missed this fantastic move,
leading to a study-like win} Bb5 (62... Bb3 63. Rf2 Bxe6 64. Nf8+ Kg8 65. Nxe6
{Is a familiar idea}) (62... Ne7 63. Nf8+ Kh8 64. Rd8 {Also wins}) 63. Rf2 Bd3
64. Nf8+ Kg8 65. Nd7 Bc4 66. Rf8+ Kh7 67. Re8 {[pgndiagram] And the knight
will return to g6}) (62. Rd3 Bc2 {Is not as effective}) 62... Bc2 63. Rc5 Ba4
64. g5 hxg5+ 65. Rxg5 Nd8 66. Re5 Be8 67. e7 {Thus, Ding managed to retain his
'perfect' score, but in this game he came very close to losing. Caruana has
been incredibly consistent throughout the event, but in the next round he
faces Mamedyarov with Black and he might regret dearly the chances he missed
in this one} 1/2-1/2

[Event "World Championship Candidates"]
[Site "Berlin GER"]
[Date "2018.03.19"]
[Round "8.2"]
[White "Alexander Grischuk"]
[Black "Vladimir Kramnik"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D26"]
[WhiteElo "2767"]
[BlackElo "2800"]
[PlyCount "181"]
[EventDate "2018.03.10"]
[SourceDate "2018.03.11"]

1. c4 e6 2. Nc3 d5 3. d4 Nf6 4. Nf3 c5 5. e3 dxc4 6. Bxc4 a6 {A fairly
standard position of the Queen's Gambit accepted has arisen} 7. Bb3 (7. a4 {
Was played in the past automatically, but ever since, White players have
switched to the preemptive retreat of the bishop - it doesn't weaken the b4
square}) (7. e4 cxd4 8. Nxd4 {And here Black doesn't go b5, as in the game,
but rather} Bd6 {Preventing e5}) 7... b5 8. e4 {[pgndiagram] The point of the
move order} cxd4 9. Nxd4 Bb7 {Slightly provocative. If allowed, Black would
like to continue with Nc6} (9... Nbd7 {Is safer.}) 10. e5 $1 {Of course} Ne4 $6
{But this looks counterintuitive. It is not easy to criticize the play of
Vladimir Kramnik in the opening, but still this is not principled} (10... Nfd7
11. O-O {Seems more logical} Nc6 (11... Nxe5 12. Bxe6 {Is very dangerous} fxe6
13. Qh5+ Ng6 14. Nxe6) 12. Nxc6 (12. Bxe6 {Now doesn't work} Nxd4) 12... Bxc6
13. Bf4 Nc5 14. Bc2 Qxd1 15. Rfxd1 Be7 {[pgndiagram] With merely slightly
inferior endgame}) 11. O-O $6 (11. Nxe4 $1 {Might be the best. After} Bxe4 12.
O-O Nc6 13. Nxc6 Bxc6 14. Be3 {White obviously has much better version of the
endgame} Qxd1 15. Rfxd1 Be7 16. Rac1 Rc8 17. f4 {[pgndiagram] And White is
playing for two results, as they say}) 11... Nxc3 {Now Black manages to create
some pawn weaknesses} 12. bxc3 Nc6 13. a4 $5 Bc5 $2 (13... Nxd4 14. cxd4 (14.
Qxd4 Qxd4 15. cxd4 b4 {And once again, the endgame is favourable for Black})
14... b4 15. a5 Be7 {Seems more reasonable, since} 16. Ba4+ Kf8 17. Be3 h5 {Is
actually very good for Black}) 14. axb5 {[pgndiagram] Now White simply has no
problems} axb5 15. Rxa8 Bxa8 16. Nxb5 O-O $6 {Not sure why Kramnik refrained
from pawn recapture} (16... Nxe5 {Was still possible though} 17. Qxd8+ Kxd8)
17. Qxd8 Rxd8 18. Bf4 {[pgndiagram] Now White is simply a pawn up, although
Black's activity more than compensates} h6 19. h4 Ne7 20. Rd1 Rb8 21. Bc4 Bc6
22. Nd4 Be4 23. h5 Nd5 24. Bxd5 Bxd5 25. Be3 Rc8 26. Re1 Bc4 27. Nf3 Ba3 28.
Bd4 Bd3 29. Nd2 Bb2 30. Nf1 Rc4 31. Re3 Bf5 $6 {[pgndiagram] The first refusal
of the draw} (31... Bxc3 {Would lead to complete equality} 32. Bxc3 Rxc3 {
Kramnik probably feared the pin, but White is unable to take advantage of it}
33. f3 g6 34. hxg6 fxg6 {[pgndiagram] And now whenever White moves his king,
Black has a move} 35. Kf2 (35. Kh2 Rc1 $1) 35... Rc2+) 32. Ng3 Bh7 33. Kh2 {
Now White manages to extricate his king and can start playing for the win more
seriously} Bc1 34. Re2 Bf4 35. Kh3 Ra4 36. Kg4 Bc1 37. Kf3 Bd3 38. Re1 Bb2 39.
Ke3 Bc2 40. Kd2 Bb3 41. Rb1 Ra2 42. Kd3 Ba4 43. Ne4 {[pgndiagram] Grischuk has
obviously improved his position} Ba3 44. Ke3 (44. Rb8+ {Was maybe worth
including} Kh7 45. Nd6 $1 {Forcing Black to find the move} Bd1 $1 {[pgndiagram]
A fantastic move with a very deep idea} (45... f5 {Doesn't quite work} 46. Nf7
g5 47. Rb7) 46. f3 (46. Rb7 $1 Kg8 (46... Bxh5 47. Ra7) 47. Ra7 Be2+ 48. Ke4
Bxh5 49. c4 {And Black still has some difficulties to solve} Re2+ 50. Kd3 Bb4
51. c5 Bxc5 52. Bxc5 Rxe5 {[pgndiagram] Athough here, his compensation for the
piece might be sufficient}) 46... f5 47. Nf7 g5 48. Rb7 Be2+ 49. Ke3 Bc1+ 50.
Kf2 Ba6+) 44... Bc2 45. Re1 (45. Rb8+ {Again ignoring this check}) 45... Bb3
46. g4 Bd5 47. Kd3 Be7 48. Rb1 Ra8 49. f4 Bh4 {[pgndiagram] Deciding to wait
further} (49... g6 {Was better than it looks} 50. hxg6 fxg6 51. Nf6+ Bxf6 52.
exf6 Rc8 {And Black can probably hold this position}) 50. Rh1 Bd8 51. Rb1 Bc7 (
51... Bh4 $5) 52. Ke3 Kh7 53. Nd6 Kg8 54. Rb5 Bc6 55. Rb4 Bd8 56. Bb6 Bh4 57.
Bd4 Bg2 58. Rb2 Bc6 59. Rb6 Bg2 60. Rb2 Bc6 61. Rb3 Bg3 62. c4 Bh2 63. f5 exf5
$6 (63... Kf8 {It was maybe better to avoid exchaning on f5}) 64. Nxf5 (64.
gxf5 $5 {was also possible} f6 65. Rb6 $1 Ra3+ (65... fxe5 66. Rxc6) 66. Kd2
Bf4+ 67. Kc2 Bf3 68. exf6 gxf6 69. c5 Bxd6 70. Rxd6 {And White has some
practical chances}) 64... Kh7 {Now Black is solid enough} 65. Bb2 Re8 (65...
Ra4 {Would lead to immediate equality} 66. Nd6 Bd7 67. Nxf7 Rxc4 68. g5 hxg5
69. Nxg5+ Kh6 70. Nf3 Bf4+ {[pgndiagram] And Black can't lose this}) 66. Nd6
Re7 67. Rb8 f6 $6 {[pgndiagram] Seems logical, but not the best} (67... Bxe5 $1
68. Bxe5 Rxe5+ 69. Kd4 f6 {[pgndiagram] Was sufficient, but psychologicaly
unpleasant - what if the c-pawn becomes dangerous} 70. Nf7 Re8 71. Rxe8 Bxe8
72. Nd6 Bd7 {But Black has it all under control}) 68. Rc8 Bh1 $2 {The key
mistake of the game} (68... Bd7 69. Rc7 fxe5 {Looked unpleasant, but Black is
perfectly fine} 70. c5 e4 71. c6 Bg1+ 72. Kd2 $6 {It is risky to play for the
win} (72. Kf4 {White should best take the draw} Bh2+ 73. Ke3) 72... e3+ 73. Kc1
{(Avoiding all light squares)} e2 74. Bc3 Be3+ 75. Kc2 Bf5+ 76. Nxf5 Rxc7 77.
Nxe3 Rxc6 78. Ng2 Rc4 {[pgndiagram] And only Black can win here}) 69. Nf5 Rb7 (
69... Re6 70. Nh4 {With the threat of Ng6 was very annoying} fxe5 71. Ng6 Rxg6
72. hxg6+ Kxg6 73. c5 {[pgndiagram] And White is most probably winning}) 70.
exf6 $2 {Missing the chance} (70. e6 Rb3+ (70... Rxb2 71. e7) 71. Bc3 Bb7 72.
e7 Bxc8 73. e8=Q Bxf5 74. gxf5 Rxc3+ 75. Kd4 {And White has to be winning here}
) 70... Rb3+ 71. Bc3 gxf6 72. Kd2 Bf4+ {[pgndiagram] Now Black should safely
draw, but this is not the end of the miracles} 73. Kc2 Rb8 74. Rxb8 Bxb8 75.
Ne7 Be4+ 76. Kd2 Bf4+ $2 {[pgndiagram] The final mistake of the game - an
amazing one} (76... Bf3 77. Nd5 Kg7 78. Nxf6 Bf4+ 79. Ke1 Kf7 {[pgndiagram]
And as long as Black keeps the g-pawn under surveillance, White can't move his
knight. And it is hard to see how White can improve}) 77. Ke2 {Now there is no
hope - f6 or h6 are falling} Kg7 78. Nf5+ Kf7 79. Bd2 Be5 80. Bxh6 Ke6 81. Be3
Bxf5 82. gxf5+ Kxf5 83. h6 Kg6 84. c5 f5 85. Kf3 Kf7 86. Bf4 Bd4 87. c6 Ke7 88.
c7 Kd7 89. h7 Kc8 90. Ke2 Kd7 91. Kd3 {[pgndiagram] A tragic and amazing loss
for Kramnik, who has been always regarded as a great technician. IT is not
clear why he continued to play for so long in this position.} 1-0

[Event "World Championship Candidates"]
[Site "0:45:33-0:30:33"]
[Date "2018.03.22"]
[Round "10"]
[White "Shakhriyar Mamedyarov"]
[Black "Fabiano Caruana"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "E04"]
[PlyCount "115"]
[EventDate "2018.03.10"]
[SourceDate "2018.03.11"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. g3 {Another Catalan in this tournament.} dxc4 {
And yet another new continuation. Previously, Caruana employed a sharp
variation with the exchange sacrifice.} 5. Bg2 Nc6 $5 {[pgndiagram] The most
common follow up to the early taking on c4. Black intends to play Rb8-b5 if
allowed. The text move also eyes the e5 square} 6. O-O $5 {A relatively rare
continuation} (6. Qa4 {Is where the main battles take place} Bb4+ 7. Bd2 Nd5 {
Etc}) 6... Rb8 {Black is continuing with his plan} 7. e3 Be7 {Deviating
nevertheless} (7... b5 {Is perfectly playable, although early advance of the
b-pawn makes it a target} 8. a4 b4 9. Nbd2 Ba6 {Was nevertheless possible,
with complex battle}) 8. Nfd2 $1 {[pgndiagram] The only way - White needs to
get rid of the c pawn. Any other move would have allowed b5} (8. Nbd2 b5) (8.
Qe2 b5) 8... e5 {Perhaps a bit risky, but playable nevertheless} (8... O-O {
Was safer} 9. Nxc4 b5 $1 10. Bxc6 (10. Ncd2 Na5) 10... bxc4 {And Black should
most probably be able to equalize}) 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 10. dxe5 Ng4 {It is not
clear whether Caruana was still in his preparation, or whether this is over
the board improvisation.} (10... Nd5 $5 {Is preferred by the engine - not
blocking the way of the bishop to h3} 11. Nxc4 Bh3 12. Re1 Nb4 {With
reasonable compensation for the pawn}) 11. Nxc4 Be6 12. Nbd2 h5 {[pgndiagram]
Only this might be an inaccuracy} (12... Qd3 {Maybe it was better to plant her
Majesty at the heart of White's position} 13. b3 h5 {And only then advance the
h-pawn}) 13. f3 Qd3 $6 (13... Rb4 {IS the engine preferrence} 14. fxg4 Bxc4 15.
Nxc4 Qxd1 16. Rxd1 Rxc4 17. gxh5 Rxh5 {With sufficient compensation for the
pawn}) 14. fxg4 Bxc4 15. Nxc4 Qxc4 16. gxh5 {[pgndiagram] Now Black loses
another pawn. True, White's pawn structure is incredibly ugly, and Black will
probably get some material back, but it is a question whether White can use
the time usefully in the meantime} Qe6 17. Qf3 Rb5 18. Qf5 (18. Bd2 $5 {Was
interesting} Rxe5 19. Rac1 Bc5 20. g4 O-O 21. Kh1 {But here as well it is hard
for White to count on something serious}) 18... Rxe5 19. Qxe6 Rxe6 20. Rf5 Rd6
21. Kg2 Rd1 22. b3 g6 23. Re5 $6 {Now Caruana is able to equalize completely} (
23. Rf2 Rxh5 24. Bb2 {And White is only slightly better, due to Black's pawn
structure}) 23... c5 (23... Rxh5 {Is also sufficient} 24. Rxe7+ Kxe7 25. Ba3+
Rd6 26. Rc1 Kd7 27. Bxd6 cxd6 {And we have a dead draw on the board}) 24. hxg6
fxg6 25. e4 {[pgndiagram] Threatening Rxe7 and Bg5+.} Rh7 {The only move to
defend against the threat, but sufficient} 26. h4 Rf7 27. Kh3 Rff1 28. Bg5 {
[pgndiagram] Shark really goes all out in this game, but he is unable to shake
the equilibrium} Rxa1 29. Rxe7+ Kf8 30. Rxc7 {Despite sacking an exchange,
White is not risking very much - Black has to be precise} Rxa2 31. Be7+ Kg8 32.
Rc8+ Kg7 33. Bxc5 Re2 34. e5 Rd1 {[pgndiagram]} (34... Rxe5 {Would be, of
course, quite terrible, due to} 35. Bd4) 35. Re8 Rd5 36. Bd6 Re3 37. Re7+ Kg8
38. Re8+ Kf7 39. Re7+ Kg8 40. b4 {Playing on for the win} Rdd3 41. Kg4 Rxg3+
42. Kf4 Rgf3+ 43. Ke4 Rfe3+ 44. Kf4 Rf3+ 45. Kg5 Rf7 $1 {Another very precise
move, after which White also needs to be slightly careful not to overextend}
46. e6 (46. Kxg6 {Was also sufficient} Rg3+ (46... Rxe7 47. Bxe7 {Is losing})
47. Kh6 Rxe7 48. Bxe7 Kf7 {With equality}) 46... Rd5+ {A practical decision} (
46... Rxe7 {Is also sufficient, but why allow the activation of the White king?
} 47. Bxe7 Kh7 48. Kf6 Rf3+ 49. Ke5 Kg7 50. Kd5 {Of course, this is also dead
equal, but still}) 47. Kxg6 Rxd6 48. Rxf7 Rxe6+ 49. Rf6 Re4 {[pgndiagram] And
at this particular moment, a draw could have been easily agreed.} 50. b5 Rxh4
51. Ra6 Rh7 52. b6 Rg7+ 53. Kh6 Rh7+ 54. Kg5 Rg7+ 55. Kf5 axb6 56. Rxb6 Rf7+
57. Rf6 Rxf6+ 58. Kxf6 {A high quality, tense encounter between the leaders of
the tournament, that did nothing to resolve the status quo at the top.} 1/2-1/2

[Event "Candidates Tournament 2018 R11"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "2018.03.23"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Ding, Liren"]
[Black "Grischuk, Alexander"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "D40"]
[PlyCount "192"]
[EventDate "2018.??.??"]
[SourceDate "2018.03.11"]

1. c4 c5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. e3 e6 5. d4 d5 {Symmetrical semi-Tarrasch
defense} 6. a3 dxc4 {[pgndiagram] This move screams: "I want to win this one".
Before this round, Grischuk was 3rd, 1 point behind Caruana and 0,5 behind
Mamedyarov.} (6... a6 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. b4 Bd6 9. Bb2 O-O 10. Qc2 Qe7 {Game
beetwen Anand and Kramnik in 2013, was played in this line. Kramnik won
quickly in only 27 moves.}) 7. Bxc4 a6 (7... cxd4 8. exd4 Be7 9. O-O O-O {
Another route black could take. White has space advantage for the price of an
isolated pawn.}) 8. O-O b5 9. Ba2 Bb7 10. Qe2 Qc7 11. Bd2 (11. d5 exd5 12. Nxd5
Nxd5 13. Bxd5 Be7 14. e4 $11) 11... Be7 12. Rac1 c4 {[pgndiagram] While c4
blocks white's bishop and rook it's also reducing pressure on d4 pawn.} 13. e4
Rd8 {Pressuring d4 pawn again.} 14. Be3 (14. e5 Nd5 15. Bb1 Na5 16. Bc2 Nxc3
17. Bxc3 Bd5 $11) 14... Ng4 15. e5 (15. h3 Nxe3 16. fxe3 O-O 17. e5 f5 18. exf6
Bxf6) 15... Na5 $2 {[pgndiagram] First mistake in the game. By playing Na5
white is free to move his bishop away from d4 pawn. Instead black should have
castle and force white to waste a move and play h3 before taking the bishop on
e3.} 16. Bf4 {Night on g4 looks totaly lost.} Qb6 17. Rcd1 h5 18. h3 Nh6 19.
Bb1 {Ding is not playing anything spectacular, just following general advice-
in the middlegame reposition your pieces on better squares.} Nb3 20. Be3 Qc7 (
20... Bd5 21. Nxd5 exd5 22. Rfe1 Ng8 23. Ng5 $16) 21. Rfe1 Kf8 (21... O-O 22.
d5 exd5 23. Qc2 g6 24. Bxh6 $16) 22. d5 $1 {[pgndiagram] This shows how chess
is sometimes simple. White's pieces are perfectly placed and the most logical
thing is to open centre and attack black king.} exd5 23. e6 {Key move in the
position. While it's not strongest move according to engine it gives black
most trouble in practical game.} (23. Nd4 Qxe5 24. Nxb3 cxb3 25. Qd2 Ng4 26.
Bf4 Qf6 27. Bg3 d4 28. Ne4 Bxe4 29. Rxe4 $16) 23... fxe6 24. Ng5 Qd7 (24...
Bxg5 25. Bxg5 Re8 26. Nxd5 Bxd5 27. Rxd5 $16) 25. Bg6 {White is 2 pawns down
but he's absolutely winning. Black's pieces are not doing anything and his
king is weak.} Bf6 26. Bb6 $1 {[pgndiagram] Exploiting black's weak 8th rank.}
Rc8 (26... Bxg5 27. Bxd8 Bxd8 28. Qxe6 Qxe6 29. Rxe6 Be7 30. Nxd5 Bxd5 31. Rxd5
{White threatens Rd8 and after bishop takes Re8 mate} Nf7 32. Rd7 Nc5 33. Rexe7
Nxd7 34. Rxd7 $18 {It's all over. Black can't defend mate and save all of his
pieces.}) 27. Nxe6+ Kg8 28. Nxd5 Bxd5 {[pgndiagram] Critical moment. After
next move black can only resign.} 29. Nf4 $2 {It was quite surprising that
Ding missed both Nd8 and Rd5. They aren't too complicated compared to many
variants in this game. Probably the combination of nerves and not having
enough time got better of him. Even after this move, white is still much
better.} (29. Nd8 Rxd8 30. Rxd5 Qxd5 31. Qe8+ Rxe8 32. Rxe8#) (29. Rxd5 Qxd5
30. Nd8 {Black simply can't defend mate on 8th rank.}) 29... Nc1 {It's always
interesting to see Grischuk play on 30 seconds. According to many top
Grandmaster's he's still making best moves although he has little or no time.
Unsurprisingly he has won World Blitz Championship 3 times ( 2006, 2012 and
2015 ).} 30. Qxh5 (30. Rxc1 Bf7 31. Rcd1 Qc6 32. Ba5 Qc5 33. Bxf7+ Nxf7 34. Qe6
$18 {Maybe Ding should have gone for this variation purely from practical side.
While it's not the strongest it's much easier to play.}) 30... Nd3 31. Nxd5 Nf7
32. Qe2 $2 (32. Nxf6+ gxf6 33. Bxf7+ Qxf7 34. Qg4+ Kh7 35. Qf5+ Kg7 36. Re4 $18
{Another variation that wins the game missed by Ding.}) 32... Nfe5 33. Be4 Rh4
{[pgndiagram] It's not that simple any more. White is still better but now
black's pieces are dead any more.} 34. f4 Rxf4 35. Nxf4 Nxf4 36. Bh7+ Kh8 (
36... Kxh7 37. Qe4+ Nfg6 38. Rxd7) 37. Qe4 Qc6 38. Bd4 (38. Be3 Qxe4 39. Bxe4
Ned3 40. Bxf4 Nxf4 41. Rf1 $18 {White has one more pawn than in the game.})
38... Nxh3+ 39. Kh2 Ng5 40. Qxc6 Rxc6 41. Bc2 Ngf7 {Weird and weak move. Black
pieces are weaker than white's. Why would you give strong bishop for "double"
night?} 42. Bxe5 $2 (42. Bc3 $18 {Stronger, simple move opening d file for rook
}) 42... Nxe5 43. Rd5 Re6 44. Kh3 Kg8 45. b3 $2 {It's in black's favor to
exchange as many pawns as possible. I can't find the reason why white would
play this.} Kf7 46. bxc4 Nxc4 47. Rd7+ Be7 48. Rxe6 Kxe6 49. Ra7 Nxa3 50. Rxa6+
$14 {[pgndiagram] Now it's clear that this position isn't winning for white
any more. White still has some winning chances but objectively the position is
draw.} Kd5 51. Bh7 Nc4 52. Kg4 Bf6 53. Ra7 Kd4 54. Bg8 Nd6 55. Kf3 Kc5 56. Ra8
b4 57. Ke2 Bc3 58. Kd3 Kb6 59. Ke3 Kc6 60. Kf4 Nb7 61. Kf5 Nc5 62. g4 Kb5 63.
g5 b3 64. Kg6 Kb4 65. Bd5 Bd4 66. Kh7 Kc3 67. g6 b2 68. Ba2 Kc2 69. Rb8 Ne4 70.
Rb4 Nc3 71. Be6 Kc1 72. Bf5 Be5 73. Rb7 b1=Q 74. Bxb1 Nxb1 75. Rxg7 Nc3 (75...
Bxg7 $4 76. Kxg7 Nc3 77. Kf8 {That would be horrible way to lose a game.}) 76.
Re7 Bd4 77. Rd7 Be5 78. Kh6 Kc2 79. Kg5 Kb3 80. Kf5 Bh8 81. Rb7+ Kc2 82. Rh7
Bd4 83. Ke6 Kb3 84. Rd7 Bh8 85. Rd8 Bg7 86. Kf7 Be5 87. Re8 Bd4 88. Rb8+ Kc2
89. Rb7 Be5 90. Rb6 Bd4 91. Rf6 Bxf6 92. Kxf6 Nd5+ 93. Kf7 Ne3 94. Kf6 (94. g7
Nf5 95. g8=Q Nh6+ 96. Kf8 Nxg8 97. Kxg8) 94... Nd5+ 95. Kf7 Ne3 96. g7 Nf5 {
Crazy game ! Watching it was like being on a roller coaster ride. Ding can
really be sad that he didn't win this one but I think he'll have an
opportunity to learn quite a lot from this game specially from the
psychological aspect. Grischuk can be happy he miraculosly saved this game and
still has good chances for the first 3 places.} 1/2-1/2

[Event "World Championship Candidates"]
[Site "0:44:33-0:48:33"]
[Date "2018.03.24"]
[Round "12"]
[White "Sergey Karjakin"]
[Black "Fabiano Caruana"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C42"]
[PlyCount "95"]
[EventDate "2018.03.10"]
[SourceDate "2018.03.11"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. Nc3 $5 {In previous rounds,
Kramnik and So have played 5 Qe2 here, but Karjakin chooses the most topical
continuation} (5. Qe2) 5... Nxc3 (5... Nf6 {There is something to be said
about this move as well}) 6. dxc3 Nc6 7. Be3 Be7 8. Qd2 Be6 9. O-O-O Qd7 (9...
Bxa2 {Taking the a-pawn is not to be recommended} 10. b3 a5 11. Kb2 a4 12. Ra1
axb3 13. cxb3 Bxb3 14. Rxa8 Qxa8 15. Kxb3 {[pgndiagram] And although I have
lost something similar in blitz many times, White should be objectively much
better here}) 10. a3 {[pgndiagram] Many moves have been played here. The
modest advance of the a-pawn was essayed by Caruana's second, Rustam
Kasimdzhanov} (10. h3) (10. h4) (10. Kb1) 10... h6 {This is I think a waiting
move - inviting White to declare his intentions} (10... O-O {Castling at once
would have allowed white to go for the immediate push with} 11. h4 Bg4 12. Bd3
{[pgndiagram] And this is dangerous for Black} Rae8 13. h5 h6 14. Kb1 Bf5 (
14... Bxf3 {Is not really an option - opening the g-file}) 15. Bxf5 Qxf5 16.
Rh3 {And White holds the initiative}) (10... O-O-O 11. Nd4 {Is now
inconvinient - the weakness of a7 and g7} Nxd4 12. Qxd4 {[%csl Ra7,Rg7][%cal
Rd4a7,Rd4g7]}) 11. Nd4 {Deviating from the Kasimdzhanov game. Former FIDE
Champion chose the king move instead} (11. Kb1 Rg8 12. Bd3 O-O-O {Kasimdzhanov
- Li,D, Chengdu, 2017}) 11... Nxd4 {Black virtually has no choice but to take
on d4 - where will the light squared bishop go?} (11... Bg4 12. f3 Bh5 13. Nxc6
Qxc6 14. Bd3 {Is much better for White - Black has trouble castling. Say} O-O-O
(14... O-O 15. Bxh6 gxh6 16. Qxh6 {[pgndiagram] Is winning} Bg6 17. Bxg6 fxg6
18. Qxg6+ Kh8 19. Rd4) 15. Rhe1 $1 {[pgndiagram]} (15. Bxa7 b6 16. Ba6+ Kd7 17.
Qd3 Bg6 $1 {Is not so clear}) 15... Rhe8 16. Bf5+ Kb8 17. Qd4 {[pgndiagram]
And already these brief variations show that Black has definite problems in
similar lines.}) 12. Bxd4 (12. cxd4 {[pgndiagram] Seems more natural - after
all, as beginners we are taught to undouble our pawns. So why did Karjakin
refuse to do so? Well, this was a must win game for him, and going for
symmetry eases Black's play} Bf5 13. Kb1 d5 {And it is hard to win this}) 12...
Rg8 (12... O-O {Is probably playable, but with the pawn on h6, it is risky to
castle in practical game - White has clear plan of pushing on the kingside})
13. Be2 c5 {Taking care a7 will not be attacked after castling} 14. Be3 d5 {
[pgndiagram] The opening is over. White is better developed and optically
better, but can he really dream of breaching White's defences?} 15. f4 {A good
move, gaining some space and preparing to pressure the center} O-O-O 16. Bf3
Bg4 $6 {With hindisght, this is an oversight, but what could be more natural
than the bishop exchange?} (16... g5 {Is the combative option - Black wants to
fight for the share in the center as well. Still after} 17. g3 f5 18. Bg2 Kb8
19. Rhe1 {[pgndiagram] He is definitely on the back foot}) (16... f5 {Was
Caruana's suggestion. However, after} 17. Rhe1 Bf7 18. Bf2 b6 19. h3 {White
retains some pressure}) 17. Bxd5 $1 {[pgndiagram] A staggering decision -
White gives up an exchange for the pawn and the strong d5 bishop. Karjakin
said he wasn't sure about the consequences, but decided to go so nevertheless.
Putin's Minister of Defence proves he has nerves of steel.  Or balls.} Bxd1 18.
Rxd1 Qc7 (18... c4 {Was an idea that went through my mind, but it is crap} 19.
Qe2 (19. Bxa7 {Is also sufficient}) 19... b6 (19... b5 20. a4 a6 21. axb5 axb5
22. Bb6)) 19. c4 {[pgndiagram] Of course, the exchange sacrifice is not
winning by itself, but it sets White some serious issues} Rge8 20. Qf2 b6 21.
g4 {White plans to push his kingside pawns, create some weaknesses and then
penetrate the back ranks.} Bf6 22. Kb1 Rd7 23. Rd3 g5 $6 {[pgndiagram] This is
probably the first move that helps White. Black creates an opening himself.} (
23... Rde7 24. g5 (24. Qf3 {Of course, White can continue to increase the
pressure instead of commiting the pawn to g5}) 24... hxg5 25. fxg5 Be5 {
[pgndiagram] And now White has to find the accurate} 26. Bg2 Rd8 27. Qf5+ Rdd7
28. Bh3 Kd8 29. Rxd7+ Qxd7 30. Qxd7+ Rxd7 31. Bxd7 Kxd7 32. h4 {[pgndiagram]
Although still, Black should be able to hold}) (23... Ree7 24. Qg2) 24. Ka2
Ree7 (24... Rde7 {I didn't quite understand why Black didn't want to double
his rooks.} 25. fxg5 Bxg5 26. Qf5+ {[pgndiagram] But this check reveals it -
Black loses another pawn} Kb8 27. Bxg5 hxg5 28. Qxg5 {[pgndiagram] And now
White is probably close to winning - the tactics work in his favour} Qxh2 29.
Bxf7 Rxf7 30. Rd8+ Rxd8 31. Qxd8+ Kb7 32. Qd5+ Kb8 33. Qxf7 {[pgndiagram] And
White is winning}) 25. Qf3 Kd8 (25... Kb8 {Seems more logical, although after}
26. fxg5 Bxg5 27. Bf2 Bf4 28. Bh4 Re8 29. h3 {[pgndiagram] White retains the
pressure, but he hasn't yet won any material}) 26. Bd2 {[pgndiagram] Removing
the bishop from attack} (26. fxg5 {Is once again indicated by the engine} Bxg5
27. Bxg5 hxg5 28. Bc6 {And now Black is in trouble, since} Rxd3 29. Qxd3+ Kc8
30. Qf5+ Kb8 31. Qxg5 {Is once again in White favour}) 26... Kc8 {Continuing
to wait} 27. Qf1 $1 {[pgndiagram] Another strong move - Karjakin intends Rh3.
A fantastic conception} Rd6 {Deciding to give up the pawn, but preventing Rh3}
(27... Kd8 28. Rh3 Bg7 29. fxg5 Qe5 30. Qc1 Rxd5 31. cxd5 {And now, in
contrast to Qb8 variation, Qxd5 is not possible} Qxd5+ 32. Kb1 hxg5 33. Bxg5 {
[pgndiagram]And White wins} f6 34. Rd3) (27... Kb8 28. Rh3 Bg7 29. fxg5 Qe5 {
Once again doesn't work, due to the simple} 30. Bf4) (27... Qb8 {Is the
computer resource. The point is that after} 28. Rh3 Bg7 29. fxg5 Qe5 $1 {
[pgndiagram] Retains the pawn} 30. Qc1 Rxd5 31. cxd5 Qxd5+ 32. Kb1 hxg5) 28.
fxg5 Bxg5 29. Bxg5 hxg5 30. Qf5+ {[pgndiagram] Now it is almost all over - two
pawns for the exchange} Rdd7 31. Qxg5 Qe5 32. Qh6 $1 {It is important to
retain the queens} (32. Qxe5 {Would be a terrible decision} Rxe5 33. h4 Re2 34.
Kb3 Rh2 {And Black is in time}) 32... Kd8 $2 {The decisive mistake - once
White advances g5 everything is definitely over} (32... f6 {Was the last
chance of creating some resistance}) 33. g5 {[pgndiagram] Now White will
simply push his pawns. Black's pieces are quite horrible} Qd6 34. Qh8+ Re8 35.
Qh4 Qg6 36. Qg4 Re5 37. h4 Ke7 38. Rd2 b5 39. Bxf7 {[pgndiagram] A very nice
tactical finish} Qf5 40. Rxd7+ Kxd7 41. Qxf5+ Rxf5 42. g6 Ke7 43. cxb5 Rh5 44.
c4 Rxh4 45. a4 Rg4 46. a5 Kd6 47. a6 Kc7 48. Kb3 {[pgndiagram] There is
nothing Black can do against the advance of the White king, so Caruana
resigned. A fantastic game by Sergey, which put him in the lead two rounds
before the end. Moreover, at this point, he had better tiebreaks than Caruana,
so in the event of them sharing the lead, he would be proclaimed as a winner. 
Last two rounds will be once again incredibly dramatic and we can only thank
FIDE they decided to supplement knock-out matches with a Candidates tournament.
} 1-0

[Event "Candidates Tournament 2018 (13)"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "2018.03.26"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Caruana, Fabiano"]
[Black "Aronian, Levon"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "C88"]
[PlyCount "77"]
[SourceDate "2018.03.11"]

1. e4 {As we have just seen, in the previous round Caruana has suffered a
devastating loss and in this game he basically had to win because his tiebreak
was inferior to Karjakin's. It is interesting he decided to go 1 e4, but he
was probably aware Aronian doesn't play the Berlin, but rather the Marshall} e5
2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 {Voila - no Berlin} 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 (5... Nxe4 {It
seems no body ventures to play the Open Ruy these days, but perhaps this is
not the time and place to talk about that}) 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. d3 {And
this has become a standard way of avoiding the Marshall these days} (8. h3 {
Doesn't quite prevent the d5 advance} Bb7 9. d3 d5 {And Black has definite
compensation. So's brilliant victory against Ivanchuk comes to mind}) (8. a4 {
Is also playable, though. I am not sure about the state of theory regarding
these two lines, but Caruana MIGHT have known a bit more than yours trully})
8... d6 9. Bd2 $5 {[pgndiagram] And this is a very interesting move,
preventing Na5 in the most direct and brutal fashion} (9. c3 Bb7 10. Nbd2 {
Would lead to a more typical Ruy Lopez}) 9... Bg4 {Also quite logical - once
Na5 is prevented, Nd4 is an option} 10. c3 {Preventing Nd4} d5 $6 {Perhaps
this is a matter of taste, but I don't like this move. The old dogma says
Black solves his problems once he carries out the d5 advance, but in the given
instance, the symmetrical pawn structure doesn't lead to immediate equality} (
10... Na5 11. Bc2 c5) 11. h3 (11. exd5 {Is alo playable} Nxd5 12. Qe2 Re8 {
Although White's pieces do look a bit cumsy}) 11... Bh5 (11... Bxf3 {Was the
alternative} 12. Qxf3 d4 13. Bd5 Qd7 14. Bxc6 Qxc6 15. cxd4 exd4 16. Rc1 Qb6 {
[pgndiagram] With equality. Something similar is possible on Black's next move
as well}) 12. Qe2 Rb8 (12... Bxf3 13. Qxf3 d4) 13. Bg5 {Forcing Black to
declare his intentions, since White is threatening to take on f6 and then on d5
} dxe4 (13... Bxf3 14. Qxf3 d4) (13... h6 14. Bxf6 Bxf6 15. exd5) 14. dxe4 h6
15. Bc1 $1 {[pgndiagram] A great move - the bishop has fullfiled its role, and
now gets out of the way for the remainder of the pieces to develop} Bg6 {
Rushing to prepare the Nh5-Nf4 plan, but Black is not in time with this idea} (
15... a5 {Is the suggestion of the engine - gaining space on the queenside . 
It might be better than the text move} 16. Nbd2 a4 17. Bc2 Qc8 {And it is
completely another game. Still, it seems it is favourable for Black to gain
space on the queenside - in the game White undermined the structure on that
side of the board as well.}) 16. Nbd2 Nh5 17. Nf1 {Just in time} Bc5 (17... Nf4
18. Bxf4 exf4 19. Rad1 {Is simply better for White}) (17... Bd6 {Covering the
e5 square in advance and insisting on Nf4 was playable} 18. a4 b4 19. Bc2 a5
20. Qc4 {[pgndiagram] And once again White retains the initiative - Black's
queenside structure is once again the key}) 18. g3 $5 {Not sure about validity
of this move, but it won Caruana the game - the idea of Nh4 Nf5 is very potent}
Kh7 $2 {[pgndiagram] I think this is too passive - From this moment Aronian's
game starts going downhill} (18... Nf6 {Going for the e4 pawn once Ng3 is
impossible, was more forcing, but also more direct} 19. Be3 Nxe4 (19... Bxe3
20. Nxe3 Bxe4 21. Ng4 Bxf3 22. Qxf3 Nxg4 23. Qxc6 Nf6) 20. Rad1 Bd6 {And now}
21. Bxh6 (21. Bd5 Ne7) 21... gxh6 22. Bd5 Nxg3 23. Nxg3 Ne7 {Is approximately
equal}) 19. Kg2 Qe7 20. Bc2 Rfd8 21. b4 $1 {[pgndiagram] First shifting his
intention toward queenside} Bb6 22. a4 Nf6 23. Nh4 $1 {[pgndiagram] Now the
other side of the board!} (23. axb5 $1 {It was possible to take the pawn} axb5
24. Qxb5 Bxf2 (24... Qe6 25. Qe2 {With clear plus}) 25. Qxc6 Bxe1 26. Nxe1 {
[pgndiagram] And White's material means he has a clear advantage. Caruana
prefers to increase the pressure}) 23... Qe6 24. Bd3 $6 {I am still not
certain whether allowing the piece sacrifice was necessary or whether the
sacrifice is fully correct} (24. Nxg6 {Was simpler - White retains a clear edge
}) 24... Bh5 $5 {Going for an interesting piece sacrifice} (24... Qd7 25. Rd1
Ne7 {Was the alternative, which also doesn't look quite rosy}) 25. g4 Bxg4 26.
hxg4 {[pgndiagram] The g4 and f2 pawns are falling, but White is able to
counter Black's idea with a knight jump to f5} Nxg4 (26... Qxg4+ {Doesn't
really lead anywhere} 27. Qxg4 Nxg4 28. Be2 Nxf2 29. axb5 axb5 30. Bxb5 {
[pgndiagram] And Black doesn't have sufficient compensation for the piece}) 27.
Nf5 $1 Nxf2 28. Bc2 $1 {[pgndiagram] Another only move, but sufficient. N1e3
is coming and Black's knight is stuck} g6 29. N1e3 gxf5 30. exf5 Qf6 (30...
Rg8+ {Doesn't quite help} 31. Kf1 Qf6 32. Qxf2) 31. Qxf2 e4 $2 {[pgndiagram]
This is the decisive mistake of the game and the best indication of Aronian's
form in this tournament. Once agian in the critical moment he fails to find a
tactical combination, in a position that usually suits his style} (31... Nxb4 {
Amazingly, but it would appear that the sacrifice of another piece, deflecting
the pawn on c3, would very much keep Black in the game} 32. cxb4 Rd4 {And it
is hard to suggest White's next move. The engine plays the extraordinary} 33.
Kh3 {But after} Qg5 {[pgndiagram] In practical game, it is incredibly hard to
play the Black pieces. So it would appear it was wrong to allow the sacrifice
and that Aronian's intuition didn't betray him.}) 32. Rh1 {[pgndiagram] Now
White is safe and sound} (32. Bxe4 {Was also working}) 32... Rd6 33. Bxe4 Rg8+
34. Kf1 Ne5 35. Qf4 c6 {This move speaks for itself} 36. axb5 Rg5 37. bxa6 Qd8
38. f6+ Ng6 39. Rxh6+ {[pgndiagram] A beautiful finish to a very nervy
encounter. But kudos to Caruana for bravery; after finding himself in a
desperate situation, he managed to win 'to order' and now the pressure is on
Karjakin who has the White pieces tomorrow. In any case, an extremely
interesting final round awaits us tomorrow.} *

[Event "World Championship Candidates"]
[Site "0:09:33-0:14:33"]
[Date "2018.03.27"]
[Round "14"]
[White "Alexander Grischuk"]
[Black "Fabiano Caruana"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C43"]
[PlyCount "138"]
[EventDate "2018.03.10"]
[SourceDate "2018.03.11"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 {A somewhat surprising, but perfectly logical decision,
considering the tournament situation. In contrast to the last round of the
previous Candidates Tournament where he played the Rauzer Sicilain, Caruana
opts for safety first and sticks to his guns. He decides to keep the game in
safe waters and to see how the remaining games are going to unfold with a draw
in his pocket.} 3. d4 $5 {For the first time in the tournament he has to face
this continuation. Previously, everyone took on e5} Nxe4 4. dxe5 d5 5. Nbd2
Nxd2 6. Bxd2 Be7 {[pgndiagram] I highly doubt this is the critical way of
battling the Petroff, but then again, everything is playable these days} 7. Bd3
c5 8. c3 Nc6 9. O-O Bg4 10. Re1 Qd7 {[pgndiagram] Okay, it is time to take the
stop and consider the situation arising out of the opening. White has a pawn
majority on the kingside, which he would very much like to advance, but it is
very hard to achieve. His pawn on e5 gives him more space, but also needs some
protection. Black on the other hand, can consider playing on the queenside,
undermining the structure with a timely f6 or advancing the center with d4. It
is hard to say whether anyone is better, but the position is not an automatic
draw} 11. h3 Bh5 12. Bf4 {Overprotecting the e5 pawn. Nimzowitsch would be
proud} Qe6 {Waiting for the moment to see what White will do and placing the
queen on a defended square - very important.} (12... O-O {Castling gives White
the option of} 13. Qc2 Bg6 (13... Bxf3 {Is not something to be recommended} 14.
Bxh7+ Kh8 15. Bf5 Qc7 (15... Be4 16. Qxe4 dxe4 17. Bxd7) 16. gxf3 {With a
clear plus}) 14. Bxg6 hxg6 15. Rad1 {With some advantage}) 13. a3 $6 {Deciding
on a plan of the queenside expansion. It would appear that this selection of
the plan was not the most accurate - it relinquished all the hopes of
advantage White might have had.} (13. Be2 {Breaking the pin, is instead
recommended by the engine. After} O-O (13... Bg6 {Is more prudent}) 14. Nd4 $5
Bxe2 15. Nxe6 Bxd1 16. Nxf8 Bc2 17. Nd7 {[pgndiagram] White is an exchange up,
but his knight is in trouble. The engine gives the eternal 0.0 evaluation, but
in practical game it is hard as Black to go for this}) 13... O-O 14. b4 {
[pgndiagram] Grischuk is consistent with his plan} (14. Qc2 {Is now met by}
Bxf3 {Since} 15. Bxh7+ Kh8 16. Bf5 {Loses to} Be4 17. Bxe6 (17. Qxe4 dxe4 {
[pgndiagram] And now we see the point of Qe6 - White is a piece down after} 18.
Bxe6 fxe6) 17... Bxc2 18. Bxd5) 14... h6 15. Bg3 b6 {Not sure if it was
required to go for the queen exchange} (15... Rad8 {Would prevent Nd4 and was
arguably better. The point is that the d5 pawn is defended in advance - see
the note to Black's 16th move} 16. Nd4 cxd4 17. Qxh5 dxc3) 16. Nd4 (16. Qc2 {
Would maintain the tension}) 16... Bxd1 (16... cxd4 {Is now bad} 17. Qxh5 dxc3
18. Bf5 g6 19. Bxe6 gxh5 20. Bxd5 $1) 17. Nxe6 fxe6 18. Raxd1 {[pgndiagram]
Still, the position is complex - White's bishop pair is severely restricted by
both pawn chains and it is not clear who is better} c4 $1 {A good move,
closing the queenside} 19. Bc2 b5 20. a4 a6 21. f3 Bg5 (21... Nxb4 {
[pgndiagram] Was a very interesting possibility, that might give Black the
initiative. However, at this point, it was not yet neccessary to go all in, so
Caruana decided to keep it safe}) 22. Bf2 Bf4 23. Bc5 Rfd8 24. Bd6 Bg3 25. Re2
g5 {[pgndiagram] It is instructive how the maneouvre of the bishop to g3 in
combination with g5 restricted White's kingside majority.} 26. Kf1 Kf7 27. Bc7
Re8 28. Bd6 Rac8 29. Ra1 Red8 30. Bb1 $6 {[pgndiagram] The start of the wrong
path. Objectively, if both sides keep shuffling, neither play can count on any
advantage} (30. Rd1 {Preventing d4, was enough to maintain equality. I guess
Grischuk's time trouble and ambition forced him to play for the win}) 30... Rd7
31. Ra3 $6 {The second mistake, missing Black's strong reply} (31. axb5 axb5
32. Ra6 {Was more prudent. Black can't get d4 now since} d4 33. Be4 d3 34. Bxc6
Rxc6 35. Rxc6 dxe2+ 36. Kxe2 Ra7 {[pgndiagram] Doesn't bring him any advantage}
) 31... d4 $1 {[pgndiagram] It is counter intuitive to open the position when
White has the two bishops, but Black's pieces spring to life and White's
bishops are out of play} 32. axb5 (32. cxd4 Nxd4 33. Rd2 Nc6 {And the e5 falls,
since} 34. Re2 Nxe5) 32... axb5 33. cxd4 Nxd4 34. Rea2 Nc6 35. Be4 {
[pgndiagram] Now e5 falls all the same, but at least White has managed to
activate a bit} Bxe5 (35... Nxe5 36. Ra7 {Is inferior}) 36. Bxc6 Rxd6 37. Bxb5
Rd1+ 38. Ke2 Rg1 39. Ke3 Rb1 40. Ra7+ {[pgndiagram] At this point, Caruana
said he knew he was better, but not certain whether it was enough for win. I
am not sure, but I think Mamedyarov's game had already finished (or finished
very soon) so Caruana could basically offer a draw at any moment. Instead, he
kept on playing for the win - a brave and courageous decision, displaying his
character traits. Kudos!} Kf6 41. Bd7 Bf4+ 42. Ke2 Rd8 43. Rc2 Rxb4 44. Bc6 c3
45. Rd7 Rc8 46. Be4 {[pgndiagram] Practically speaking, it is incredibly
difficult to defend this position as White. Moreover, he has zero counterplay,
which makes Caruana's decision to continue much more understandable.} h5 47.
Kd3 Rb2 48. Ke2 h4 49. Rd1 Ke5 50. Ra1 Rd8 51. Rd1 Rdb8 52. Ra1 Bd2 53. Ra6 Rd8
54. Rc6 Rb1 55. Kf2 Ra1 56. Rc4 Rd4 57. Rc8 Rb4 58. Ke2 $2 {[pgndiagram]
Finally, Grischuk cracks under the pressure and commits a decisive mistake} (
58. Bd3 {Would still allow him to hold, since after} Rb2 59. Rc4 Rxc2 60. Bxc2
Ra2 61. Bb1 {Black can't make direct progress. So he has to shuffle some more})
58... Kf4 $2 {[pgndiagram] Missing the best way} (58... Re1+ {Was immediately
winning} 59. Kf2 Rbb1 60. Rc5+ Kf6 {[pgndiagram] Keeping the f4 square open
for the bishop!} (60... Kf4 61. R2xc3) 61. R2xc3 (61. f4 g4 62. hxg4 Rxe4)
61... Bf4 {[pgndiagram] With mate to follow}) 59. Kf2 (59. Kd3 {Was more
resillient}) 59... Rbb1 60. Rf8+ Ke5 61. Bd3 Rb2 62. Ke2 Re1+ 63. Kf2 Rc1 64.
Rxb2 cxb2 65. Rb8 Bc3 66. Be4 Bd4+ 67. Ke2 Kf4 68. Rb4 e5 69. Rb7 Kg3 {
[pgndiagram] As it is, Grischuk resigned and there was a thunder of clapping
among the spectators. At 25 years of age, Caruana finally managed to get
through to the Candidates. Judging by the play in the tournament, he has
completely deserved the shot at the title and I believe he will be definitely
a worthy challenger.} 0-1

