1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 Bg4 3. Bg2 c6 4. O-O e6 {The modern recipe against Reti. Just
put your bishop outside the chain and go e6 and c6.} 5. d4 Nf6 6. Nbd2 Be7 7.
Re1 {Preparation of e4 is on of the plans for White.} (7. c4 {Is the other})
7... O-O 8. e4 Bh5 9. c3 a5 $5 {An unorthodox decision to grab some space. I
find Bosiocic's play very appealing.} 10. h3 (10. a4 {Was probably more
circumspect, not allowing the expansion}) 10... a4 11. exd5 cxd5 12. g4 Bg6 13.
Ne5 Nbd7 14. Nxg6 hxg6 15. a3 {White will have trouble with the b2 pawn once
the queen appears on b6 and the knight on c4.} Qc7 16. Nf1 Bd6 17. g5 {But
this is asking for it. There were other, less provocative moves.} Nh5 18. Bf3
Nf4 19. Bg4 Nb6 20. h4 Rae8 (20... Nc4 {Was more precise. The point is that
after} 21. Bxf4 Bxf4 22. Re2 Ra6 {With crude ideas of Rb6 appears.}) 21. Bxf4
Bxf4 {Even so, White's position is unpleasant} 22. Qd3 $6 {A pseudoactive move}
(22. Be2 {Preventing Nc4, was probably better.}) 22... Nc4 23. Re2 e5 $5 {Not
the only way to continue. It is debatable whether this move is the best, but
it is definitely unplesant to face.} 24. dxe5 Rxe5 $1 {Very precise,
preventing the doubling of White rooks on e1} (24... Nxe5 25. Qh3) 25. Bf3 (25.
Rae1 Nxb2) 25... Rd8 {Now White's position is already difficult} 26. Qc2 (26.
Rd1 {Preventing d4 push at all costs was mandatory} Qd7 27. Kg2) 26... Qd7 27.
Kg2 d4 28. cxd4 Qxd4 (28... Rxe2 {Was actually more precise. After} 29. Qxe2 (
29. Bxe2 Nd6) 29... Qxd4 (29... Nd6 30. Bg4) 30. Bxb7 (30. Rd1 {\/The point is
that with the queen on e2, this move odesn't work} Qxd1 31. Qxd1 Rxd1 32. Bxd1
Nxb2) 30... Nxb2 {Black is the one pushing}) 29. Rxe5 Nxe5 30. Bxb7 $2 {A
fatal blunder.} (30. Rd1 {And suddenly White gets back in the game}) 30... Qd7
31. Bf3 Nxf3 32. Kxf3 Qh3+ $1 33. Kxf4 Rd4+ 34. Ke5 Rxh4 {And here White
resigned - it is clear he can't survive with the king on e5} (34... Qxh4 {Was
actually better, but who the hell cares :D}) 0-1

