1. d4 {Bosiocic is a true modern grandmaster with a wide repertoire, although
I believe that 1 d4 is his childhood love} Nf6 2. c4 c6 {Ivan has started
playing Semi-Slav regularly in recent years. In his youth, he was famous for
venturing the King's Indian - something his fans somehow always hope to see.}
3. Nf3 d5 4. e3 Bg4 {But this is a bit surprising, since my impression was
that Ivan always went for e6. I may be wrong though} 5. Qb3 Qb6 6. Nc3 e6 7.
Nh4 Be7 8. h3 Bh5 9. g4 Bg6 10. Nxg6 hxg6 {Up to this point, we have seen a
theoretical discussion, but now Bosiocic employs a new move} 11. Rg1 {Removing
the rook from the pin} (11. Bg2 {And}) (11. g5 {Have been exclusively played})
11... g5 12. Bd2 Nbd7 13. O-O-O Qc7 {Perhaps it was easier to exchange the
queens} (13... Qxb3 14. axb3 Nf8 {And Black is not worse, despite his
opponent's bishop pair}) (13... Nf8 {Immediately was also possible}) 14. Kb1 a6
$6 {Also a bit slow. Ivan starts drifting here} (14... Nf8 15. cxd5) (14...
dxc4 {Seems like a concession, but after} 15. Qxc4 O-O-O 16. Bg2 Nb6 {Black is
only slightly worse}) (14... a5 {With the idea of a4 was also an option.} 15.
cxd5 (15. Bg2 a4 16. Nxa4 dxc4 17. Qxc4 b5 18. Qxc6 Qxc6 19. Bxc6 Rxa4 20. Bxb5
{Is not worse for Black}) 15... Nxd5 16. Qc2 {With equality}) 15. Bg2 {Now
Black's position is already a bit dubious. Computer indicates a5 or Qb6 as
options. Saric goes wrong} Rc8 $2 {But this is a serious mistake, leading to a
very favourable position for White} (15... O-O 16. cxd5 Nxd5 17. Bxd5 $1 {
Apparently an important idea in this set-ups} (17. Nxd5 cxd5 18. Rc1 {Is also
good}) 17... exd5 18. e4 {Is also better for White}) 16. cxd5 cxd5 (16... Nxd5
17. Nxd5 exd5 (17... cxd5 18. Rc1 Qb8 19. Rxc8+ Qxc8 20. e4 {Is horrible for
Black}) 18. e4 {With initiative}) (16... exd5 17. e4 (17. f4 {Is also an option
})) 17. e4 $1 {Opening the position for his bishops.} (17. f4 {Was also
playable}) 17... dxe4 18. Nxe4 Nxe4 19. Bxe4 O-O 20. h4 $1 {Excellent play!
This is an extremely unplesant move to face} (20. Qxb7 {Was also good, but a
bit primitive} Qd6 {And the game continues}) 20... Nf6 (20... gxh4 21. g5 {And
White will have a strong attack in the near future. Even so, perhaps Black
could have netted a pawn for his trouble}) 21. Bf3 gxh4 22. g5 Nd5 23. Rh1 $1 {
Now White is simply winning. Saric manages to put up a tough resistance, though
} Nf4 24. Rxh4 Ng6 25. Rh3 Rfd8 26. Be4 $1 {The strongest move, targeting the
weak knight} Qc4 {The best move, seeking salvation in a tough endgame} 27. Bxb7
Qxb3 28. Rxb3 Rb8 29. d5 $1 {Another great move! Bosiocic plays this phase of
the game extremely precisely} (29. Be3 a5 {And now White has to go for} 30. d5
{In order to maximize the advantage}) 29... exd5 30. Ba5 Rd7 31. Bxd5 Rxb3 32.
Bxb3 Rxd1+ 33. Bxd1 Nf4 34. Bb3 Bxg5 {Even though the material is equal, the
two bishops and the queenside pawn majority are simply too strong. Besides,
the a-pawn is bound to fall} 35. Bc4 Nh3 36. Bb6 Bh4 37. a4 (37. f3 {Was
satisfactory as well}) 37... Bxf2 38. a5 Bg3 39. Bxa6 Ng5 40. Bb7 Ne6 41. a6
Bb8 42. b4 Kf8 43. b5 Ke7 44. Bd5 Kd6 45. Bxe6 Kxe6 46. Bc7 $1 {A sweet final
point. Saric resigned here.     A great game by Bos, worthy of a champion!} 1-0

