1. e4 c6 {Zelcic sticks to his guns} 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 {But he is the first to
deviate from the previous game} 4. dxc5 {Certainly the most critical} e6 5. a3
{But this is relatively a new approach, but a sensible idea - White returns
the pawn at once in order to grab some space on the queenside and to develop
his bishop on b2} Bxc5 6. b4 Bb6 7. Bb2 {According to my openins book, this is
already a new move} (7. Qg4) (7. Nf3 {Have been played}) 7... Ne7 8. Nf3 a5 9.
Nbd2 axb4 10. axb4 Rxa1 11. Qxa1 O-O 12. Bd3 {The position is probably
balanced, but White's position is easier to play. He will castle, put his rook
somewhere and play a timely c4.     As usual in such positions with lack of
space - it is not clear what Black should be doing. Preparing f6 is a bit
difficult due to the Qa1 and Bb2 battery} Na6 13. Qa3 Ng6 14. h4 Nc7 {The
first questionable decision - Black probably shouldn't have allowed h5} (14...
h5 {Looks risky though}) (14... Nf4 {Gains some time} 15. Bf1 Bd7 16. g3 Ng6
17. h5 Ne7 18. h6 g6 19. Nh2 {But the position is still complicated and
probably slightly better for White}) 15. h5 Nf4 16. Bf1 {But not the knight on
f4 runs into trouble} d4 $2 (16... g6 {Was the only way - as ugly as it looks})
17. g3 {Good enough, but} (17. h6) (17. Rh4 {Were more critical}) 17... Nfd5
18. h6 g6 19. Bxd4 Bxd4 20. Nxd4 Qe7 21. c3 f6 22. Be2 (22. N2f3 fxe5 23. Nxe5
Qd6 24. f4 {Is probably better for White, but it doesn't look like a human
decision}) 22... fxe5 23. N4f3 b5 {Preventing c4} 24. Ne4 $2 {But this move
throws away the entire advantage - at least according to the computer} (24.
Nxe5 {Was the best, although Black does have counterplay}) 24... Nf6 $2 {But
Zelcic misses his chance} (24... Bb7 {Looks ugly due to} 25. Nc5 {But there is
a great shot available} (25. Nfg5 {Is what White has to play, but after} Nf6
26. Bd3 {It is clear we have a completely different game}) (25. Qa7 Ba8) 25...
Ne3 26. fxe3 Bxf3 {And black takes over}) 25. Nxf6+ Qxf6 26. Qa7 {Now White
manages to keep control. The queen is extremely strong on a7, preventing the
development of the bishop and watching over g7 square} Nd5 27. Ng5 $1 Bb7 28.
Nxe6 $1 {Diverting the queen} e4 $2 {Losing on the spot} (28... Qxe6 {Would
prolong the game, although after} 29. Qxb7 Qf7 30. Qxf7+ Rxf7 31. Bxb5 {Black
can't save the game}) 29. Nxf8 Qxc3+ 30. Kf1 {And it is all over} (30. Kd1 $4 {
Was the only trap to avoid:} e3 31. Qa2 Kxf8 {And suddenly Black is winning})
30... Qc7 31. Ne6 Qe7 32. Qb8+ Kf7 33. Nd8+ {Another crushing defeat which put
an end to Zelcic's hopes} 1-0

