1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. Nc3 a6 {I always thought you don't want to
force the exchange on c6 when White wants to take anyway, but what do I know}
5. Bxc6 dxc6 6. a4 Bg4 7. h3 Bh5 8. a5 {The advance of the a-pawn so early in
the game is a bit strange} e6 9. g4 Bg6 10. Qe2 Nd7 11. Nd1 {But this is
indeed a bad move. White probably wanted to put the knight to c4, but he
doesn't have time} (11. d3 h5 (11... c4 {Is another possibility} 12. dxc4 Bb4)
12. g5 {Was more normal. The difference with the subsequent variation lies in
the fact bishop can be developed} h4 13. Bf4 {And now e5 is prevented in the
future, as after} Bh5 14. Qe3 c4 15. d4 e5 {White can simply take the pawn} 16.
Nxe5) 11... h5 12. Rg1 (12. g5 h4 13. d3 Bh5 {Is now unpleasant} 14. Qe3 c4 15.
d4 e5 {And the bishop on e5 doesn't prevent this move. After} 16. dxe5 Nxe5 {
Black can't take the knight due to the mate on d1 (another downside of Nd1)})
12... hxg4 13. hxg4 c4 $5 {Simply a great move - just giving up a weak pawn in
order to gain some time} (13... Be7 {There were simpler ways of continuing,
Be7 being one of them}) 14. Qxc4 Qf6 15. Qe2 Rh3 16. Rg3 {The only move} Rxg3
17. fxg3 Ne5 18. Nh4 O-O-O {Now Black's position is so much easier to play.
White's pawn structure is full of targets} 19. d3 {This basically returns the
pawn} (19. Nf2 {Is the comp's suggestion. It claims 0.0 here, which means
Black has full compensation for the pawn}) 19... Bb4+ 20. Bd2 Bxd2+ 21. Qxd2 (
21. Kxd2 Qg5+ {Is not particularly helpful} 22. Kc3 Nxg4) 21... Bxe4 22. Qf4
Bf3 23. Qxf6 gxf6 24. Nxf3 Nxf3+ 25. Kf2 Ne5 {The endgame is excellent for
Black} 26. Ne3 Rd4 {But this is a bit strange} ({Why not} 26... Rg8) ({Or} 
26... Rh8) 27. Ke2 Kd8 28. b3 Ke7 29. Rh1 c5 30. Rh8 {This just gives a tempo
for Rg8} (30. Rh4 Rd8 31. Nc4 {White should be aiming to exchange knights, not
rooks}) 30... Rd8 31. Rh5 Rg8 {This should have probably been played
immediately} 32. Nc4 Rxg4 33. Nxe5 fxe5 34. Kf3 {Another imprecision, moving
the king away from the queenside} (34. Rxe5 Kd6 (34... Rxg3 35. Rxc5) 35. Re3
Rb4 36. Rf3 f5 37. Rf1 {Was the best - White defends all his pawns and the
game should end in a draw}) 34... Rb4 35. Rxe5 Rb5 {Now the a-pawn falls and
Black is again in the driver's seat} 36. Rh5 Rxa5 37. b4 {It is logical to
exchange the pawns, but now Black's a pawn becomes passed} (37. Ke3 {Passive
defence was probably better}) 37... Rb5 38. bxc5 b6 39. d4 bxc5 40. dxc5 a5 41.
Ke3 a4 42. Kd4 Rb4+ $2 {I am the first who should never give question marks in
the endgames, but this deserves it} (42... a3 43. Rh1 a2 44. Ra1 Ra5 {And
Black is winning}) 43. c4 $2 {Returning the favor} (43. Kc3 {Was the best
defence. I am not sure Black can win after say} Rg4 44. Rh1 Rxg3+ 45. Kb4 a3
46. Rd1) 43... a3 {Now it is all over. We get a scenario similar to the one
examined on Black's 42nd move} 44. Rh2 Ra4 45. Ra2 f5 46. c6 Kd6 47. c7 Kxc7
48. Ke5 Kd7 49. Kf6 Ra6 50. c5 Ra5 51. c6+ Kd6 52. c7 Kxc7 53. Kxe6 Kc6 54. Kf6
Kd6 55. Kg5 Ke5 56. Re2+ Kd4 57. Ra2 Kc4 58. Ra1 a2 59. Kf4 Kb3 60. Re1 a1=Q {
A crushing win in an important moment. This win allowed Kozul to catch the
leaders and fight for the first place in the last round. But more on that later
} 0-1

