1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. g3 {These days, when playing against Kozul, you can more
or less count on facing the Catalan} Bb4+ 4. Nd2 c5 5. a3 Bxd2+ 6. Qxd2 cxd4 7.
Nf3 d5 8. cxd5 e5 $1 {A good choice of variation for a decisive game. Here
White needs to know what he is doing in order not to end up significantly worse
} 9. Bg2 (9. Nxe5 Qxd5 10. Nf3 Nc6 {Doesn't lead to advantage}) (9. Qg5 {As
played in my database seems like a critical test of Black's system.} Nbd7 10.
Nxe5 (10. Qxg7 {Leads to a draw after} Rg8 11. Qh6 Rg6 12. Qh4 Rg4 13. Qh6 Rg6)
10... O-O {With sharp play}) 9... Nbd7 10. O-O O-O {Now White has a concrete
problem - how is he going to defend his d-pawn?} 11. Nh4 $6 {A step in the
wrong direction} (11. e3 {Undermining Black's central chain, seems better} dxe3
12. Qxe3 Re8 (12... Nxd5 13. Qb3 N5f6 14. Bg5 {Leads to good compensation}) 13.
d6 {With complicated, double-edged play}) 11... Nb6 {Now d5 pawn is a goner}
12. e4 $6 (12. f4 {Is the comp's suggestion, but it is understandable why
Kozul didn't play it. After} Nfxd5 13. fxe5 Ne3 14. Rf4 Nbc4 {Things look
pretty ugly for White, even though he might survive}) 12... dxe3 13. Qxe3 e4 (
13... Re8 $5) 14. Rd1 $2 {But this loses a pawn without any compensation} (14.
f3 {Was already the only move} Nbxd5 15. Qd4 exf3 16. Nxf3 {And despite the
lost pawn, White's bishops have prospects and guarantee him some compensation})
14... Nbxd5 15. Qd4 Bg4 (15... Re8 {Is a serious alternative}) 16. Re1 Re8 {
Now White manages to regain the pawn, although Black is still better} 17. Bxe4
Nxe4 18. Rxe4 Rxe4 19. Qxe4 Nf6 20. Qa4 Qd5 21. Be3 Rd8 {The critical moment
of the game and the entire championship} (21... Bh3 {Is too early due to} 22.
Rd1) 22. Bxa7 $2 {Uncharacteristic of Kozul, who underestimates the danger.
His nerves probably failed him} (22. Ng2 {Transfering the knight to f4, was
the best move}) (22. Qf4 {Was also not bad, intending f3}) 22... Bh3 {Now g5
is a terrible threat, against which there is no defence. if White moves the
queen, then Qd1 also comes} 23. Re1 g5 24. f3 {Forced} gxh4 25. Qxh4 Qf5 {And
White can resign} 26. Bd4 Rd6 27. Bc3 h5 28. Kf2 Ng4+ {What a crushing game
against a strong opponent with the Black pieces!  Such a victory in a must-win
situation bears the hallmark of a true champion!} 0-1

