1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 {The Italian Game - a trendy way of avoiding the
bulletproof Berlin Defence.} Bc5 4. O-O Nf6 5. d3 a6 6. c3 Ba7 7. Bb3 {This
has all been played many times} (7. Bg5 {Is maybe a bit early, since Black
hasn't castled. But it is playable, of course} h6 8. Bh4 g5) 7... d6 8. Re1 O-O
9. h3 Be6 10. Nbd2 Re8 11. Nf1 d5 {Typical freeing move in various 1 e5 e5
openings, but in the given instance it is probably premature.} (11... h6 {
Controling the g5 square was logical}) (11... Qd7 {Maintaining the tension was
possible}) (11... Bxb3 {Was also playable} 12. Qxb3 Rb8 {And White is slightly
better}) 12. Ng5 $1 {The point. Now White gets a comfortable edge and the
bishop pair.} d4 $6 {From frying pan to fire. Now Black gets ugly doubled
pawns for no compensation} (12... Na5 13. Nxe6 Rxe6 14. Bc2 {Was probably
better. White retains a small plus.}) 13. Nxe6 fxe6 14. c4 $5 {A very
counterintuitive move, blocking the bishop. But White stops Black's
counterplay and has all the time in the world to prepare his own play.      It
was not strictly necessary, though.} (14. Nh2) (14. Qe2) 14... Rb8 {Hinting at
b5} 15. Bd2 Bc5 16. Ba4 b5 17. cxb5 axb5 18. Rc1 {A good move, forcing a piece
to d6 and increasing the strength of future f4} Qd6 (18... bxa4 19. Rxc5 {Is
terrible}) (18... Nd7 19. Bb3 Qe7 {Might be the best}) 19. Bb3 Nb4 20. Qe2 Na6
21. f4 $1 {The point of White's 18th move. Black can't take due to fork on e5}
Kh8 {This move tells a lot about Black's position. This prevents the threat of
f5, but gives White time} (21... Bb4 {Is the computer's suggestion, but after}
22. Bxb4 (22. fxe5 Qxe5 {And there is no Nf3}) 22... Nxb4 23. f5 c5 24. Bxe6+ {
The engine insists on} Rxe6 25. fxe6 Nxa2 {And claims Black has compensation.
It may well be so, but good luck playing it in an actual game.}) 22. Nh2 {
Going for f3. When you don't know what to do - improve your worst placed piece.
} Nd7 (22... Bb4 23. fxe5 Qxe5 24. Bxb4 Nxb4 25. a3 Na6 26. Nf3 {Now loses the
d4 pawn}) 23. fxe5 Qxe5 24. Nf3 Qg3 25. e5 Bb6 26. Rc6 (26. Ng5 $5) 26... Nac5
27. Bc2 Ra8 28. b4 $1 {A neat tactical blow.} Rxa2 {Trying to complicate
matters, but Brkic calculated correctly.} (28... Na6 29. Bb3 {Is hopeless}) 29.
Bb1 Rb2 (29... Rxd2 {Might have been the most resilient, but White's position
is won all the same}) 30. bxc5 {A piece is a piece... Black has no compensation
} Nxc5 31. Bf4 $1 {A final touchm which leads to simplification.} Rxe2 32. Bxg3
Rb2 33. Bf2 Rd8 34. Bxd4 Rxd4 35. Nxd4 Kg8 36. Kh1 b4 1-0

[Event "CRO Champ"]
