[BlackClock "1:30:00"]

1. e4 c5 {I don't think Zelcic plays anything else apart from 1 e4} 2. Nf3 {
And I don't think Kozul plays anything else against 1 e4} e6 {But this is a
small surprise. Kozul is one of the great experts of the Rauzer variation. He
probably wanted to mix things and surprise his opponent} 3. Nc3 a6 4. d4 cxd4
5. Nxd4 d6 {A standard Scheveningen set-up had appeared on the board} 6. Bd3 (
6. g4 {Has its merits}) 6... Nd7 7. O-O Ngf6 8. Be3 g6 $6 {But I am not sure
whether the fianchetto is the best combination with an early e6 -the d6 will
be weak and White has more targets on the kingside. According to my database
this was not played before} (8... Be7 {Is of course more normal}) 9. Qd2 Bg7
10. Rad1 O-O 11. Bh6 {Also probably not forced} (11. Nb3 {Also wants to target
d6, but the knight is better on d4 for the moment} b5 12. Be2 b4 13. Na4 Nxe4
14. Qxb4) (11. Be2 {Might be the most precise - preparing to target d6} Qc7 (
11... b5 12. Nc6) 12. Nb3 {And Black has to go for} d5 13. exd5 Nxd5 14. Nxd5
exd5 15. c3 {With clearly unfavourable isolani position}) 11... Qc7 12. Bxg7
Kxg7 13. Rfe1 {Now it is not so easy to target d6 and Black's set-up is
justified} b5 14. a4 b4 15. Na2 e5 16. Nb3 a5 17. Bb5 Nc5 18. Qxd6 Qxd6 19.
Rxd6 Nxb3 (19... Nfxe4 {Was playable, but complicated} 20. Rdd1 Nxb3 21. cxb3
Nf6 22. Rxe5 Be6 23. Bc4 Rad8 {A key move} 24. Rxd8 Rxd8 25. Kf1 Bxc4+ 26. bxc4
Rd1+ 27. Re1 (27. Ke2 Ra1) 27... Rd2 28. Re2 Rd1+ {And this long computer
variation ends in a draw. But I don't think Kozul was playing for a draw}) 20.
cxb3 Be6 21. Nc1 {Black has sufficient compensation for the pawn} Rfd8 ({Why
not} 21... Rfc8 {?}) 22. Rd3 Rd4 $2 {But this is a mistake} (22... Rdc8 {Was
still OK for Black - the White rook doesn't stand better on d3 than it stood
on d6}) 23. f3 (23. Rxd4 exd4 24. f3 {Transposes}) 23... Rc8 24. Rxd4 exd4 25.
Ne2 d3 {Trying to complicate matters} (25... Rc2 26. Nxd4 Rxb2 27. Re2 {And
White keeps control}) 26. Nd4 $1 {Keeping the pawn on b3} (26. Bxd3 Bxb3 {
Should be avoided}) 26... d2 27. Rd1 Rc1 28. Be2 $1 {Just in time} Nd7 29. Kf2
Nc5 30. Nxe6+ fxe6 31. Rxd2 Nxb3 {Now the endgame is close to winning for White
} 32. Rd7+ Kf6 33. f4 (33. Ke3 {Was probably cleaner} e5 34. Rxh7 Nd4 {But
Black does get a lot of activity.}) 33... e5 34. fxe5+ (34. g3) (34. Ke3) 34...
Kxe5 35. Rd5+ $1 {A fine move} Kf6 (35... Kxe4 {Would be a major blunder due to
} 36. Bf3+ Kf4 37. g3# {With checkmate}) 36. Ke3 Rc2 37. Rb5 {Preparing to
give some side checks} Rxb2 38. e5+ Ke7 (38... Kf5 {Seems more reasonable, but
there is a beautiful hidden point -} 39. Bd3+ Kg5 40. h4+ $3 {Diverting the
king} Kxh4 41. e6 {And the pawn decides}) 39. Bc4 Rc2 40. Bd5 (40. Bxb3 {Was
also good enough}) 40... Nc1 41. Rb7+ Kd8 42. e6 b3 43. e7+ Ke8 44. Kd4 b2 45.
Ke5 b1=Q 46. Rxb1 Kxe7 47. Rb7+ Ke8 48. Kd6 Kf8 49. Rf7+ Ke8 50. Rxh7 {And
here Black resigned. A very complicated and difficult game and a deserved
victory for Zelcic.} 1-0

