[BlackClock "1:34:00"]

{A curious endgame has been reached, where White has two pawns for the piece.
Even though it is normally a fatal material deficit, in this particular
instance he should be able to save himself} 51. g5 $1 {Correct decision,
saving and exchanging a pawn} Nf7 {The only way of continuing to play for the
win} (51... Ng8 {Here the knight doesn't control e5 square} 52. gxf6 gxf6 53.
e5 f5 54. Kg5 $11) 52. g6 $4 {But this is a losing move that has a very
concrete refutation} (52. h6 {Was the saving move. It looks suicidal, but after
} fxg5+ 53. Ke3 {White attacks the bishop and Black doesn't have time to take
on h6. After} gxh6 54. Kxe2 {The position is drawn}) 52... Nh6 $4 {Failing to
take the opportunity} (52... Ne5 $1 {Was winning. The point is that after} 53.
h6 gxh6 54. g7 Kf7 55. Bxe5 fxe5+ 56. Kxe5 Kxg7 {Black's bishop is of the
right colour and he easily deals with White's pawns.}) 53. e5 {Now the game is
indeed close to being drawn} Ng8 $2 {Giving away all chances} (53... Bxh5 {
Might be the best try. After} 54. exf6 gxf6 55. b5 Be2 {Black can still play on
}) 54. exf6 Nxf6 55. Bb2 Nxh5+ 56. Kg5 Nf6 57. Bxf6 gxf6+ 58. Kh6 Ke7 59. g7
Bc4 60. b5 Bf7 61. b6 Kd6 62. b7 Kc7 63. g8=Q {A very strange couple of
oversights. But it just tells how difficult chess can become, especially in
the 5th or 6th hour of play.} 1/2-1/2

