{Up to this point, Kozul has played another energetic and convincing game. But
now he starts drifting} 23. Nc4 $2 {Not the best} (23. Rxe7 {Was simpler.} dxe3
24. Nc5 {And White's knight dominated the bishop} e2 25. Nxa6 Qxa6 26. Re1) (
23. Nxd4 {Was also good.}) 23... Qa7 24. Nxd4 $2 {But this is a blunder that
loses a clear exchange} f5 25. Rxe7 Qxe7 26. Nb6 {You would expect Black can
only lose this in a blitz game, but just see what happens next} Rc7 27. Rc1 Qf7
28. Qc5 f4 29. g4 f3 30. h3 Re8 31. Qd6 Rce7 32. Kh2 Re5 33. Kg3 c5 $2 {Losing
a big portion of the advantage} (33... h5 {Was crushing here. The threat is h4,
e.g.} 34. Nd7 h4+ 35. Kxh4 g5+ 36. Kg3 Qf4#) 34. Nd7 $1 {Probably missed by
Saric. Now he has to find the only move to survive} Re1 $2 {Losing on the spot}
(34... cxd4 35. Nf6+ (35. Nxe5 {Is also sufficient}) 35... Kh8 36. Rc7 R5e7 37.
Nxe8 Rxc7 38. Nxc7 {Is winning}) (34... g5 {Was the only move. The point is
that} 35. Nxe5 {Is met by} (35. Nf6+ Kh8 36. Nxf3 {And the play is still very
sharp and concrete, but computer claims the position is dynamically balanced})
35... Qf4#) 35. Nf6+ Kh8 36. Nxe8 {Now it is all over - Black's king is no
match for the knights and the queen} Rxc1 (36... Rxe8 37. Rxc5 {Is also
hopeless}) 37. Qe5+ Kg8 38. Nf6+ Kh8 39. Ne6 {With devastating threats} Rg1+
40. Kh4 g5+ 41. Nxg5 Qf8 42. Nd7+ Qg7 43. Nf7+ {And here Saric resigned. A
mind-boggling encounter and another confirmation of the old adage that "A good
player is always lucky"} 1-0

