{Black has been better (probably even winning) the whole game, but he was
unable to find the knock-out blow. White has reached this drawn endgame where
he has to make one precise move to save himself} 68. Rd8 $4 {But this is not
the one} (68. Rg8 {The idea is simple - White wants to give a check on the
d-file. If black does the same as in the game then} Rh7 69. Kc3 {Draws, after}
Rxh6 70. Rg1+ Ka2 71. Rg2 {As Black can't give a check on c6. An amazing,
study-like position}) 68... Rb7 {Now the pawn simply queens and there is no
satisfactory defence. White tried} 69. h7 Rxh7 70. Rxd6 Ra7 {But here he had
to resign, as the pawn promotion is inevitable. The game could have continued}
71. Rb6 Ka1 {Another instuctive moment - the only move} (71... Ka2 72. Kc2 {Is
horrible} Rc7+ 73. Kd3 b1=Q+ 74. Rxb1 Kxb1 75. f4 {And White manages to draw})
72. Kc2 Ra2 73. Kd3 b1=Q+ 74. Rxb1+ Kxb1 75. f4 (75. Ke4 Kc2 76. f4 Kc3 77. Ke5
Kc4 78. f5 Kc5 79. Ke6 Kc6 80. f6 Re2+ 81. Kf7 Kd7 {And Black wins}) 75... Ra4
{And with the king being cut-off, the win is easy} 76. f5 Kb2 77. Ke3 (77. f6
Rf4) 77... Kc3 78. f6 Ra6 79. f7 Rf6 0-1

