51. Nd6+ {Zelcic has convincingly outplayed his opponent and reached a winning
ending. It is hard to fathom how he can not win this position, let alone lose
it. But here amazing things started to happen:} Ke6 52. Ne4+ Kf5 53. Nd6+ (53.
Nxg5 {Was the easiest, transitioning into a won rook ending}) 53... Ke6 54.
Nc4+ Kf5 55. Ke2 (55. b7 {Was also sufficient here} e4+ 56. Kf2 exd3 57. Rb6)
55... Be7 56. Ne3+ Kg5 57. Nd5 Bf8 58. Rc8 (58. Ke3) 58... Bd6 59. Rc6 Bf8 60.
Nc3 Rb2+ 61. Kf3 Be7 62. Re6 $2 {But this is a serious mistake, allowing the
bishop on d8} (62. Ke4) (62. Nd5) 62... Bd8 63. Nd5 $2 (63. Rxe5+ $5) (63. Na4
$5 {Attacking the rook!} Rb3 (63... Kf5 64. Re8 Bxb6 65. Nxb2) 64. Ke2 {With
chances}) 63... Kf5 64. Rd6 (64. Re8 {Now doesn't work} Bxb6 65. Rb8 Rf2#)
64... Bg5 {Now Black has activated} 65. Nc3 Be7 $2 {Losing on the spot} (65...
Rb3 {Was the only move}) 66. Rc6 $6 {Returning the favour} (66. Nd1 {Was
winning.} Rb1 67. Ne3+ Kg5 68. Re6 {And we are back where we were}) 66... g5
67. g4+ $6 {Also not the best} (67. Ne4 {Looked ugly, but} g4+ 68. Ke3 Rxg2 69.
Rh6 Bg5+ 70. Nxg5 Kxg5 71. b7 Rb2 72. Rh7 {Although probably even here Black
can hold}) 67... hxg4+ 68. Kg3 Rb3 69. Kh2 Bd8 {Now White needs to be careful}
70. Ne4 (70. Rd6 {Was sufficient to maintain the balance} Be7 (70... Bxb6 71.
Nd5) 71. Rc6 Bd8) 70... Rxb6 71. Ng3+ (71. Rc8 {Was also better}) 71... Kf4 72.
Rc8 $4 {The losing move, blundering a whole piece} (72. Rc4+ {Was mandatory})
72... Rh6+ 73. Kg1 Bb6+ {What a swindle. What a tragic loss for Zelcic} 0-1

